3303:
** μέν**,
Particle, used partly to express certainty on the part of the speaker or writer; partly, and more commonly, to point out that the word or clause with which it stands is correlative to another word or clause that is to follow, the latter word or clause being introduced by δέ.
**A**
**A.I** μέν used absolutely to express certainty, not followed by correlative δέ, **indeed, of a truth**, synonymous with μήν, as appears from the _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ form ἦ μέν in protestations and oaths (where _Attic dialect_ used ἦ μήν), καί μοι ὄμοσσον, ἦ μ. μοι πρόφρων ἔπεσιν καὶ χερσὶν ἀρήξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.77, compare 14.275; ἦ μέν τοι τάδε πάντα τελείεται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.160, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.416; τοῦτον ἐξορκοῖ, ἦ μέν οἱ διηκονήσειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, compare 5.93, etc.: with _negative_, οὐ μὲν γάρ τι κακὸν βασιλευέμεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.392, etc.; ὤμοσα, μὴ μὲν.. ἀναφῆναι 4.254, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.118, 179; ἔξαρνος ἦν, μὴ μὲν ἀποκτεῖναι [prev. author] 3.67, compare 99: without _negative_, ἀνδρὸς μὲν τόδε σῆμα πάλαι κατατεθνηῶτος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.89}}: also in Trag., ἀκτὴ μὲν ἥδε τῆς περιρρύτου χθονός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1, compare 159 (_anapaest meter_), “OC” 44, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 676, 1129, etc.; καὶ μέν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.269, 9.632, etc.; οὐδὲ μὲν οὐδὲ.. 2.703, 12.212}}; γε μέν, compare γε {{Refs|1.5.}}
**A.I.2** an answering clause with δέ is sometimes implied, τὴν μὲν ἐγὼ σπουδῇ δάμνημ᾽ ἐπέεσσι her can I hardly subdue, [**but** all others easily], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.893}}; ὡς μὲν λέγουσι as **indeed** they say, [**but** as I believe not], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 8}}; καὶ πρῶτον μὲν ἦν αὐτῷ πόλεμος (with no ἔπειτα δέ to follow), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.14}}; so νῦν μέν σ᾽ ἀφήσω I will let you go **this time**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.81}}: to give force to assertions made by a person respecting himself, wherein opposition to other persons is implied, ὡς μὲν ἐμῷ θυμῷ δοκεῖ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.154}}; δοκεῖν μέν μοι ἥξει τήμερον [τὸ πλοῖον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43d}}: hence with the _person_ _pronoun_, ἐγὼ μέν νυν θεοῖσι ἔχω χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71}}; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐδέν (i.e. θέλω) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 498; ἐμοῦ μὲν οὐχ ἑκόντος [prev. author] “Aj.” 455; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ οἶδα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.12, compare 4.2.45, etc.}}: with the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_, τούτου μὲν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἐγὼ σοφώτερός εἰμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21d: generally, to emphasize the preceding word, πολλὴ μὲν ἡ μεταβολή μοι γέγονεν}} great **indeed** has been the change, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.1, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.1, etc.}}
**A.I.3** μέν is used alone in questions, when the answer is assumed, **I take it**, θέμις μὲν ἡμᾶς χρησμὸν εἰδέναι θεο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 676, compare “Ion” 520 (troch.), “Hipp.” 316, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1214}}; Ἕλλην μέν ἐστι καὶ Ἑλληνίζε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82b.}}
**A.II** μέν followed by δέ in the correlative clause or clauses, **on the one hand, on the other hand**; commonly in Classical Gr., less frequently in later Gr. (rare in NT
**A.II.1** μέν.., δέ.. (or when the correlative clause is _negative_, μέν.., οὐδέ.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.318, 536)}}, to mark opposition, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}—The opposed clauses commonly stand together, but are frequently separated by clauses, parenthetic or explanatory; e.g. μέν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.494 is answered by δέ in 511, 527f}}; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.2, πρῶτον μέν is answered by ἐπεὶ δέ}} in <{{Refs|*> 6; in [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.2, πρῶτον μέν is answered by θαυμαστὸν δέ in 1.2.1.}}
**A.II.2** to connect a series of clauses containing different matter, though with no opposition, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.18s q., 306f (five δέ-clauses), 433f (eight δέ-clauses),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14, 7.10s q.: frequently when the members of a group or class are distinctly specified, παῖδες δύο, πρεσβύτερος μὲν Ἀρταξέρξης, νεώτερος δὲ Κῦρος [prev. work] 1.1.1; τάφρος.., τὸ μὲν εὖρος ὀργυιαὶ πέντε, τὸ δὲ βάθος ὀργυιαὶ τρεῖς [prev. work] 1.7.14; πρῶτος μέν.., δεύτερος δέ.., τρίτος δέ.. [prev. work] 5.6.9}}; τότε μέν.., τότε δέ.., at **one** time.., at **another**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.1.9, etc.: especially with the _Article_ used as a _pronoun_}} ὁ μέν.., ὁ δέ..; τὸ μέν.., τὸ δέ.., etc.
**A.II.3** the principal word is frequently repeated, οἳ περὶ μὲν βουλὴν Δαναῶν, περὶ δ᾽ ἐστὲ μάχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.258, compare 288, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.70; ἔνι μὲν φιλότης, ἐν δ᾽ ἵμερος, ἐν δ᾽ ὀαριστύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.216; Ξέρξης μὲν ἄγαγεν.., Ξέρξης δ᾽ ἀπώλεσεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 550, compare 560, 694, 700 (all Lyric poetry)}}; χαλεπαίνει μὲν πρῳρεύς, χαλεπαίνει δὲ κυβερνήτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.20.}}
**A.II.4** one of the correlative clauses is sometimes independent, while the other takes the _participle_ or some other dependent form, ἐβλασφήμει κατ᾽ ἐμοῦ.., μάρτυρα μὲν.. οὐδένα παρασχόμενος.., παρεκελεύετο δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.11; οἱ ἀμφὶ βασιλέα, πεζοὶ μὲν οὐκέτι, τῶν δὲ ἱππέων ὁ λόφος ἐνεπλήσθη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.12, compare 2.1.7, 5.6.29; ὧν ἐπιμεμφομένα σ᾽ ἁδεῖα μὲν ἀντία δ᾽ οἴσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 123, compare “OC” 522 (bothlyr.); χωρὶς μὲν τοῦ ἐστερῆσθαι.., ἔτι δὲ καὶ.. δόξω ἀμελῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44}} b.
**A.II.5** μέν and δέ frequently oppose two clauses, whereof one is subordinate to the other in meaning or emphasis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀλλ᾽ ἐκεῖνο θαυμάζω, εἰ Λακεδαιμονίοις μέν ποτε.. ἀντήρατε,.. νυνὶ δὲ ὀκνεῖτ᾽ ἐξιέναι (for εἰ.. ἀντάραντες νυνὶ ὀκνεῖτε) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.24, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 116, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 34.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.11, etc.}}: so in an anacoluthon, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|τρία μὲν ὄντα.. ναυτικά.., τούτων δ᾽ εἰ περιόψεσθε τὰ δύο, κτλ., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36.}}
**A.II.6** μέν is not always answered by δέ, but frequently by other equivalent Particles, as ἀλλά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.22f, 2.703f, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 176, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.17:—by μέντοι, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 350, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.189, etc.:—by ἀτάρ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.84, 124, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 342, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1051s q., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172c, etc. (so μέν.., αὐτάρ}}] in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.50, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.513, etc.):— by αὖ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.108, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.210:—by αὖθις, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 165:—by αὖτε, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.234, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.5:—by temporal Particles, πρῶτα μέν.., εἶτα.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 261}}; πρῶτον μέν.., μετὰ τοῦτο.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.5 - 7}}; μάλιστα μὲν δὴ.., ἔπειτα μέντοι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 350, compare “OT” 647:}} —rarely by μήν with _negative_, οὐδὲν μὴν κωλύει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268e; οὐ μὴν αὐταί γε [prev. author] “Phlb.” 12d.}}
**A.II.6.b** when the opposition is emphatic, δέ is sometimes strengthened, as ὅμως δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 785, “Ph.” 473, 1074, etc. (so ἀλλ᾽ ὅμως “El.” 450)}}; δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.415, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.5}}; δ᾽ ἔμπης.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.561 - 2.}}
**A.II.6.c** μέν is sometimes answered by a _copulative_ Particle, κάρτιστοι μὲν ἔσαν καὶ καρτίστοις ἐμάχοντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 267, compare 459, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.475, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1, “Tr.” 689, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 125 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: rarely in Prose, τρία μὲν ἔτη ἀντεῖχον.., καὶ οὐ πρότερον ἐνέδοσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65 (uncertain).}}
**B** μέν before other Particles:
**B.I** where each Particle retains its force,
**B.I.1** μὲν ἄρα, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus μέν ῥα, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.1, 6.312, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.127, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258d, “R.” 467d, etc.}}
**B.I.2** μὲν γάρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 62, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, etc.:— in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} there is frequently no second clause, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.173, 392, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1062, etc.; μὲν γὰρ δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.825; μὲν γάρ τε 17.727.}}
**B.I.3** μέν γε, when a general statement is explained in detail, Κορινθίοις μέν γε ἔνσπονδοί ἐστε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, compare 70, 6.86, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.46, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.27, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1382, “V.” 564, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 909.4.}}
**B.I.4** μὲν δή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.514, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, etc.}}: frequently used to express positive certainty, ἀλλ᾽ οἶσθα μὲν δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 627, compare “OT” 294; τὰ μὲν δὴ τόξ᾽ ἔχεις [prev. author] “Ph.” 1308}}; especially as a conclusion, τοῦτο μὲν δὴ.. ὁμολογεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 470b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.6, etc.}}: in closing a statement, τοιαῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 500, etc.: used in answers to convey full assent, ἦ μὲν δή (compare supr. A) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.348, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.33; καὶ μὲν δή.. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 409b}}; οὐ μὲν δή, to deny positively, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.238, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148e, etc.; οὐ μὲν δή.. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.3, 3.2.14}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὔ τι μὲν δή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187a.}}
**B.I.5** μὲν οὖν, see below 11.2.
**B.II** where the Particles combine so as to form a new sense,
**B.II.1** μέν γε **at all events, at any rate** (not in Trag.), τοῦτο μέν γ᾽ ἤδη σαφές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 154, compare “Nu.” 1172, “Lys.” 1165, “Ra.” 80, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39; μέν γέ που 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 559b, “Tht.” 147a.}}
**B.II.2** μὲν οὖν is frequently used with a corresponding δέ, so that each Particle retains its force, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.780, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.111, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 244, 843; “Ph.” 359, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5, etc.}}: but frequently also _absolutely_, **so then**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 65; ταῦτα μὲν οὖν παραλείψω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.3}}; especially in replies, sometimes in strong affirmation, παντάπασι μὲν οὖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158d; κομιδῇ μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 159e; πάνυ μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 159b; ἀνάγκη μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 189e}}; also to substitute a new statement so as to correct a preceding statement, **nay rather**, κακοδαίμω; Answ. βαρυδαίμων μὲν οὖν! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1102}}; μου πρὸς τὴν κεφαλὴν ἀποψῶ wipe your nose on my head: Answ. ἐμοῦ μὲν οὖν.. **nay** on mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 911, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1032 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 1090 (Lyric poetry), 1396, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1363, “El.” 1503, “OT” 705, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 241, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44b, “Grg.” 466a, 470b, “Prt.” 309d, etc.; also μὲν οὖν δή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 153; καὶ δὴ μὲν οὖν [prev. author] “OC” 31}}; compare οὐμενοῦν: in “NT” μενοῦν and μενοῦνγε", to begin a sentence, **yea rather,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.11.28, NT.Rom.9.20, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 322, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus —In _Ionic dialect_, μέν νυν is used for μὲν οὖν, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, 4.145, etc.}}
**B.II.3** by μέν τε, if δέ τε follows, the two clauses are more closely combined than by τε.., τε.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.139, +others; μέν τε is frequently answered by δέ alone, 16.28, +others}}; by ἀλλά, αὐτάρ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.727, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.215, +others}}; perhaps by ἠδέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341:— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also uses μέν τε}} _absolutely_, when τε loses its force, as after ἦ, τίς, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.145, +others}}
**B.II.4** μέν τοι in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always occurs in speeches, where τοι can be regarded as the _dative_ of the _pronoun_: later, μέντοι is written as a single word, and is used:
**B.II.4.a** with a _conjunctive_ force, **yet, nevertheless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 320, 1054 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 413, etc.}}; and sometimes stands for δέ, answering to μέν, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.6 a.}}
**B.II.4.b** as an _adverb_, in strong protestations, οὐ μέντοι μὰ Δία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.49}}; in eager or positive assent, **of course**, φαμέν τι εἶναι..; Answ. φαμὲν μέντοι νὴ Δία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65d, +others: with a _negative_ to give emphasis to a question, οὐ σὺ μέντοι..}}; **why**, are you not.. ? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 309a, compare “Phdr.” 229b, “R.” 339b, etc.: sometimes to express impatience, ὄμνυμι γάρ σοι—τίνα μέντοι, τίνα θεῶ; [prev. author] “Phdr.” 236d}}; τί μ. πρῶτον ἦν, τί πρῶτον ἦ; **nay** what **was** the first? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 787; οὗτος, σὲ λέγω μ. [prev. author] “Ra.” 171}}; σὺ μέντοι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 44: with _imperative_, to enforce the command, τουτὶ μ. σὺ φυλάττου}} **only** take heed.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1100, compare “Av.” 661, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.8: in answers, γελοῖον μέντἂν εἴη}} **nay** it would be absurd, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158e}}; summing up a long temporal clause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.130.}}
**B.II.4.c** μέντοι γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.24, etc.; οὐ μ. γε 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 5: in later Gr. μέντοιγε}} stands first in the sentence, μ. οὐ θέλω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.897.13 (1st c.AD)}}; also γε μέντοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 938, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 778, 1292, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 600; ὅμως γε μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 61.}}
**B.II.4.d** καὶ μ. καί is used to add a point to be noted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331d}}; also καί.. μ., νῦν σοι καιρός ἐστιν ἐπιδείξασθαι τὴν παιδείαν, καὶ φυλάξασθαι μέντοι.. and **of course** to take care.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.15 (variant), compare 1.8.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339c, “Tht.” 143a.}}
**B.II.4.e** ἀλλὰ μέντοι **well, if it comes to that**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.16}}; **well, of course**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331e, etc.}}; compare μέντον.
**C** for μέν after other Particles, see each Particle.
**D** Position of μέν. Like δέ, it usually stands as the second word in a sentence. But when a sentence begins with words common to its subordinate clauses, μέν stands second in the first of these clauses, as ἥδε γὰρ γυνὴ δούλη μέν, εἴρηκεν δ᾽ ἐλεύθερον λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 63}}; οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι ἐτάξαντο μέν.., ἡσύχαζον δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.73, compare 113, etc. It also attaches itself to words which mark opposition, as πρῶτον μέν, τότε μέν, ἐγὼ μέν}}, even when these do not stand first: sometimes however it precedes them, ὡς μὲν ἐγὼ οἶμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228b; ὡς μέν τινες ἔφασαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.28}}. It generally stands between the _Article_ and Noun, or the _preposition_ and its Case: but if special stress is laid on the Noun, this is sometimes neglected, as οἱ Τεγεᾶται μὲν ἐπηυλίσαντο, Μαντινῆς δὲ ἀπεχώρησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.134}}; ἀνὰ τὸ σκοτεινὸν μέν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.22; also τῇ σῇ μὲν εὐδαιμονίῃ, τῇ ἐμεωυτοῦ δὲ κακοδαιμονίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87}}.
**D.II** μέν is frequently repeated:
**D.II.1** when, besides the opposition of two main clauses, a subordinate opposition is introduced into the first, ὁ μὲν ἀνὴρ τοιαῦτα μὲν πεποίηκε τοιαῦτα δὲ λέγει, ὑμῶν δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9, compare 5.8.24, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.104, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.214, 23.208.}}
**D.II.2** in apodosi with the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ or _adverb_, τὸν μὲν καλέουσι θέρος, τοῦτον μὲν προσκυνέουσι, τὸν δὲ χειμῶνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121; ὅσοι μὲν δὴ νομοῦ τοῦ Θηβαίου εἰσί, οὗτοι μέν [νυν].. αἶγας θύουσι· ὅσοι δὲ.. νομοῦ τοῦ Μενδησίου εἰσί, οὗτοι δὲ.. ὄϊς θύουσι [prev. work] 42, compare 3.108, +others}}; ὅτε μέν με οἱ ἄρχοντες ἔταττον.., τότε μὲν ἔμενον.., τοῦ δὲ θεοῦ τάττοντος,.. ἐνταῦθα δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28e, compare “Grg.” 512a.}}
**D.II.3** μέν used absolutely is frequently followed by a correlative μέν, εἰ μὲν οὖν ἡμεῖς μὲν.. ποιοῦμεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 421a.}}
**D.III** μέν is sometimes omitted (especially in Poetry) where it is implied in the following δέ, φεύγων, ὁ δ᾽ ὄπισθε διώκων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.157; ἐλευθεροῦτε πατρίδ᾽, ἐλευθεροῦτε δὲ παῖδας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 403; σφραγῖδε.. χρυσοῦν ἔχουσα τὸν δακτύλιον, ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρα ἀργυροῦν “IG” 22.1388.45, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 396, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 221e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447b14, etc.}}

3304:
Included with: ** μέν**,
Particle, used partly to express certainty on the part of the speaker or writer; partly, and more commonly, to point out that the word or clause with which it stands is correlative to another word or clause that is to follow, the latter word or clause being introduced by δέ.
**A**
**A.I** μέν used absolutely to express certainty, not followed by correlative δέ, **indeed, of a truth**, synonymous with μήν, as appears from the _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ form ἦ μέν in protestations and oaths (where _Attic dialect_ used ἦ μήν), καί μοι ὄμοσσον, ἦ μ. μοι πρόφρων ἔπεσιν καὶ χερσὶν ἀρήξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.77, compare 14.275; ἦ μέν τοι τάδε πάντα τελείεται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.160, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.416; τοῦτον ἐξορκοῖ, ἦ μέν οἱ διηκονήσειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, compare 5.93, etc.: with _negative_, οὐ μὲν γάρ τι κακὸν βασιλευέμεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.392, etc.; ὤμοσα, μὴ μὲν.. ἀναφῆναι 4.254, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.118, 179; ἔξαρνος ἦν, μὴ μὲν ἀποκτεῖναι [prev. author] 3.67, compare 99: without _negative_, ἀνδρὸς μὲν τόδε σῆμα πάλαι κατατεθνηῶτος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.89}}: also in Trag., ἀκτὴ μὲν ἥδε τῆς περιρρύτου χθονός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1, compare 159 (_anapaest meter_), “OC” 44, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 676, 1129, etc.; καὶ μέν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.269, 9.632, etc.; οὐδὲ μὲν οὐδὲ.. 2.703, 12.212}}; γε μέν, compare γε {{Refs|1.5.}}
**A.I.2** an answering clause with δέ is sometimes implied, τὴν μὲν ἐγὼ σπουδῇ δάμνημ᾽ ἐπέεσσι her can I hardly subdue, [**but** all others easily], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.893}}; ὡς μὲν λέγουσι as **indeed** they say, [**but** as I believe not], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 8}}; καὶ πρῶτον μὲν ἦν αὐτῷ πόλεμος (with no ἔπειτα δέ to follow), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.14}}; so νῦν μέν σ᾽ ἀφήσω I will let you go **this time**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.81}}: to give force to assertions made by a person respecting himself, wherein opposition to other persons is implied, ὡς μὲν ἐμῷ θυμῷ δοκεῖ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.154}}; δοκεῖν μέν μοι ἥξει τήμερον [τὸ πλοῖον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43d}}: hence with the _person_ _pronoun_, ἐγὼ μέν νυν θεοῖσι ἔχω χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71}}; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐδέν (i.e. θέλω) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 498; ἐμοῦ μὲν οὐχ ἑκόντος [prev. author] “Aj.” 455; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ οἶδα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.12, compare 4.2.45, etc.}}: with the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_, τούτου μὲν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἐγὼ σοφώτερός εἰμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21d: generally, to emphasize the preceding word, πολλὴ μὲν ἡ μεταβολή μοι γέγονεν}} great **indeed** has been the change, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.1, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.1, etc.}}
**A.I.3** μέν is used alone in questions, when the answer is assumed, **I take it**, θέμις μὲν ἡμᾶς χρησμὸν εἰδέναι θεο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 676, compare “Ion” 520 (troch.), “Hipp.” 316, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1214}}; Ἕλλην μέν ἐστι καὶ Ἑλληνίζε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82b.}}
**A.II** μέν followed by δέ in the correlative clause or clauses, **on the one hand, on the other hand**; commonly in Classical Gr., less frequently in later Gr. (rare in NT
**A.II.1** μέν.., δέ.. (or when the correlative clause is _negative_, μέν.., οὐδέ.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.318, 536)}}, to mark opposition, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}—The opposed clauses commonly stand together, but are frequently separated by clauses, parenthetic or explanatory; e.g. μέν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.494 is answered by δέ in 511, 527f}}; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.2, πρῶτον μέν is answered by ἐπεὶ δέ}} in <{{Refs|*> 6; in [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.2, πρῶτον μέν is answered by θαυμαστὸν δέ in 1.2.1.}}
**A.II.2** to connect a series of clauses containing different matter, though with no opposition, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.18s q., 306f (five δέ-clauses), 433f (eight δέ-clauses),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14, 7.10s q.: frequently when the members of a group or class are distinctly specified, παῖδες δύο, πρεσβύτερος μὲν Ἀρταξέρξης, νεώτερος δὲ Κῦρος [prev. work] 1.1.1; τάφρος.., τὸ μὲν εὖρος ὀργυιαὶ πέντε, τὸ δὲ βάθος ὀργυιαὶ τρεῖς [prev. work] 1.7.14; πρῶτος μέν.., δεύτερος δέ.., τρίτος δέ.. [prev. work] 5.6.9}}; τότε μέν.., τότε δέ.., at **one** time.., at **another**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.1.9, etc.: especially with the _Article_ used as a _pronoun_}} ὁ μέν.., ὁ δέ..; τὸ μέν.., τὸ δέ.., etc.
**A.II.3** the principal word is frequently repeated, οἳ περὶ μὲν βουλὴν Δαναῶν, περὶ δ᾽ ἐστὲ μάχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.258, compare 288, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.70; ἔνι μὲν φιλότης, ἐν δ᾽ ἵμερος, ἐν δ᾽ ὀαριστύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.216; Ξέρξης μὲν ἄγαγεν.., Ξέρξης δ᾽ ἀπώλεσεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 550, compare 560, 694, 700 (all Lyric poetry)}}; χαλεπαίνει μὲν πρῳρεύς, χαλεπαίνει δὲ κυβερνήτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.20.}}
**A.II.4** one of the correlative clauses is sometimes independent, while the other takes the _participle_ or some other dependent form, ἐβλασφήμει κατ᾽ ἐμοῦ.., μάρτυρα μὲν.. οὐδένα παρασχόμενος.., παρεκελεύετο δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.11; οἱ ἀμφὶ βασιλέα, πεζοὶ μὲν οὐκέτι, τῶν δὲ ἱππέων ὁ λόφος ἐνεπλήσθη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.12, compare 2.1.7, 5.6.29; ὧν ἐπιμεμφομένα σ᾽ ἁδεῖα μὲν ἀντία δ᾽ οἴσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 123, compare “OC” 522 (bothlyr.); χωρὶς μὲν τοῦ ἐστερῆσθαι.., ἔτι δὲ καὶ.. δόξω ἀμελῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44}} b.
**A.II.5** μέν and δέ frequently oppose two clauses, whereof one is subordinate to the other in meaning or emphasis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀλλ᾽ ἐκεῖνο θαυμάζω, εἰ Λακεδαιμονίοις μέν ποτε.. ἀντήρατε,.. νυνὶ δὲ ὀκνεῖτ᾽ ἐξιέναι (for εἰ.. ἀντάραντες νυνὶ ὀκνεῖτε) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.24, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 116, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 34.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.11, etc.}}: so in an anacoluthon, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|τρία μὲν ὄντα.. ναυτικά.., τούτων δ᾽ εἰ περιόψεσθε τὰ δύο, κτλ., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36.}}
**A.II.6** μέν is not always answered by δέ, but frequently by other equivalent Particles, as ἀλλά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.22f, 2.703f, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 176, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.17:—by μέντοι, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 350, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.189, etc.:—by ἀτάρ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.84, 124, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 342, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1051s q., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172c, etc. (so μέν.., αὐτάρ}}] in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.50, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.513, etc.):— by αὖ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.108, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.210:—by αὖθις, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 165:—by αὖτε, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.234, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.5:—by temporal Particles, πρῶτα μέν.., εἶτα.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 261}}; πρῶτον μέν.., μετὰ τοῦτο.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.5 - 7}}; μάλιστα μὲν δὴ.., ἔπειτα μέντοι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 350, compare “OT” 647:}} —rarely by μήν with _negative_, οὐδὲν μὴν κωλύει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268e; οὐ μὴν αὐταί γε [prev. author] “Phlb.” 12d.}}
**A.II.6.b** when the opposition is emphatic, δέ is sometimes strengthened, as ὅμως δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 785, “Ph.” 473, 1074, etc. (so ἀλλ᾽ ὅμως “El.” 450)}}; δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.415, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.5}}; δ᾽ ἔμπης.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.561 - 2.}}
**A.II.6.c** μέν is sometimes answered by a _copulative_ Particle, κάρτιστοι μὲν ἔσαν καὶ καρτίστοις ἐμάχοντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 267, compare 459, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.475, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1, “Tr.” 689, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 125 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: rarely in Prose, τρία μὲν ἔτη ἀντεῖχον.., καὶ οὐ πρότερον ἐνέδοσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65 (uncertain).}}
**B** μέν before other Particles:
**B.I** where each Particle retains its force,
**B.I.1** μὲν ἄρα, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus μέν ῥα, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.1, 6.312, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.127, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258d, “R.” 467d, etc.}}
**B.I.2** μὲν γάρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 62, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, etc.:— in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} there is frequently no second clause, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.173, 392, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1062, etc.; μὲν γὰρ δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.825; μὲν γάρ τε 17.727.}}
**B.I.3** μέν γε, when a general statement is explained in detail, Κορινθίοις μέν γε ἔνσπονδοί ἐστε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, compare 70, 6.86, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.46, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.27, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1382, “V.” 564, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 909.4.}}
**B.I.4** μὲν δή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.514, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, etc.}}: frequently used to express positive certainty, ἀλλ᾽ οἶσθα μὲν δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 627, compare “OT” 294; τὰ μὲν δὴ τόξ᾽ ἔχεις [prev. author] “Ph.” 1308}}; especially as a conclusion, τοῦτο μὲν δὴ.. ὁμολογεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 470b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.6, etc.}}: in closing a statement, τοιαῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 500, etc.: used in answers to convey full assent, ἦ μὲν δή (compare supr. A) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.348, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.33; καὶ μὲν δή.. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 409b}}; οὐ μὲν δή, to deny positively, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.238, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148e, etc.; οὐ μὲν δή.. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.3, 3.2.14}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὔ τι μὲν δή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187a.}}
**B.I.5** μὲν οὖν, see below 11.2.
**B.II** where the Particles combine so as to form a new sense,
**B.II.1** μέν γε **at all events, at any rate** (not in Trag.), τοῦτο μέν γ᾽ ἤδη σαφές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 154, compare “Nu.” 1172, “Lys.” 1165, “Ra.” 80, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39; μέν γέ που 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 559b, “Tht.” 147a.}}
**B.II.2** μὲν οὖν is frequently used with a corresponding δέ, so that each Particle retains its force, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.780, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.111, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 244, 843; “Ph.” 359, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5, etc.}}: but frequently also _absolutely_, **so then**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 65; ταῦτα μὲν οὖν παραλείψω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.3}}; especially in replies, sometimes in strong affirmation, παντάπασι μὲν οὖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158d; κομιδῇ μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 159e; πάνυ μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 159b; ἀνάγκη μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 189e}}; also to substitute a new statement so as to correct a preceding statement, **nay rather**, κακοδαίμω; Answ. βαρυδαίμων μὲν οὖν! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1102}}; μου πρὸς τὴν κεφαλὴν ἀποψῶ wipe your nose on my head: Answ. ἐμοῦ μὲν οὖν.. **nay** on mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 911, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1032 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 1090 (Lyric poetry), 1396, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1363, “El.” 1503, “OT” 705, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 241, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44b, “Grg.” 466a, 470b, “Prt.” 309d, etc.; also μὲν οὖν δή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 153; καὶ δὴ μὲν οὖν [prev. author] “OC” 31}}; compare οὐμενοῦν: in “NT” μενοῦν and μενοῦνγε", to begin a sentence, **yea rather,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.11.28, NT.Rom.9.20, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 322, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus —In _Ionic dialect_, μέν νυν is used for μὲν οὖν, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, 4.145, etc.}}
**B.II.3** by μέν τε, if δέ τε follows, the two clauses are more closely combined than by τε.., τε.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.139, +others; μέν τε is frequently answered by δέ alone, 16.28, +others}}; by ἀλλά, αὐτάρ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.727, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.215, +others}}; perhaps by ἠδέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341:— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also uses μέν τε}} _absolutely_, when τε loses its force, as after ἦ, τίς, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.145, +others}}
**B.II.4** μέν τοι in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always occurs in speeches, where τοι can be regarded as the _dative_ of the _pronoun_: later, μέντοι is written as a single word, and is used:
**B.II.4.a** with a _conjunctive_ force, **yet, nevertheless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 320, 1054 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 413, etc.}}; and sometimes stands for δέ, answering to μέν, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.6 a.}}
**B.II.4.b** as an _adverb_, in strong protestations, οὐ μέντοι μὰ Δία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.49}}; in eager or positive assent, **of course**, φαμέν τι εἶναι..; Answ. φαμὲν μέντοι νὴ Δία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65d, +others: with a _negative_ to give emphasis to a question, οὐ σὺ μέντοι..}}; **why**, are you not.. ? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 309a, compare “Phdr.” 229b, “R.” 339b, etc.: sometimes to express impatience, ὄμνυμι γάρ σοι—τίνα μέντοι, τίνα θεῶ; [prev. author] “Phdr.” 236d}}; τί μ. πρῶτον ἦν, τί πρῶτον ἦ; **nay** what **was** the first? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 787; οὗτος, σὲ λέγω μ. [prev. author] “Ra.” 171}}; σὺ μέντοι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 44: with _imperative_, to enforce the command, τουτὶ μ. σὺ φυλάττου}} **only** take heed.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1100, compare “Av.” 661, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.8: in answers, γελοῖον μέντἂν εἴη}} **nay** it would be absurd, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158e}}; summing up a long temporal clause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.130.}}
**B.II.4.c** μέντοι γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.24, etc.; οὐ μ. γε 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 5: in later Gr. μέντοιγε}} stands first in the sentence, μ. οὐ θέλω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.897.13 (1st c.AD)}}; also γε μέντοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 938, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 778, 1292, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 600; ὅμως γε μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 61.}}
**B.II.4.d** καὶ μ. καί is used to add a point to be noted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331d}}; also καί.. μ., νῦν σοι καιρός ἐστιν ἐπιδείξασθαι τὴν παιδείαν, καὶ φυλάξασθαι μέντοι.. and **of course** to take care.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.15 (variant), compare 1.8.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339c, “Tht.” 143a.}}
**B.II.4.e** ἀλλὰ μέντοι **well, if it comes to that**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.16}}; **well, of course**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331e, etc.}}; compare μέντον.
**C** for μέν after other Particles, see each Particle.
**D** Position of μέν. Like δέ, it usually stands as the second word in a sentence. But when a sentence begins with words common to its subordinate clauses, μέν stands second in the first of these clauses, as ἥδε γὰρ γυνὴ δούλη μέν, εἴρηκεν δ᾽ ἐλεύθερον λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 63}}; οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι ἐτάξαντο μέν.., ἡσύχαζον δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.73, compare 113, etc. It also attaches itself to words which mark opposition, as πρῶτον μέν, τότε μέν, ἐγὼ μέν}}, even when these do not stand first: sometimes however it precedes them, ὡς μὲν ἐγὼ οἶμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228b; ὡς μέν τινες ἔφασαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.28}}. It generally stands between the _Article_ and Noun, or the _preposition_ and its Case: but if special stress is laid on the Noun, this is sometimes neglected, as οἱ Τεγεᾶται μὲν ἐπηυλίσαντο, Μαντινῆς δὲ ἀπεχώρησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.134}}; ἀνὰ τὸ σκοτεινὸν μέν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.22; also τῇ σῇ μὲν εὐδαιμονίῃ, τῇ ἐμεωυτοῦ δὲ κακοδαιμονίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87}}.
**D.II** μέν is frequently repeated:
**D.II.1** when, besides the opposition of two main clauses, a subordinate opposition is introduced into the first, ὁ μὲν ἀνὴρ τοιαῦτα μὲν πεποίηκε τοιαῦτα δὲ λέγει, ὑμῶν δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9, compare 5.8.24, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.104, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.214, 23.208.}}
**D.II.2** in apodosi with the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ or _adverb_, τὸν μὲν καλέουσι θέρος, τοῦτον μὲν προσκυνέουσι, τὸν δὲ χειμῶνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121; ὅσοι μὲν δὴ νομοῦ τοῦ Θηβαίου εἰσί, οὗτοι μέν [νυν].. αἶγας θύουσι· ὅσοι δὲ.. νομοῦ τοῦ Μενδησίου εἰσί, οὗτοι δὲ.. ὄϊς θύουσι [prev. work] 42, compare 3.108, +others}}; ὅτε μέν με οἱ ἄρχοντες ἔταττον.., τότε μὲν ἔμενον.., τοῦ δὲ θεοῦ τάττοντος,.. ἐνταῦθα δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28e, compare “Grg.” 512a.}}
**D.II.3** μέν used absolutely is frequently followed by a correlative μέν, εἰ μὲν οὖν ἡμεῖς μὲν.. ποιοῦμεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 421a.}}
**D.III** μέν is sometimes omitted (especially in Poetry) where it is implied in the following δέ, φεύγων, ὁ δ᾽ ὄπισθε διώκων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.157; ἐλευθεροῦτε πατρίδ᾽, ἐλευθεροῦτε δὲ παῖδας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 403; σφραγῖδε.. χρυσοῦν ἔχουσα τὸν δακτύλιον, ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρα ἀργυροῦν “IG” 22.1388.45, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 396, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 221e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447b14, etc.}}

3305:
Included with: ** μέν**,
Particle, used partly to express certainty on the part of the speaker or writer; partly, and more commonly, to point out that the word or clause with which it stands is correlative to another word or clause that is to follow, the latter word or clause being introduced by δέ.
**A**
**A.I** μέν used absolutely to express certainty, not followed by correlative δέ, **indeed, of a truth**, synonymous with μήν, as appears from the _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ form ἦ μέν in protestations and oaths (where _Attic dialect_ used ἦ μήν), καί μοι ὄμοσσον, ἦ μ. μοι πρόφρων ἔπεσιν καὶ χερσὶν ἀρήξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.77, compare 14.275; ἦ μέν τοι τάδε πάντα τελείεται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.160, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.416; τοῦτον ἐξορκοῖ, ἦ μέν οἱ διηκονήσειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, compare 5.93, etc.: with _negative_, οὐ μὲν γάρ τι κακὸν βασιλευέμεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.392, etc.; ὤμοσα, μὴ μὲν.. ἀναφῆναι 4.254, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.118, 179; ἔξαρνος ἦν, μὴ μὲν ἀποκτεῖναι [prev. author] 3.67, compare 99: without _negative_, ἀνδρὸς μὲν τόδε σῆμα πάλαι κατατεθνηῶτος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.89}}: also in Trag., ἀκτὴ μὲν ἥδε τῆς περιρρύτου χθονός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1, compare 159 (_anapaest meter_), “OC” 44, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 676, 1129, etc.; καὶ μέν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.269, 9.632, etc.; οὐδὲ μὲν οὐδὲ.. 2.703, 12.212}}; γε μέν, compare γε {{Refs|1.5.}}
**A.I.2** an answering clause with δέ is sometimes implied, τὴν μὲν ἐγὼ σπουδῇ δάμνημ᾽ ἐπέεσσι her can I hardly subdue, [**but** all others easily], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.893}}; ὡς μὲν λέγουσι as **indeed** they say, [**but** as I believe not], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 8}}; καὶ πρῶτον μὲν ἦν αὐτῷ πόλεμος (with no ἔπειτα δέ to follow), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.14}}; so νῦν μέν σ᾽ ἀφήσω I will let you go **this time**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.81}}: to give force to assertions made by a person respecting himself, wherein opposition to other persons is implied, ὡς μὲν ἐμῷ θυμῷ δοκεῖ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.154}}; δοκεῖν μέν μοι ἥξει τήμερον [τὸ πλοῖον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43d}}: hence with the _person_ _pronoun_, ἐγὼ μέν νυν θεοῖσι ἔχω χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71}}; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐδέν (i.e. θέλω) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 498; ἐμοῦ μὲν οὐχ ἑκόντος [prev. author] “Aj.” 455; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ οἶδα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.12, compare 4.2.45, etc.}}: with the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_, τούτου μὲν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἐγὼ σοφώτερός εἰμι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21d: generally, to emphasize the preceding word, πολλὴ μὲν ἡ μεταβολή μοι γέγονεν}} great **indeed** has been the change, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.1, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.1, etc.}}
**A.I.3** μέν is used alone in questions, when the answer is assumed, **I take it**, θέμις μὲν ἡμᾶς χρησμὸν εἰδέναι θεο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 676, compare “Ion” 520 (troch.), “Hipp.” 316, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1214}}; Ἕλλην μέν ἐστι καὶ Ἑλληνίζε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82b.}}
**A.II** μέν followed by δέ in the correlative clause or clauses, **on the one hand, on the other hand**; commonly in Classical Gr., less frequently in later Gr. (rare in NT
**A.II.1** μέν.., δέ.. (or when the correlative clause is _negative_, μέν.., οὐδέ.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.318, 536)}}, to mark opposition, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}—The opposed clauses commonly stand together, but are frequently separated by clauses, parenthetic or explanatory; e.g. μέν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.494 is answered by δέ in 511, 527f}}; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.2, πρῶτον μέν is answered by ἐπεὶ δέ}} in <{{Refs|*> 6; in [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.2, πρῶτον μέν is answered by θαυμαστὸν δέ in 1.2.1.}}
**A.II.2** to connect a series of clauses containing different matter, though with no opposition, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.18s q., 306f (five δέ-clauses), 433f (eight δέ-clauses),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14, 7.10s q.: frequently when the members of a group or class are distinctly specified, παῖδες δύο, πρεσβύτερος μὲν Ἀρταξέρξης, νεώτερος δὲ Κῦρος [prev. work] 1.1.1; τάφρος.., τὸ μὲν εὖρος ὀργυιαὶ πέντε, τὸ δὲ βάθος ὀργυιαὶ τρεῖς [prev. work] 1.7.14; πρῶτος μέν.., δεύτερος δέ.., τρίτος δέ.. [prev. work] 5.6.9}}; τότε μέν.., τότε δέ.., at **one** time.., at **another**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.1.9, etc.: especially with the _Article_ used as a _pronoun_}} ὁ μέν.., ὁ δέ..; τὸ μέν.., τὸ δέ.., etc.
**A.II.3** the principal word is frequently repeated, οἳ περὶ μὲν βουλὴν Δαναῶν, περὶ δ᾽ ἐστὲ μάχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.258, compare 288, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.70; ἔνι μὲν φιλότης, ἐν δ᾽ ἵμερος, ἐν δ᾽ ὀαριστύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.216; Ξέρξης μὲν ἄγαγεν.., Ξέρξης δ᾽ ἀπώλεσεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 550, compare 560, 694, 700 (all Lyric poetry)}}; χαλεπαίνει μὲν πρῳρεύς, χαλεπαίνει δὲ κυβερνήτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.20.}}
**A.II.4** one of the correlative clauses is sometimes independent, while the other takes the _participle_ or some other dependent form, ἐβλασφήμει κατ᾽ ἐμοῦ.., μάρτυρα μὲν.. οὐδένα παρασχόμενος.., παρεκελεύετο δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.11; οἱ ἀμφὶ βασιλέα, πεζοὶ μὲν οὐκέτι, τῶν δὲ ἱππέων ὁ λόφος ἐνεπλήσθη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.12, compare 2.1.7, 5.6.29; ὧν ἐπιμεμφομένα σ᾽ ἁδεῖα μὲν ἀντία δ᾽ οἴσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 123, compare “OC” 522 (bothlyr.); χωρὶς μὲν τοῦ ἐστερῆσθαι.., ἔτι δὲ καὶ.. δόξω ἀμελῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44}} b.
**A.II.5** μέν and δέ frequently oppose two clauses, whereof one is subordinate to the other in meaning or emphasis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀλλ᾽ ἐκεῖνο θαυμάζω, εἰ Λακεδαιμονίοις μέν ποτε.. ἀντήρατε,.. νυνὶ δὲ ὀκνεῖτ᾽ ἐξιέναι (for εἰ.. ἀντάραντες νυνὶ ὀκνεῖτε) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.24, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 116, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 34.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.11, etc.}}: so in an anacoluthon, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|τρία μὲν ὄντα.. ναυτικά.., τούτων δ᾽ εἰ περιόψεσθε τὰ δύο, κτλ., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36.}}
**A.II.6** μέν is not always answered by δέ, but frequently by other equivalent Particles, as ἀλλά, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.22f, 2.703f, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 176, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.17:—by μέντοι, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 350, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.189, etc.:—by ἀτάρ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.84, 124, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 342, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1051s q., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172c, etc. (so μέν.., αὐτάρ}}] in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.50, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.513, etc.):— by αὖ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.108, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.210:—by αὖθις, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 165:—by αὖτε, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.234, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.5:—by temporal Particles, πρῶτα μέν.., εἶτα.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 261}}; πρῶτον μέν.., μετὰ τοῦτο.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.5 - 7}}; μάλιστα μὲν δὴ.., ἔπειτα μέντοι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 350, compare “OT” 647:}} —rarely by μήν with _negative_, οὐδὲν μὴν κωλύει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268e; οὐ μὴν αὐταί γε [prev. author] “Phlb.” 12d.}}
**A.II.6.b** when the opposition is emphatic, δέ is sometimes strengthened, as ὅμως δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 785, “Ph.” 473, 1074, etc. (so ἀλλ᾽ ὅμως “El.” 450)}}; δ᾽ αὖ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.415, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.5}}; δ᾽ ἔμπης.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.561 - 2.}}
**A.II.6.c** μέν is sometimes answered by a _copulative_ Particle, κάρτιστοι μὲν ἔσαν καὶ καρτίστοις ἐμάχοντο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 267, compare 459, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.475, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1, “Tr.” 689, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 125 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: rarely in Prose, τρία μὲν ἔτη ἀντεῖχον.., καὶ οὐ πρότερον ἐνέδοσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65 (uncertain).}}
**B** μέν before other Particles:
**B.I** where each Particle retains its force,
**B.I.1** μὲν ἄρα, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus μέν ῥα, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.1, 6.312, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.127, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258d, “R.” 467d, etc.}}
**B.I.2** μὲν γάρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 62, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, etc.:— in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} there is frequently no second clause, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.173, 392, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1062, etc.; μὲν γὰρ δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.825; μὲν γάρ τε 17.727.}}
**B.I.3** μέν γε, when a general statement is explained in detail, Κορινθίοις μέν γε ἔνσπονδοί ἐστε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, compare 70, 6.86, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.46, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.27, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1382, “V.” 564, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 909.4.}}
**B.I.4** μὲν δή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.514, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, etc.}}: frequently used to express positive certainty, ἀλλ᾽ οἶσθα μὲν δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 627, compare “OT” 294; τὰ μὲν δὴ τόξ᾽ ἔχεις [prev. author] “Ph.” 1308}}; especially as a conclusion, τοῦτο μὲν δὴ.. ὁμολογεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 470b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.6, etc.}}: in closing a statement, τοιαῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 500, etc.: used in answers to convey full assent, ἦ μὲν δή (compare supr. A) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.348, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.33; καὶ μὲν δή.. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 409b}}; οὐ μὲν δή, to deny positively, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.238, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148e, etc.; οὐ μὲν δή.. γε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.3, 3.2.14}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὔ τι μὲν δή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187a.}}
**B.I.5** μὲν οὖν, see below 11.2.
**B.II** where the Particles combine so as to form a new sense,
**B.II.1** μέν γε **at all events, at any rate** (not in Trag.), τοῦτο μέν γ᾽ ἤδη σαφές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 154, compare “Nu.” 1172, “Lys.” 1165, “Ra.” 80, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39; μέν γέ που 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 559b, “Tht.” 147a.}}
**B.II.2** μὲν οὖν is frequently used with a corresponding δέ, so that each Particle retains its force, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.780, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.111, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 244, 843; “Ph.” 359, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5, etc.}}: but frequently also _absolutely_, **so then**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 65; ταῦτα μὲν οὖν παραλείψω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.3}}; especially in replies, sometimes in strong affirmation, παντάπασι μὲν οὖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158d; κομιδῇ μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 159e; πάνυ μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 159b; ἀνάγκη μὲν οὖν [prev. work] 189e}}; also to substitute a new statement so as to correct a preceding statement, **nay rather**, κακοδαίμω; Answ. βαρυδαίμων μὲν οὖν! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1102}}; μου πρὸς τὴν κεφαλὴν ἀποψῶ wipe your nose on my head: Answ. ἐμοῦ μὲν οὖν.. **nay** on mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 911, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1032 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 1090 (Lyric poetry), 1396, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1363, “El.” 1503, “OT” 705, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 241, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44b, “Grg.” 466a, 470b, “Prt.” 309d, etc.; also μὲν οὖν δή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 153; καὶ δὴ μὲν οὖν [prev. author] “OC” 31}}; compare οὐμενοῦν: in “NT” μενοῦν and μενοῦνγε", to begin a sentence, **yea rather,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.11.28, NT.Rom.9.20, etc., compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 322, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus —In _Ionic dialect_, μέν νυν is used for μὲν οὖν, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, 4.145, etc.}}
**B.II.3** by μέν τε, if δέ τε follows, the two clauses are more closely combined than by τε.., τε.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.139, +others; μέν τε is frequently answered by δέ alone, 16.28, +others}}; by ἀλλά, αὐτάρ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.727, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.215, +others}}; perhaps by ἠδέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341:— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus also uses μέν τε}} _absolutely_, when τε loses its force, as after ἦ, τίς, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.145, +others}}
**B.II.4** μέν τοι in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always occurs in speeches, where τοι can be regarded as the _dative_ of the _pronoun_: later, μέντοι is written as a single word, and is used:
**B.II.4.a** with a _conjunctive_ force, **yet, nevertheless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 320, 1054 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 413, etc.}}; and sometimes stands for δέ, answering to μέν, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.6 a.}}
**B.II.4.b** as an _adverb_, in strong protestations, οὐ μέντοι μὰ Δία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.49}}; in eager or positive assent, **of course**, φαμέν τι εἶναι..; Answ. φαμὲν μέντοι νὴ Δία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65d, +others: with a _negative_ to give emphasis to a question, οὐ σὺ μέντοι..}}; **why**, are you not.. ? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 309a, compare “Phdr.” 229b, “R.” 339b, etc.: sometimes to express impatience, ὄμνυμι γάρ σοι—τίνα μέντοι, τίνα θεῶ; [prev. author] “Phdr.” 236d}}; τί μ. πρῶτον ἦν, τί πρῶτον ἦ; **nay** what **was** the first? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 787; οὗτος, σὲ λέγω μ. [prev. author] “Ra.” 171}}; σὺ μέντοι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 44: with _imperative_, to enforce the command, τουτὶ μ. σὺ φυλάττου}} **only** take heed.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1100, compare “Av.” 661, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.8: in answers, γελοῖον μέντἂν εἴη}} **nay** it would be absurd, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158e}}; summing up a long temporal clause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.130.}}
**B.II.4.c** μέντοι γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.24, etc.; οὐ μ. γε 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 5: in later Gr. μέντοιγε}} stands first in the sentence, μ. οὐ θέλω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.897.13 (1st c.AD)}}; also γε μέντοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 938, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 778, 1292, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 600; ὅμως γε μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 61.}}
**B.II.4.d** καὶ μ. καί is used to add a point to be noted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 28, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331d}}; also καί.. μ., νῦν σοι καιρός ἐστιν ἐπιδείξασθαι τὴν παιδείαν, καὶ φυλάξασθαι μέντοι.. and **of course** to take care.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.15 (variant), compare 1.8.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339c, “Tht.” 143a.}}
**B.II.4.e** ἀλλὰ μέντοι **well, if it comes to that**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.16}}; **well, of course**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331e, etc.}}; compare μέντον.
**C** for μέν after other Particles, see each Particle.
**D** Position of μέν. Like δέ, it usually stands as the second word in a sentence. But when a sentence begins with words common to its subordinate clauses, μέν stands second in the first of these clauses, as ἥδε γὰρ γυνὴ δούλη μέν, εἴρηκεν δ᾽ ἐλεύθερον λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 63}}; οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι ἐτάξαντο μέν.., ἡσύχαζον δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.73, compare 113, etc. It also attaches itself to words which mark opposition, as πρῶτον μέν, τότε μέν, ἐγὼ μέν}}, even when these do not stand first: sometimes however it precedes them, ὡς μὲν ἐγὼ οἶμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228b; ὡς μέν τινες ἔφασαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.28}}. It generally stands between the _Article_ and Noun, or the _preposition_ and its Case: but if special stress is laid on the Noun, this is sometimes neglected, as οἱ Τεγεᾶται μὲν ἐπηυλίσαντο, Μαντινῆς δὲ ἀπεχώρησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.134}}; ἀνὰ τὸ σκοτεινὸν μέν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.22; also τῇ σῇ μὲν εὐδαιμονίῃ, τῇ ἐμεωυτοῦ δὲ κακοδαιμονίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87}}.
**D.II** μέν is frequently repeated:
**D.II.1** when, besides the opposition of two main clauses, a subordinate opposition is introduced into the first, ὁ μὲν ἀνὴρ τοιαῦτα μὲν πεποίηκε τοιαῦτα δὲ λέγει, ὑμῶν δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9, compare 5.8.24, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.104, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.214, 23.208.}}
**D.II.2** in apodosi with the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ or _adverb_, τὸν μὲν καλέουσι θέρος, τοῦτον μὲν προσκυνέουσι, τὸν δὲ χειμῶνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121; ὅσοι μὲν δὴ νομοῦ τοῦ Θηβαίου εἰσί, οὗτοι μέν [νυν].. αἶγας θύουσι· ὅσοι δὲ.. νομοῦ τοῦ Μενδησίου εἰσί, οὗτοι δὲ.. ὄϊς θύουσι [prev. work] 42, compare 3.108, +others}}; ὅτε μέν με οἱ ἄρχοντες ἔταττον.., τότε μὲν ἔμενον.., τοῦ δὲ θεοῦ τάττοντος,.. ἐνταῦθα δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28e, compare “Grg.” 512a.}}
**D.II.3** μέν used absolutely is frequently followed by a correlative μέν, εἰ μὲν οὖν ἡμεῖς μὲν.. ποιοῦμεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 421a.}}
**D.III** μέν is sometimes omitted (especially in Poetry) where it is implied in the following δέ, φεύγων, ὁ δ᾽ ὄπισθε διώκων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.157; ἐλευθεροῦτε πατρίδ᾽, ἐλευθεροῦτε δὲ παῖδας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 403; σφραγῖδε.. χρυσοῦν ἔχουσα τὸν δακτύλιον, ἡ δ᾽ ἑτέρα ἀργυροῦν “IG” 22.1388.45, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 396, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 221e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447b14, etc.}}

3306:
** μένω**,
_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ μενέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.486; 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} _present_ _participle_ μίνονσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 657.49 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ μένεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.42, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42}}: _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ _future_ μενέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.308, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.119}}; _Attic dialect_ μενῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 564, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἔμεινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.656, etc.}}: _perfect_ μεμένηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.321}}; compare **μίμνω**:—**stay, wait**:
**I** **stand fast**, in battle, οὐδ᾽ ἴφθιμοι Λύκιοι μένον, ἀλλὰ φόβηθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.659; μενέω καὶ τλήσομαι 11.317}}; φεύγειν μηδὲ μένειν Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.55, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.45, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 295; ἐμπέδως μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 854; ἀραρότως [prev. author] “Supp.” 945}}; μ. κατὰ χώραν, of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26.}}
**I.2** **stay at home, stay where one is**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.838; ἔντοσθε μένοντες 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 598; μ. αὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.62; οἴκοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 317; εἴσω δόμων [prev. author] “Th.” 232; κατ᾽ οἶκον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 656; ἐν δόμοις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.43, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 80; ἔνδον 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 1.3.}}
**I.2.b** **lodge, stay**, παρὰ ματρί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.186; πρὸς τοὺς γονέας 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 13; ἐκεῖ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.4.10 codices (perhaps οἴκοι),compare 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.5.}}
**I.2.c** μ. ἀπὸ ἧς ἀλόχοιο **stay away, be absent** from.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.292; ἀπὸ πτολέμοιο 18.64}}: and so _absolutely_, **to be a shirker**, ἴση μοῖρα μένοντι καὶ εἰ μάλα τις πολεμίζοι {{Refs|9.318.}}
**I.2.d** οἱ μένοντες, opposed to οἱ φεύγοντες (exiles), {{Refs|“IG” 12.10.27.}}
**I.3** **stay, tarry**, ἐς ἠέλιον καταδύντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.570; μενέουσι, εἰς ὅ κέ περ Τροίην διαπέρσομεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.45}}; **loiter, be idle**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.666, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 796; οἱ μένοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.19, etc.}}
**I.4** of things, **to be lasting, remain, stand**, στήλη μένει ἔμπεδον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.434; ἀσφαλὲς αἰὲν.. μένει οὐρανός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.4; τάδ᾽ αἰανῶς μένοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 672; αἰῶνα δ᾽ ἐς τρίτον μένει [prev. author] “Th.” 744 (Lyric poetry)}}; opposed to φέρεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 261d}}; εἰ μηδὲν μένει if nothing **is fixed**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 440a; τὴν μεμενηκυῖαν κρίσιν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ τῶν Στωικῶν” 339.15}}; οἱ μένοντες (i.e. ἀστέρες) **having no proper motion**, opposed to οἱ πλάνητες, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 290a21; μένων κύκλος 4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 12, +others, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Hyp.” 1.3}}; μένουσιν ἀριστοκρατίαι **are stable, permanent**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308a3.}}
**I.4.b** μ. παρά τινι **remain** in one's possession, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CPR” 18.37 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**I.5** of condition, **remain as one was**, of a maiden, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.263}}; τῶν βεβαίως μοι φίλων μενόντων Ps.-Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.11; τὸ νόμισμα βούλεται μένειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133b14}}: generally, **stand, hold good**, ἢν μείνωσιν ὅρκοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1000; μένειν τὸ ὅρκιον κατὰ χώρην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.201; εἰ τὰ πρότερον μένει ἡμῖν ὁμολογήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b}}; μ. τὰ βουλήματα καὶ οὐ μεταρρεῖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167b7}}; of circumstances, οὐ μενεῖν κατὰ χώραν τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.76}}; οὐδαμὰ ἐν τὠυτῷ μ., of prosperity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5; μένειν ἐμπέδοις φρονήμασι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 169}}; μ. ἐπὶ τούτων [ἃ κατέστραπται] **remain contented** with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.9; μ. ἐπὶ τούτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.7}}; ἐπὶ τούτῳ τῷ βίῳ μ. **be content with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 466c, compare 496b; μ. ἐλεύθερον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 145}}; of wine, **keep good**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.2.8.}}
**I.6** **abide** by an opinion, conviction, etc., ἐπὶ τῷ ἀληθεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e}}; μενέτωσαν ἐν τοῖς διαγνωσθεῖσι Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.94}}; ὁ μένων the party which **observes** an engagement, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PTeb.” 391.24 (1st c.AD).}}
**I.7** _impersonal_ with _infinitive_, **it remains** for one to do, μένει.. ἐκτίνειν θέμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 435 (Lyric poetry); τοῖς πᾶσιν ἀνθρώποισι κατθανεῖν μένει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 733.}}
**II** _transitive_, of persons, **await, expect** (compare μίμνω), ἡμέρας μεῖναι φάος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 66; τοὺς Ἰλλυριούς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.124, compare 8.78}}; especially **await** an attack without blenching, Δαναοὶ Τρῶας μένον ἔμπεδον, οὐδὲ φέβοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.527, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 436}}; of a rock, **bide** the storm, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.620; ἀπορίαν γὰρ οὐ μενῶ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 740: reversely of things, τὸ μόρσιμον γὰρ τόν τ᾽ ἐλεύθερον μένει}} **awaits** him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 103}}; ἐπίξηνον μένει (i.e. με) {{NT|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1277; ἀγὼν γὰρ ἄνδρας οὐ μένει λελειμμένους [prev. author] “fragment” 37; δεσμά με καὶ θλίψεις μένουσιν NT.Act.20.23.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **wait for**, ἦ μένετε Τρῶας σχεδὸν ἐλθέμε; **wait** ye **for** the Trojans to come nigh? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.247; οὐ μενῶ πόσιν μολεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 255}}; μένον δ᾽ ἐπὶ ἕσπερον ἐλθεῖν they **waited for** evening's coming on, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.422, etc.; οὐκ ἔμειν᾽ ἐλθεῖν τράπεζαν νυμφίαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.16}}; τί μένεις.. ἰένα; why **wait** to go? {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 351}}; μένω δ᾽ ἀκοῦσαι I **wait**, i.e. **long**, to hear, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 677, compare “Ag.” 459 (Lyric poetry). (Cf. O 4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus}} **man**- 'wait', Latin _maneo._)

3307:
** μερίζω**,
_Doric dialect_ **μερ-ίσδω**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 2.31}}: _Attic dialect_ _future_ -ιῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 131c}}: _aorist_ ἐμέρισα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.27}}; _Doric dialect_ _participle_ μερίξας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 99d}}: _perfect_ μεμέρικα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 4}}:—_middle_, _future_ -ίσομαι {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus in Rhetores Graeci 8.306 W., -ιοῦμαι LXX.Pro.14.18}}: _aorist_ ἐμερισάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.24, etc.}}: _perfect_ μεμέρισμαι [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.34 (variant{νενέμημαι})}}]:—_passive_, _future_ μεριοῦμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 664a27, μερισθήσομαι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.8, 6.4.4}}: _aorist_ ἐμερίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 56d, etc.}}: _perfect_ μεμέρισμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prm.” 144b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.5, etc.}}: (μερίς):—**divide, distribute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 131c; μ. τὸ ἄπειρον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 204a34; μ. [ἀρχήν] τινα εἰς πλείους [prev. author] “Pol.” 1321b37}}; καθ᾽ ἕκαστον εἶδος πολιτείας μ. **make a division**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1304b19: _absolutely_}}**split up the amount**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1268b15: Arith., μ. τι παρά τι, εἴς τι}}**divide** by.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 4.33, 34, +others: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.223.}}
**2** **assign a part, allot**, ἐφ᾽ ἕκαστον μ. τὸ φιλεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1213b5}}; μ. τοὺς τόκους πρὸς τὸν πλοῦν **allot** the interest according to the voyage, i.e. **pay only a part** of it, if a part only of the voyage has been performed, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.49}}; **allot, assign** spheres of duty, τινι {{Refs|“PHamb.” 24.6 (_passive_)}}; **apportion, allocate** funds, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.29.18, +others, “SIG” 577.22 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC)}} (_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 22.1672.116, +others); τὸ μὲν εἰς δαπάνην, τὸ δ᾽ εἰς θησαυρισμόν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.71 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, compare “Sto.” 339.15, 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.13}}; **bestow,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 713.29 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; κατὰ τόπους μ. τὰς ἀναγραφάς **divide, arrange** them, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 9}}; μ. τινὰ τοῖς ποιηταῖς, i. e. make one a theme for several tragedies, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 4.18}}:—_passive_, **to be delivered over**, εἰς ὕβριν καὶ δουλείαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Chor. p.216 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**3** **sever, cut off**, πελέκει χεῖρα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 2.21.}}
**4** **apply by turns**, τὰς χεῖρας τῷ τε τείχει καὶ τῇ λύρᾳ {{Refs|[prev. work] 21.12.}}
**II** _middle_, μερίζεσθαί τι **divide among themselves**, χρήματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.10, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.31; τι μετά τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.18; πρός τινα τὴν ἀρχήν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.10.6}}; **take possession of**, τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.35}}; ἠρόμην αὐτὸν πότερα μεμερισμένος εἴη πρὸς τὸν ἀδελφόν whether he **had gone shares** with his brother, {{Refs|[prev. author] 47.34.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **get a portion of**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.24.}}
**II.2.b** **take part in**, τοῦ ἀδικήματος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1137a2.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be divided**, κατὰ μέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.9 (assuming variant); ἐπὶ πολλά 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 86; τὸ μερίζεσθαι τὰς οὐσίας εἰς ὁποσονοῦν πλῆθος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265b3}}; μ. πρὸς ἑκάστην διοίκησιν (i.e. αἱ πρόσοδοι) **are distributed**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1321b32}}; ἐς πᾶσαν πεῖραν μ. make attempts in every direction, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.78, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 24.1; μερίζεταί τι ἀπό τινος [prev. author] “Nav.” 8.}}
**III.2** **to be dispersed**, ὕδωρ ὑπὸ πυρὸς μερισθέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 56d}}; **to be split up**, ἄνθρωπος πληγῇ τινι μεριζόμενος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 32}}: _metaphorically_, **have divided interests, disperse one's energy**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Chor. p.11 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; also, **to be split into parties** or **factions**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.21.9, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.1, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.10.4}}; μεμέρισται ὁ Χριστό; {{NT|NT.1Cor.1.13.}}
**III.3** **to be reckoned as part**, ἐν τῇ ἀρχῇ τινος μ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.5.}}

3308:
** μέριμν-α**, ἡ,
**care, thought, solicitude**, ὅν τε θαμιναὶ ἐπιστρωφῶσι μέριμναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 44, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.54, etc.}}; μ. τινός **care for**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 132, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1460; ἀμφί τι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 843 (Lyric poetry); ἐλθεῖν ἐς τόδε μερίμνης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 244, compare 404}}: _plural_, **cares**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 178, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 11.1, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 13.8, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 343, etc.; γνώμαις δὲ λεπταῖς.. ξύνειμι καὶ μερίμναις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1404; λύπας, μερίμνας, ἁρπαγάς 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 88.}}—Rare in early Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 89, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Amatores” 134b (_plural_); later ἡ τοῦ βίου μ. “UPZ” 20.29 (2nd c.BC)}}; μ. καὶ φροντίς{{NT+2nd c.AD+|Aristeas 271; μ. τοῦ αἰῶνος NT.Matt.13.22, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 131.3, etc.}}
**2** concrete, **object of care** or **thought**, μεγάλην σε πατὴρ ἐφύτευσε μ. θνητοῖς ἀνθρώποισι {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 160.}}
**3** **pursuit, ambition**, especially in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.108, “N.” 3.69; κουφόταται μ. 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.69: _sing._, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.92.}}
**4** Κηΐα μ. the Cean poet's **fancy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 18.11.}}
**5** **anxious mind**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 460 (Lyric poetry)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _smárati_ 'remember', Latin _memor._)

3310:
** μερ-ίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
(μέρος) **part, portion**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 266a, etc.; κρεῶν 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 45 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.82, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.4, LXX.Gen.43.34, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 17, Hippoloch. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.130d; μ. δίκρεως “SIG” 1013.4 (Chios, 4th c.BC); γυναιξὶν μερίδας ποησάντωσανἴσας [prev. work]1044.42 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 345.16}}; τὴν μ. τὴν ἐξ Ἀρείου πάγου **portion of sacrificial meat** allotted to a member of the court, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.56; τὴν τοῦ Προμηθέως μ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 26; μ. τῆς οὐσίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 235.5, compare “PLond.” 3.880 (2nd c.BC)}}; **share** in a mine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.3: generally, ἐκτῶν λόγων μ. ἑκατέρῳ ἴση ἐστί (with variants), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.51; ἐν προσθήκῃ μ. οὐ μικρά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.14.}}
**2** **contribution, quota**, μερίδ᾽ εἰς σωτηρίαν ὑπάρχειν **contribute** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 21.70; μεγάλη.. μ. καὶ πλεονεξία [prev. work]184; τὰ δεῖπνα πρὸς μερίδα γίγνεται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.644c; ἀποστείλαντος μερίδα τοῦ φίλου 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 17.2.}}
**3** **plot** of ground, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.4Ki.9.21, +others, “CIL” 11.1147 pp.iii 2, vi86, +others (from Veleia); μ. γῆς “PStrassb.” 84.3 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3.b** **region, district,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3pp.28,78 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 32.18 (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 177.12 (Egypt, 2nd-1st c.BC), NT.Act.16.12, etc.}}
**3.c** Astrology texts, ἡ κάτω μ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.15.}}
**3.d** _metaphorically_, **province**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}} **adThem.**266b.
**II** **division, class**, πολιτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 238, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**adThem.**253c; especially in political sense, **party, faction**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 692b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.64; ἡ Σύλλα μ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.203b.}}
**III** ={μέρος} IV. 3, εἰς ἀρετῆς μερίδα τὸ ψεύσασθαι τιθέμενος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Mar.” 29; χρυσὸν ἐν οὐδενὸς μερίδι ποιήσασθαι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.28.4.}}
**IV** of persons, κακὰ μ. you bad **lot!** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.433 (3rd c.BC: Tymnes Epigrammaticus), compare 355.3 (3rd c.BC: Damagetus Epigrammaticus).}}

3311:
** μερ-ιστέον**,
**one must divide**, τὸ καθόλου εἰς γένη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.4 M.}}; **one must distribute**, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 83.12.}}
**II** _adjective_ μεριστέος, α, ον, **to be divided**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 156.1.}}

3313:
** μέρος**, εος, τό,
(μείρομαι) first in {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 399 (see. infr. IV), “hymnus ad Mercurium” 53 (see. infr. II. 2)}}:— **share, portion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.77, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.145, “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene), etc.; μέρος ἔχοντα Μουσᾶν 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.71; ἔχει δόμων μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 483; κτεάνων μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1574 (_anapaest meter_); συμβαλέσθαι τὸ μ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.11; τὰ μ. τινῶν κομίζεσθαι [prev. passage]; λαβεῖν τῆς μεθόδου τὸ μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295a3}}; of work put out to contract, **allotment,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.463.7, 26.}}
**2** **heritage, lot, destiny**, μεθέξειν τάφου μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 507; ἔχετον κοινοῦ θανάτου μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 147 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τοῦτο γὰρ.. σπάνιον μ. is a rare **portion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 474 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἀπὸ μέρους προτιμᾶσθαι from **considerations of rank** or **family**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37.}}
**II** **one's turn**, ἐπείτε αὐτῆς μ. ἐγίνετο τῆς ἀπίξιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.69; μ. ἑκατέρῳ νέμειν [prev. author] 2.173}}; ὅταν ἥκῃ μ. ἔργων **the turn** or **time** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 827 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 540b}}; ἀγγέλου μ. **his turn of duty** as messenger, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 291.}}
**II.2** with Preps., ἀνὰ μέρος **in turn, successively**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 478, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a17; κατὰ μέρος “hymnus ad Mercurium” 53, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26, etc.}}; κατὰ μ. λέγειν **severally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157b; κατὰ μέρη ἄκουε [prev. work] 182b}}; τὰ κατὰ μέρος the **particulars**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 23, “D.” 1.22; τὸ κατὰ μ. ἄστρον [prev. work]3.9; ἐν μέρεϊ}} **in turn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, +others}}; κλῦθί νυν ἐν μ., ἀντάκουσον ἐν μ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 332 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 198}}; **by turns, in succession**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 332, 1192, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.93; ἐν μ. καὶ ἐφεξῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 819b}}; ἐν τῷ μέρει **in one's turn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.70, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 452, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 32, 497, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 462a}}; ἐν τῷ μ. καὶ παρὰ τὸ μ. **in** and **out of turn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}; παρὰ μέρος **in turn, by turns**, ἄρχειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 10, cf. Ant. 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista 30.1, 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.8.10, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.33 P.; [ἡ ψυχὴ] παρὰ μ. ἐν τῇ γενέσει γίνεται καὶ ἐν τοῖς θεοῖς ἐστιν 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 206}} (but also, **partially**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.66).}}
**III** **the part one takes in** a thing, μέτεστι χὑμῖν τῶν πεπραγμένων μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1299}}; ὑμέτερον μ. [ἐστί] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 180a.}}
**III.2** frequently in periphrases, τοὐμὸν μέρος, τὸ σὸν μ., **my** or **thy part**, i.e. simply I or **me, thou** or **thee**, ὅσον τὸ σὸν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1509, compare “Ant.” 1062, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45d: _absolutely_ as}} _adverb_, τοὐμὸν μ. **as to me**, οὐ καμῇ τοὐμὸν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1215, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 678}}; τὸ σὸν μέρος **as to thee**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1366; τοὐκείνου μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 989: rarely, κατὰ τὸ σὸν μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 328e.}}
**IV** **part**, opposed to the whole, ὡρέων τρίτατον μ. {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 399, etc.}}; τρίτον κασιγνητᾶν μ., i. e. one of three sisters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 12.11; μέρει τινὶ τῶν βαρβάρων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1}}; τὰ δύο μ. two-thirds,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 104, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.143, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.101; τρία μέρη.., τὸ δὲ τέταρτον 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 130.17 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; οὐδὲν ἂν μέρος οὖσαι φανεῖεν τῶν.. no **fraction** of.., i. e. infinitesimal compared with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.43, compare 12.54}}; ὅσα ἄλλα μ. ἐντὸς τοῦ Ἴστρου **parts of the country, regions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.96, compare 4.98}}; ξυγκαταδουλοῦν.. τὸ τῆς θαλάσσης μ., i. e. the sea **as their part** of the business, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.46}}: hence, **branch, business, matter**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 17, “Pk.” 107, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.4.2, 1.20.8, +others, “PRyl.” 127 (1st c.AD)}}; τὰ τοῦ σώματος μέλη καὶ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 795e}}; **division** of an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.23, etc.}}; **class** or **party**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.292}}; of the **factions** in the circus, πρασίνων μ. {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 145.2 (6th c.AD)}}; **party** in a contract or lawsuit, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 168.24 (2nd c.AD), “PRein.” 44.34 (2nd c.AD)}}; **caste**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.39}}:—special uses, in Geometry texts, **direction**, ἐπὶ θάτερον μ. interpolated in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1.13, compare 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.27, +others}}: Arith., **submultiple**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7 “Def.” 3, 4}}; τὰ μ. the **denominators** of fractions, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Stereom.” 2.14: Grammars, μ. τῆς λέξεως}} **part** of speech, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b20, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 2: more frequently μ. λόγου 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.4, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 4.6, +others}}; μ. λόγου, also, = **word**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.159, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 1.4 (see. λόγος I 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus 3 c)}}; **section** of a document, Mitteis {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 28.30 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**IV.2** _absolutely_ as _adverb_, μέρος τι **in part**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.30, etc.}}; μέρος μέν τι.., μέρος δέ τι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.12}}; τὸ πλεῖστον μ. for the most **part**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 22.10.}}
**IV.2.b** with Preps., κατά τι μέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757e; κατὰ τὸ πολὺ μ. [prev. author] “Ti.” 86d}}; ἐκ μέρους in **part**, γινώσκομεν {{NT|NT.1Cor.13.9 (but ἐκ μ. τινός}} by the **side** of, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.6.8}}; ἐκ μ. τῶν ὁρίων{{LXX|LXX.Num.20.16; ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς μέρους [prev. work] 8.2)}}; ἐκ τοῦ πλείστου μ. for the most **part**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.2.4}}; ἀπὸ μέρους in **part**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|Antip.Stoic. 3.249, “BGU” 1201.15 (1st c.AD), NT.2Cor.2.5; ἐπὶ μέρους 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 2; τὰς ἐπὶ μέρους γράφειν πράξεις}} **special** histories, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.7.6; αἱ ἐπὶ μ. συντάξεις [prev. author] 3.32.10}}; πρὸς μέρος **in proportion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.22, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.32.}}
**IV.3** ἐν μέρει τινὸς τιθέναι, etc., to put **in the class of**.., consider **as** so and so, εἰ ἐν ἀρετῆς τιθεῖς μέρει τὴν ἀδικίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348e; οὐ τίθημ᾽ ἐν ἀδικήματος μ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.148; also ἐν τεκμηρίου μ. ποιεῖσθαι τἀδίκημα [prev. author] 44.50}}; ἐν οὐδενὸς εἶναι μ. to be **as** no one, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.18; μήτ᾽ ἐν ἀνθρώπου μ. μήτ᾽ ἐν θεοῦ ζῆν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 240.2}}; ἐν προσθήκης μ. **as** an appendage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.8; ἐν ὑπηρέτου καὶ προσθήκης μ. γίγνεσθαι [prev. author] 3.31; ἐν χάριτος μ. [prev. author] 21.165; τοῦτ᾽ ἐν εὐεργεσίας ἀριθμήσει μ. [prev. work]166; ἐν ἰδιώτου μ. διαγαγεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.24; ὡς ἐν παιδιᾶς μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 424d; also εἰς εὐεργεσίας μέρος καταθέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.17.}}
**IV.4** in local sense, **district,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 2113.25 (4th c.AD).}}
**IV.5** in Neo-Platonism, **by way of species** or **element**, ἐν μέρει καὶ ὡς στοιχεῖον {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 193}}; οὕτω ὁ μέγας Ἰάμβλιχος ἐνόησεν τὸ ἓν ὂν ἐν μέρει ἑκάτερον{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 176; πάντα μὲν ἅμα, ἐν μέρει δὲ ἕκαστον 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.18.}}

3314:
** μεσημβρ-ία**
(μέσος, ἡμέρα), _Ionic dialect_ **μεσαμβρίη** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 3.8, +others}}: ἡ:— **midday**, Ζεὺς ἐκ μεσημβρίης ἔθηκε νύκτα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74. 3; ἐν μεσημβρίας θάλπει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 746; ἀποκλιναμένης τῆς μεσαμβρίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104}}; μεσαμβρίης at **noon**, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; ἔτρωγ᾽.. σῦκα τῆς μεσημβρίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 463, compare 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 106, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 80, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 500; τῇ μεσαμβρίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place]; ἐν μεσημβρίᾳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100; νύκτα ἐν μ. ἐπαγόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 897d; ἅμα μεσημβρίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.1}}; ἐκ μεσημβρίας just after **noon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 372; σμικρόν τι μετὰ μεσημβρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1499; ἤδη ἦν μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c}}; μ. ἵσταται 'tis high **noon**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 242a.}}
**II** **the South**, Μολοσσῶν πρὸς μεσημβρίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 108 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; [ποταμὸς] ῥέων ἀπὸ μεσαμβρίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6}}; κεῖται πόλις πρὸς μεσαμβρίην{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 142; τὰ πρὸς μ. [prev. author] 7.113, compare “IG” 7.3073.95 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC). [μεσημβρῑη “APl.” 4.369.]}}

3315:
** μεσῑτ-εύω**,
**act as arbiter** or **mediator**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.4.3, “BGU” 709.18 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 39.2, etc.}}; τισι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 906.7 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; μ. ὅρκῳ {{NT|NT.Heb.6.17}}; **act as go-between**, τῇ Πασιφάῃ πρὸς τὸν ἔρωτα τοῦ ταύρου {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1166.25.}}
**2** _transitive_, **mediate, negotiate**, τὴν διάλυσιν μ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.34.3; τὰς συνθήκας “OGI” 437.76 (Pergam., 1st c.BC), 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.59, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.71; τὰς διαλλαγάς 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 130.29 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**3** **pledge, mortgage** property, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CPR” 1.19 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**4**[{{Refs 1st c.AD+|={μεσιδιόω}, χειρόγραφον παρά τινι Möller “Papyrus Berl. Mus.” 2.11 (_passive_, 1st c.AD), 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}]
**5** **add as a third constituent**, χάλκανθον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Zos.Alch. p.113 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II** _intransitive_, τὰ χρήματα μ. **lies on deposit with a stakeholder**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus “Fragmenta” 183.}}
**II.2** **lie between**, μονάδος καὶ δεκάδος {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 44.}}

3316:
** μεσῑτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**mediator, umpire, arbitrator,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PLille 28.11 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.17.8 (_plural_), NT.Gal.3.19, etc.; τῶν ὁμολογιῶν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.54; θεοῦ καὶ ἀνθρώπων NT.1Tim.2.5}}; **stakeholder,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PStrassb. 1.41.14 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** _feminine_ μεσῖτις, ιδος, φιλίας μεσῖτιν τράπεζαν παραθέμενοι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 27; φιλίας μ. ἡδονή [prev. work] 54.}}
**II** **in a middle position**, of a limb, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).861.}}

3319:
** μέσος**, η, ον,
also {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus (see. ἰμέσος, μεσακόθεν)}}; _Epic dialect_ **μέσσος** (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.12, “IG” 11(4).1064b32, and Lyric poetry, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.224, and sometimes in Trag., 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 403 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1247 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 635 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1223, 1236, “fragment” 255.5)}}, _Boeotian dialect_, Cretan dialect **μέττος**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.2420.20 (3rd c.BC), “GDI” 5000}} ii **b** 2 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC)}}:—**middle, in the middle**,
**I** of Space, especially with Nouns, of the **middle** point or part, μ. σάκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.258; ἱστίον 1.481}}; οὐρανός **zenith**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.400}}; μ. ἀπήνης from **mid** chariot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 812}}; ἐν αἰθέρι μ. in **mid**-air, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 416}}; μ. μετώπῳ in the **middle** of the forehead, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 128.30 (1st c.AD)}}: in Prose frequently preceding the _Article_, κατὰ μέσον τὸν σταθμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.14; ἐν μ. τῇ χώρᾳ [prev. work] 2.1.11}}; ἐκ μ. τῆς νήσου, κατὰ μ. τὴν νῆσον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 113d, 119d}}; ἐπὶ μέσου τοῦ τμάματος at the **middle point** of the segment, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1.6; ἁ ἐπὶ μέσαν τὰν βάσιν ἀγομένα (i.e. εὐθεῖα) [prev. work] 12}}: sometimes following the Noun, ἐν τῇ ἀγορᾷ μέσῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.12}}: less frequently **midmost, central**, of three or more objects, μ. ὁδός {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 220, 331}}; ὁ μ. [δάκτυλος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 523c}}; τὸ μ. στῖφος the **central** division of the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.13}}; μέσον, τό, **centre**, ἡ ἐπὶ τὸ μ. φορά {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21.}}
**I.b** with a Verb, ἔχεται μ. **by the middle, by the waist**, proverbial from the wrestling-ring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 387 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ach.” 571 (Lyric poetry), “Nu.” 1047, “Ra.” 469; μέσην λαβόντα [prev. author] “Ach.” 274, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.107, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.17; ὁ πέπλος ἐρράγη μ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 25.5.}}
**I.c** with _genitive_, **midway between**, ἑνὸς καὶ πλήθους τὸ ὀλίγον μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 303a (also μ. ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα, [prev. passage]):— 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus has μέσος ἀπὸ [τοῦ κρατῆρος] τοῦ τε πέτρου “OC” 1595.}}
**I.2** of Time, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in phrase μέσον ἦμαρ midday, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.111, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.288, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.113; μέσαι νύκτες 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 52, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.12, etc.; θέρευς ἔτι μέσσου ἐόντος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 502; χειμῶνος μέσου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 569.1; μ. ἡμέρα 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.5.9}}; μ. ἡλικία **middle** age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 316c: so μέσοι τὴν ἡλικίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 5}}; μέσος ἀκμῆς variant in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.164.}}
**I.3** _metaphorically_, **impartial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.83, “PLond.” 1.113(1).27 (6th c.AD).}}
**I.3.b** **inter-mediate**, frequently with _genitive_, μ. τις γέγονα χρηματιστὴς τοῦ τε πάππου καὶ τοῦ πατρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330b; ψιλὸν μὲν τὸ π ¯, δασὺ δὲ τὸ φ ¯, μέσον δὲ ἀμφοῖν τὸ β ¯ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14 (see. below d); ἡ τρίτη καὶ μ. τῶν εἰρημένων δυεῖν ἁρμονιῶν [prev. work] 24; ὁ μ. χαρακτήρ [prev. work] 21}}; **indeterminate**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 28}}; τὰ μ. **things indifferent** (neither good nor bad), {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.135, +others}}; of words such as τύχη, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 626.38}}; ζῴδια (neither lucky nor unlucky) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 93.9; μ. δίαιτα Diocl.fragment 141, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.46.}}
**I.3.c** Grammars, of Verbs, **middle**, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1846.30, etc.}}; μ. διάθεσις, σχήματα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 226.10, 210.18}}; μ. ἐνεστώς present **middle**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 278.25.}}
**I.3.d** Grammars, of consonants, Latin _mediae_, i. e. β ¯ γ ¯ δ ¯, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 631.23}}: but also of **semi-vowels**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 18c: of accent, ὀξύτητι καὶ βαρύτητι καὶ τῷ μέσῳ}}, i. e. the **circumflex**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b33.}}
**II** **middling, moderate**,
**II.1** of size, μέσοι ὀφθαλμοί, ὦτα, γλῶττα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 492a8, 33, b31}}; μ. μεγέθει{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 496a21, “PPetr.” 1p.37 (3rd c.BC)}}; μ. alone, **of middle height,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PGrenf. 2.23 (a) ii 3 (2nd c.BC), “POxy.” 73.13 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**II.2** of class or quality, πάντων μέσ᾽ ἄριστα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 335; παντὶ μέσῳ τὸ κράτος θεὸς ὤπασεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 529 (Lyric poetry); μ. ἐν πόλει 6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 12}}; μ. ἀνήρ a man **of middle rank**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107; μ. πολίτης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54; τὰ μ. τῶν πολιτῶν [prev. author] 3.82 (so τῶν ἀνὰ πόλιν τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.52)}}; οἱ μ., between οἱ εὔποροι and οἱ ἄποροι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289b31, 1295b3; οἱ μ. πολῖται [prev. work] 1296a19; τὸ μ. [prev. work] 1295b37}}; μ. [πολιτεία{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|] [prev. work] 1296a7; ὁ μ. βίος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 9}}; **mediocre**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346d; τῶν ἑταιρῶν αἱ μ. Theopomp. Comedy texts 21.}} _adverb_ μέσως, ἱκανόν **fairly** adequate, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.4S.}}
**III** μέσον, τό, **midst, intervening space**, mostly with Preps.,
**III.a** ἐν μέσσῳ, ={ἐν μεταιχμίῳ}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.69, 90; ἐν τῷ μ.}}**in the midst,** {{NT|NT.Matt.14.6}}; ἡ 'ν μέσῳ [μοῖρα] σῴζει πόλεις the **middle** class, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 244: without ἐν, ἔμβαλε μέσσῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.444; ἔνθορε μέσσῳ 21.233; μέσσῳ ἀμφοτέρων 3.416, 7.277; τῶνδέ τ᾽ ἐν μ. πεσεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 583; ἐν μ. λόγους ἔχειν [prev. author] “Hel.” 630; μῆκος ἐν μ. χρόνου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 735; χρόνος οὑν μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 589 (troch.)}}; τὰ ἐν μ. what **went between**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 583}}; οἱ ἐν μ. λόγοι the **intervening** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1364, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 819; κλίνης ἐν μ. [prev. author] “Hec.” 1150}}; ἐν μ. ἡμῶν καὶ βασιλέως **between** us and him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.3; σοφίας καὶ ἀμαθίας ἐν μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203e}}; ἐν μ. νυκτῶν at **mid**night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.52}}; ἆθλα κείμεν᾽ ἐν μέσῳ **offered for competition** (compare below b), {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.5, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 994, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.21}}; ἡ τιμὴ ἐν τῷ μέσῳ ἔστω **deposited** with the court, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.90: without ἐν, καὶ μέσῳ πάντες καὶ χωρὶς ἕκαστος}} both **collectively** and severally, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).872.27,31,38, +others (from Tenos): in _plural_, κεῖτο δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐν μέσσοισι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.507; ἐν μέσοισ᾽ 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.7}}; ἐν μέσῳ εἶναι τοῦ συμμεῖξαι to stand **in the way of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.26}}; ἡ γὰρ θάλαττα ἐν τῷ μ. is an **obstacle**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ath.” 2.2; οὐδεὶς ἐν μέσσῳ γείτων πέλεν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.17; οὐδὲν ἂν ἦν ἐν μ. πολεμεῖν ἡμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.183}}; compare ἰμέσος.
**III.b** ἐς μέσον, ἐς μ. ἀμφοτέρων, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus for ἐς μεταίχμιον, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.79, 6.120}}; ἀνδρὶ δὲ νικηθέντι γυναῖκ᾽ ἐς μέσσον ἔθηκε **deposited** her **as a prize** (compare above a),{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.704; ἐς μ. δεικνύναι τινί τι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 42.3}}; ἐς μ. ἵεσθαι, ἐλθεῖν, παρελθεῖν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 514 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.183, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 9; ἐς μέσον ἀμφοτέροισι.. δικάσσατε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.574}}; ἐς τὸ μ. φέρειν bring forward **publicly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.97, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.139; ἐς τὸ μ. λέγεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.129}}; ἐς μ. Πέρσῃσι καταθεῖναι τὰ πρήγματα to give up the power **in common to all**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.80; ἐς μ. τὴν ἀρχὴν τιθεὶς ἰσονομίην ὑμῖν προαγορεύω [prev. work] 142.}}
**III.c** ἐκ τοῦ μέσου **away**, ἐκ μ. ἀνελεῖν {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.36, 18.294; [χειρόγραφον] ἦρκεν ἐκ τοῦ μ. NT.Col.2.14, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.3.15}}; also ἐκ μ. **a half**, ἔτη ὀκτὼ καὶ ἔνατον ἐκ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.133}}; also ἐκ μ. κατῆστο remained **in the middle**, i. e. neutral {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἐκ 1.6 near the end), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83, compare 4.118, 8.22, 73.}}
**III.d** διὰ μέσου **between**, τὸ διὰ μ. ἔθνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.104; διὰ μ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.3}}; διὰ μ. γενέσθαι **intervene**, of an event, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20: with _genitive_, διὰ μέσου τῆς πόλεως ῥεῖ ποταμός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.23; διὰ μ. ῥεῖ τούτων ποταμός [prev. work] 1.4.4, etc.}}; τὸ τούτων διὰ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 805e}}; also οἱ διὰ μέσου the **middle** party, the **moderates**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.75, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.25}}; τὸ διὰ μ. the **middle** class, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296a8}}; of Time, ὁ διὰ μ. χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.112}}; ἡ διὰ μ. ξύμβασις an **interim** agreement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26}}; διὰ μέσου, as a figure of speech, **use of parenthesis**, {{Refs|Herodianus Rhetor “de Figuris - Rhet.” p.95S.}}
**III.e** ἀν (ὀν) τὸ μ. **in the midst**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.11, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 839}}; ἀνὰ μέσον **midway between**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 496a22, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 13, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.21, etc.; ἀνὰ μ. τοῦ ναοῦ καὶ τοῦ βωμοῦ “GDI” 2010 (Delph.), compare “PTeb.” 13.9 (2nd c.BC), +others}}; θρὶξ ἀνὰ μέσσον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.9; ἀνὰ μέσσα 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 167}}; also ἀνὰ μέσον φέρε, ={μετρίως}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.18.}}
**III.f** κατὰ μέσσον, = {ἐν μέσῳ}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.8, 16.285, etc.: with _genitive_, κὰδ δὲ μέσον τάφρου καὶ τείχεος ἷζον}} **between**, {{Refs|9.87.}}
**III.2** μέσον, τό, **difference**, τὸ μ. πρὸς τὰς μεγίστας καὶ ἐλαχίστας **the average** between.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; πολλὸν τὸ μ., πολὺ τὸ μ., **the difference** is great, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 914 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τὸ μ. οὐδὲν τῆς ἔχθρης ἐστί there is no **middle course** for our enmity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11.}}
**III.3** **middle state, mean**, τὸ μ. καὶ τὸ εὖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109b26}}; ποιήματα μέσα, opposed to ὀγκώδη, **in the** (correct) **mean**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.5}}. _adverb_ -ως, ἀναστρέφεσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1.155S.}}
**III.4** in Logic, τὸ μ. **the middle term** of a syllogism, opposed to τὰ ἄκρα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 66a30; also ὁ μ. (i.e. ὅρος) [prev. work] 25b33.}}
**III.5** in Mathematics texts, **middle** terms in a proportion, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 6.16}}; μέση, or μέση (μέσος) ἀνάλογον a **mean proportional** (straight line or number), {{Refs|[prev. work] 13, 17, 8.11, 12, +others}}; μέσης εὕρεσις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 413a19, “Metaphorical” 996b21}}; μέση **medial**, a specific kind of irrational (straight line), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 10.21, +others}}; μέσον ὀρθογώνιον (χωρίον) **medial** rectangle (area), {{Refs|[prev. work] 24, +others}}
**III.6** Astron., ὁ διὰ μέσων τῶν ζῳδίων κύκλος the **ecliptic**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.9.3, 4, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 2.21, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 2.7: without κύκλος, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1073b20, 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.9.12; simply, ὁ διὰ μέσων 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.146}}; but, ὁ μέσος [κύκλος] the **equator** of a rotating sphere, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1073b30.}}
**III.7** μέσα, τά, ={μέζεα}, Blaes. p.191 K.
**III.7.b** ={κοιλία} 1.3, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.27.3, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.732: _sing._, 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1 (variant{τὰ μέσα} “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(2).45).}}]
**III.8** Μέσον, τό, one of the law-courts at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 120.}}
**III.9** οὐ τοῖς μέσοις τῆς βίας χρωμένη no **ordinary** force,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Hierocl. p.15 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}
**IV** μέση, ἡ, as _substantive_, see at {μέση}.
**V** _adverb_ μέσον, _Epic dialect_ μέσσον, **in the middle**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.167, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.300}}: with _genitive_, **between**, οὐρανοῦ μ. χθονός <τε> {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 983 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.22.10}}; **in the midst of**, μ. τῆς θαλάσσης {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.14.27; μ. γενεᾶς σκολιᾶς NT.Phil.2.15}}: also in _plural_, μέσα αἰετὸς οὐρανοῦ ποτᾶται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 530 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.26.}}
**V.2** regular _adverb_ μέσως, πόλεώς τ᾽ οὐ μ. εὐδαίμονος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 873, compare “Hec.” 1113, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.23}}; καὶ μ. even **in a moderate degree**, even **a little**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60}}; μ. ἔχειν πρός or περί τι to be **in the mean**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1105b28, 1119a11; θερμότερον ἢ κραυρότερον ἢ μ. ἔχον 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 7.1, compare 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.53}}; μ. βεβιωκέναι **in a middle way**, i. e. neither well nor ill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d; μ. μεθύων 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 226}}; μ. διατιθέναι **in an intermediate way**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14.}}
**V.2.b** Grammars, **in the middle voice**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 276.21.}}
**VI** irregular _comparative_ μεσαίτερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 165b}}: _superlative_ μεσαίτατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.17, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392b33, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 9.3, etc.; poetry μεσσότατος 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.649, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 6.373}}. (Cf. Sanskrit_mádhyas_ 'middle', Latin _medius_, etc.)

3320:
** μεσό-τοιχος**, ὁ,
**party-wall,** {{Refs|Milet. Ergebnisse 7.56 (from Didyma), “BCH” 33.452 (from Argos): _metaphorically_, τὸν τῆς ἡδονῆς καὶ ἀρετῆς μ. διορύττειν}} Eratosth.Cyren. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.281d.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **having a party-wall**, οἰκίαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.98.9 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}

3321:
** μεσουρᾰν-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**culmination**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.5.8, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.1, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 8.4, Theo Sm.p.159 H., etc.}}
**2** **mid-heaven, zenith,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 8.13, +others}}
**3** μ. κόσμου title of Aries as having been on the meridian at the Creation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 5.26.}}
**4** name of the tenth τόπος, {{Refs|Paul. Al. “N.” 1.}}

3322:
** μεσόω**,
(μέσος) **to be in** or **at the middle**, τόδ᾽ ἴσθι, μηδέπω μεσοῦν κακόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 435; ἐν ἀρχῇ πῆμα κοὐδέπω μεσσῖ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 60; ἐπειδὴ τὸ δρᾶμα ἤδη μεσοίη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 924}}; especially of Time, μεσοῦσα ἡμέρη **mid**day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104}}; θέρους μεσοῦντος in **mids**summer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.57; ἐν [ἐνιαυτῷ] μεσοῦντι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.2.24}}; πρὸς ἥλιον μεσοῦντα to wards **mid**day, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.4.8; μεσούντων τῶν ζῳδίων 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 2.3.19.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **to be in the middle of**, τῆς ἀναβάσιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181; τὰ δὲ καὶ μεσοῦν τούτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618b}}; μεσοῦντα τῆς ἀρχῆς **in the middle of** his time of office, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.12}}: and with _participle_, μεσοῦν δειπνοῦντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175c.}}

3324:
** μεστ-ός**, ή, όν,
**full**, ἄγγεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.5; ἐποίησεν τὴν πόλιν μεστήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 814}}; ἔγχεον μεστήν **a full cup**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 20, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 58; μὴ μεστὰς ἀεὶ ἕλκωμεν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 207.1}}; of persons, οἶνον πίνεις μ. ὤν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 164, compare 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 15.}}
**II** with _genitive_, **full of**, ἀργυρίου.. ἀρτάβη μεστή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.192; τὸ στόμα.. μεστὸν βδελλέων [prev. author] 2.68; μ. ὕδατος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 383}}; ἀλφίτων, οἴνου, ἐλαίου, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 806s q}}.; ὄνος.. οἴνου μ. **laden with**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 617; ἱμάτιον κηλίδων μ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 19.7.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, πάντα μ. ἐλπίδων ἀγαθῶν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.18}}; μεστοὶ σπουδῆς, ἀταξίας, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 1.13, “Mem.” 3.5.6; πολλῆς ἀνοίας μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.45; σοφία μ. ἐλέους καὶ καρπῶν ἀγαθῶν NT.Jam.3.17; φόβων καὶ ἐρώτων μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579b; ἀπάτης μ. [prev. author] “Phd.” 83a, etc.; ἐλευθερίας [prev. author] “R.” 563d}}; μ. θεάτρου **full of** theatric pride, i. e. spoilt by applause, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 194b}}; ὑπερηφανίας καὶ ὑπεροψίας μ. variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.195.}}
**II.2.b** **sated with**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 804; μ. εἰρήνης σαπρᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 554}}; τινος (of a person) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.541d: with}}_participle_, μ. ἦ θυμούμενος, i. e. had had my **fill** of anger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 768; μ. ἐγένετο ἀγανακτῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.28; μεστοὶ τοῦ συνεχῶς λέγοντος [prev. author] 18.308; also μ. τὸν θυμόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 13.}}

3326:
** μετά** [ᾰ,
but ᾱ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 184 (assuming variant, Lyric poetry)}}], _poetry_ **μεταί**, uncertain, only in μεταιβολί; _Aeolic dialect_, _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus πεδά (which see)}}: _preposition_ with _genitive_, _dative_, and _accusative_ (Cf. Goth. _mip_, OHG. **miti, mit** 'with'.)
**A** WITH GEN. (in which use μ. gradually superseded σύν, which see),
**A.I** **in the midst of, among, between**, with _plural_ Nouns, μετ᾽ ἄλλων λέξο ἑταίρων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.320; μ. δμώων πῖνε καὶ ἦσθε 16.140; τῶν μέτα παλλόμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.400; πολλῶν μ. δούλων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1037; μ. ζώντων εἶναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1312; ὅτων οἰκεῖς μέτα [prev. author] “OT” 414; μ. τῶν θεῶν διάγουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81a (but κεῖσθαι μ. τινός}} **with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 73): sometimes the _plural_ is implied, μετ᾽ οὐδενὸς ἀνδρῶν ναίειν}}, i.e. **among** no men, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1103 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**A.II** **in common, along with, by aid of** (implying a closer union than σύν), μ. Βοιωτῶν ἐμάχοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.700, compare 21.458}}; συνδιεπολέμησαν τὸν πόλεμον μ. Ἀθηναίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.108.7; μ. ξυμμάχων ξυγκινδυνεύσειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.24, compare 6.79, etc.}}; μ. τῆς βουλῆς **in co-operation with** the council, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.91.10: in this sense frequently (not in lines, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, rare in early Gr.) with _sing._, μετ᾽ Ἀθηναίης}} **with**, i.e. **by aid of**, Athena, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 20.2; μ. εἷο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 392}}; μ. τινὸς πάσχειν, δρᾶν τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1067 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 70}}; μ. τινὸς εἶναι to be **on** one's **side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56; μ. τοῦ ἠδικημένου ἔσεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.7; μ. τοῦ νόμου καὶ τοῦ δικαίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32b}}: generally, **with, together with**, with _substantive_ in _singular_ first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (in whom it is rare except in the phrase οἱ μ. τινός, see below), as κοιμᾶσθαι μ. τινός 3.68, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 22.2; εὕδειν μ. τινός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.84}}; οἱ μ. τινός his **companions**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.86, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315b: frequently with Prons., μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 73; μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 661 (_anapaest meter_), etc.: less frequently of things, στέγη πυρὸς μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 298; μ. κιθάρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1037 (Lyric poetry); μ. τυροῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 771, etc.; τὴν δίαιταν μεθ᾽ ὅπλων ἐποιήσαντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 573; ὄχλος μ. μαχαιρῶν καὶ ξύλων NT.Matt.26.47: indicating community of action and serving to join two subjects, Κλεομένης μετὰ Ἀθηναίων}} C. **and** the Athenians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126: with _plural_ Verb, Δημοσθένης μ. τῶν ξυστρατήγων σπένδονται [prev. author] 3.109, etc.}}; of things, **in conjunction with**, ἰσχύν τε καὶ κάλλος μετὰ ὑγιείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 591b; γῆρας μ. πενίας [prev. work] 330a.}}
**A.III** later, **in one's dealings with**, ὅσα ἐποίησεν ὁ θεὸς μετ᾽ αὐτῶν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Act.14.27; ὁ ποιήσας τὸ ἔλεος μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ NT.Luke.10.36; τί ἡμῖν συνέβη μ. τῶν ἀρχόντων “PAmh.” 2.135.15 (2nd c.AD)}}: even of hostile action, σὺ ποιεῖς μετ᾽ ἐμοῦ πονηρίαν {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 15.3; πολεμῆσαι μ. τινός NT.Rev.12.7, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 190.4 codices (but μ. may be a gloss), Wilcken “Chr.” 23.10 (5th c.AD), “OGI” 201.3 (Nubia, 6th c.AD)}}: to denote the union of persons with qualities or circumstances, and so to denote manner, τὸ ἄπραγμον.. μὴ μ. τοῦ δραστηρίου τεταγμένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63, etc.; ἱκετεῦσαι μ. δακρύων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34c; οἴκτου μέτα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1636; μετ᾽ ἀσφαλείας μὲν δοξάζομεν, μετὰ δέους δὲ.. ἐλλείπομεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, compare “IG” 22.791.12; μ. ῥυθμοῦ βαίνοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.70; ὅσα μετ᾽ ἐλπίδων λυμαίνεται [prev. work] 103, etc.}}; ψυχὴν ὁσίως βεβιωκυῖαν καὶ μετ᾽ ἀληθείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 526c, compare “Phdr.” 249a, 253d}}; also, **by means of**, μετ᾽ ἀρετῆς πρωτεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.8; γράφε μ. μέλανος “PMag.Lond.” 121.226.}}
**A.III.2** serving to join two predicates, γενόμενος μ. τοῦ δυνατοῦ καὶ ξυνετός, i.e. δυνατός τε καὶ ξυνετός, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15; ὅταν πλησιάζῃ μ. τοῦ ἅπτεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255b.
**A.IV** rarely of Time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|μ. τοῦ γυμνάζεσθαι ἠλείψαντο, for ἅμα, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6}}; μετ᾽ ἀνοκωχῆς **during**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.25.}}
**B** WITH DAT., only _poetry_, mostly _Epic dialect_:
**B.I** **between, among** others, but without the close union which belongs to the genitive, and so nearly ={ἐν}, which is sometimes exchanged with it, μ. πρώτοισι.. ἐν πυμάτοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.64:}}
**B.I.1** of persons, **among, in company with**, μετ᾽ ἀθανάτοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525; μετ᾽ ἀνθρώποις 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.30; μ. κόραισι Νηρῆος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.29}}; μ. τριτάτοισιν ἄνασσεν **in** the third generation (not μ. τριτάτων **belonging to** it), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.252}}; of haranguing an assembly, μετ᾽ Ἀργείοις ἀγορεύεις {{Refs|10.250, etc.}}; **between**, of two parties, φιλότητα μετ᾽ ἀμφοτέροισι βάλωμεν {{Refs|4.16.}}
**B.I.2** of things, μ. νηυσίν, ἀστράσι, κύμασιν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.668, 22.28, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.91; δεινὸν δ᾽ ἐστὶ θανεῖν μ. κύμασιν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 687; χαῖται δ᾽ ἐρρώοντο μ. πνοιῇς ἀνέμοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.367; αἰετὼ.. ἐπέτοντο μ. π. ἀ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.148.}}
**B.I.3** of separate parts of persons, **between**, μ. χερσὶν ἔχειν to hold **between**, i.e. **in**, the hands, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.4, 184, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1110 (Lyric poetry), etc.; τὸν μ. χ. ἐρύσατο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.344}}; ὅς κεν.. πέσῃ μ. ποσσὶ γυναικός, of a child being born, 'to fall **between** her feet', {{Refs|19.110; so μ. γένυσσιν, γαμφηλῇσιν, 11.416, 13.200; μ. φρεσί 4.245, etc.}}
**B.II** to complete a number, **besides, over and above**, αὐτὰρ ἐγὼ πέμπτος μ. τοῖσιν ἐλέγμην I reckoned myself to be **with** them a fifth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.335, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.188}}; Οὖτιν.. πύματον ἔδομαι μ. οἷς ἑτάροισι last **to complete the number**, i.e. **after**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.369, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 613, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.39, 17.84.}}
**B.III** with _dative_ _singular_, only of _collective_ Nouns (or the equivalent of such, μεθ᾽ αἵματι καὶ κονίῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.118), μ. στροφάλιγγι κονίης 21.503; στρατῷ 22.49; μ. πρώτῃ ἀγορῇ 19.50, etc.; μετ᾽ ἀνδρῶν.. ἀριθμῷ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.449; μετ᾽ ἄλλῳ λαῷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 365 (Lyric poetry).}}
**C** WITH ACCUSATIVE
**C.I** of motion, **into the middle of, coming into** or **among**, especially where a number of **persons** is implied, ἵκοντο μ. Τρῶας καὶ Ἀχαιούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.264; μ. φῦλα θεῶν 15.54, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.366, +others; μ. μῶλον Ἄρηος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.245; μ. λαὸν Ἀχαιών 5.573, +others; μ. στρατόν, μεθ᾽ ὅμιλον, μεθ᾽ ὁμήγυριν, 5.589, 14.21, 20.142: so of birds, ὥς τ᾽ αἰγυπιὸς μ. χῆνας (though this may be referred to signification 2), 17.460}}; of **things**, εἴ τινα φεύγοντα σαώσειαν μ. νῆας {{Refs|12.123}}; με μ... ἔριδας καὶ νείκεα βάλλει plunges me **into** them, {{Refs|2.376}}; of place, μ. τ᾽ ἤθεα καὶ νομὸν ἵππων {{Refs|6.511}}; δράγματα μετ᾽ ὄγμον πῖπτον **into the midst of** the furrow, {{Refs|18.552.}}
**C.I.2** **in pursuit** or **quest of**, of persons, sometimes in friendlysense, βῆ ῥ᾽ ἰέναι μ. Νέστορα went **to seek** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Nestor Epigrammaticus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.73, compare 15.221: sometimes in hostile sense, βῆναι μ. τινά}} to go **after**, pursue him, {{Refs|5.152, 6.21, +others}}; also of things, πλεῖν μ. χαλκόν to sail **in quest of** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.184}}; ἵκηαι μ. πατρὸς ἀκουήν **in search of** news of thy father, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.308, compare 13.415; οἴχονται μ. δεῖπνον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.346}}; πόλεμον μέτα θωρήσσοντο they armed **for** the battle, {{Refs|20.329}}; ὡπλίζοντο μεθ᾽ ὕλην prepared **to seek after** wood, {{Refs|7.418, compare 420; μ. δούρατος ᾤχετ᾽ ἐρωήν 11.357; μ. γὰρ δόρυ ᾔει οἰσόμενος 13.247.}}
**C.II** of sequence or succession,
**C.II.1** of Place, **after, behind**, λαοὶ ἕπονθ᾽, ὡς εἴ τε μ. κτίλον ἕσπετο μῆλα like sheep **after** the bell-wether, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.492, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.260, 21.190, “hymnus ad Venerem” 69; ἔσχατοι μ. Κύνητας οἰκέουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.49}}; μ. τὴν θάλασσαν **beyond, on the far side of** the sea, {{Refs|Theo Sm. p.122 H.}}
**C.II.2** of Time, **after, next to**, μ. δαῖτας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.352}}; μεθ᾽ Ἕκτορα πότμος ἑτοῖμος **after** Hector thy death is at the door, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.96; μ. Πάτροκλόν γε θανόντα 24.575, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34; μετ᾽ εὐχάν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 231 (Lyric poetry), etc.; μ. ταῦτα}}**thereupon, there-after,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 126, etc.; τὸ μ. ταῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 34c; τὸ μ. τοῦτο [prev. author] “Criti.” 120a}}; μετ᾽ ὀλίγον ὕστερον shortly **after**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 646c; μ. μικρόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 8; μ. ἡμέρας τρεῖς μ. τὴν ἄφεδρον 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.19; μ. ἔτη δύο 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.13.1; μ. τρίτον ἔτος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.2.8}}; μ. χρυσόθρονον ἠῶ **after** daybreak, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 326: but μετ᾽ ἡμέρην}} **by** day, opposed to νυκτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.150, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251e, etc.}}; μεθ᾽ ἡμέραν, opposed to νύκτωρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 485; μ. νύκτας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.6}}; μ. τὸν ἑξέτη καὶ τὴν ἑξέτιν **after** the boy or girl has attained the age of six years, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 794c.}}
**C.II.3** in order of Worth, Rank, etc., **next after**, following _superlative_, κάλλιστος ἀνὴρ.. τῶν ἄλλων Δαναῶν μετ᾽ ἀμύμονα Πηλεΐωνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.674, compare 7.228, 12.104, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.350, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.53, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.11, etc.; κοῦροι οἳ.. ἀριστεύουσι μεθ᾽ ἡμέας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.652, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.18}}: where _superlative_ is implied, ὃς πᾶσι μετέπρεπε.. μ. Πηλεΐωνος ἑταῖρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.195, compare 17.280, 351}}; μ. μάκαρας **next to** the gods, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1080 (_anapaest meter_)}}; also μάχεσθαι μ. πολλοὺς τῶν Ἑλλήνων to be **inferior** in fighting **to** many.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 6.}}
**C.III** **after, according to**, μ. σὸν καὶ ἐμὸν κῆρ **as** you and I **wish**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.52; μετ᾽ ἀνέρος ἴχνι᾽ ἐρευνῶν 18.321; μετ᾽ ἴχνια βαῖνε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.406.}}
**C.IV** generally, **among, between**, as with _dative_ (B.I), μ. πάντας ὁμήλικας ἄριστος best **among** all, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.54, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.419; μ. πληθύν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.143}}; μ. τοὺς τετελευτηκότας **including** those who have died, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.260.87 (1st c.AD)}}; μ. χεῖρας ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16. β᾽, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138, “POxy.” 901.9 (4th c.AD),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.14, etc.}}
**D** μετά with all cases can be put after its _substantive_, and is then by anastrophe μέτα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.301}}, but not when the _ultima _ is elided, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.258, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.147.}}
**E** _absolutely_ as _adverb_., **among them, with them**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.446, 477, etc.}}; **with him**, οὐκ οἶον, μ. καὶ Γανυμήδεα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.115.}}
**E.II** **and then, next afterwards**, opposed to πρόσθε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.133.}}
**E.III** **thereafter**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|15.67, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, 128, 150, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 759 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μ. γάρ τε καὶ ἄλγεσι τέρπεται ἀνήρ one feels pleasure even in troubles, **when past**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.400}}; μ. δέ, for ἔπειτα δέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 9.2, etc.}}
**F** μέτα, -μέτεστι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.93, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 9.4, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, 171, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 48,etc.}}
**G** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**G.I** **of community** or **participation**, as in μεταδίδωμι, μετέχω, usually with _genitive of things_.
**G.I.2** of action **in common with** another, as in μεταδαίνυμαι, μεταμέλπομαι, etc., with _dative_ _person_
**G.II** **in the midst of**, of space or time, as in μεταδήμιος, μεταδόρπιος 1; **between**, as in μεταίχμιον, μεταπύργιον.
**G.III** of **succession** of time, as in μεταδόρπιος 2, μετακλαίω, μεταυτίκα.
**G.IV** of **pursuit**, as in μεταδιώκω, μετέρχομαι.
**G.V** of **letting go**, as in μεθίημι, μεθήμων.
**G.VI** **after, behind**, as in μετάφρενον, opposed to πρόσθε.
**G.VII** **reversely**, as in μετατρέπω, μεταστρέφω.
**G.VIII** most frequently of **change** of place, condition, plan, etc., as in μεταβαίνω, μεταβάλλω, μεταβουλεύω, μεταγιγνώσκω, etc.

3327:
** μεταβάλλω**,
_future_ - βᾰλῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1568:}}_aorist_ μετέβᾰλον:—**throw into a different position, turn quickly** or **suddenly**, Hom.only once, in tmesi, μετὰ νῶτα βαλών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.94; χαλεπῶς μ. δέμας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 204 (_anapaest meter_), compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.556; μ. θοἰμάτιον ἐπιδεξιά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [same place]}}; μ. γῆν **turn**, i.e. **plough**, the earth, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.14; μετέβαλε Κύριος ἄνεμον ἐκ θαλάσσης LXX.Exo.10.19}}; μ. ποταμόν **change the course of** a river, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.126d.}}
**II** **turn about, change, alter**, τὸ οὔνομα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.57; τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b21}}; [οἱ Βρίγες] τὸ οὔνομα μετέβαλον [ἐς Φρύγας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.73; τὰς φυλὰς μετέβαλε [ὁ Κλεισθένης] ἐς ἄλλα οὐνόματα [prev. author] 5.68; μ. μορφήν τινος εἰς ἀνδρὸς φύσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 54; [τινὰ] ἐπὶ κακόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 723; ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 381b}}; μ. δίαιταν **change** one's way of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.16}}; μ. ὕδατα **drink different** water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.117; ὀργὰς μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 121 (_anapaest meter_); μ. τοὺς τρόπους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 36, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 357.7; μ. τὸ ἔθος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123}}; μ. εὔνοιαν **lose** it,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 77; μ. χώραν ἐκ χώρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 181c: frequently with _adjectives_, etc., implying change, μ. ἄλλους τρόπους}} **change and adopt** other ways, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 343 (troch.); μ. ἄλλας γραφάς [prev. work] 363 (troch.)}}; εἶδος καινὸν μουσικῆς μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 424c; πόλις ἄλλον ἐξ ἄλλου -βάλλουσα τύραννον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 1}}; μ. ἀντὶ τοῦ ὁμο- ἀ-” {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 405d; ἐμαυτὸν ἄνω κάτω μετέβαλλον [prev. author] “Phd.” 96b; ἄνω καὶ κάτω τὰς δόξας μ. [prev. author] “R.” 508d: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πολλὰς μεταβολὰς.. μ. ὑδάτων καὶ σίτων [prev. work] 404a.}}
**II.b** **translate**, νόμον εἰς τὴν Ἑλλάδα φωνήν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJProoem.” 3, compare 12.2.13 (_passive_).}}
**II.c** **stir** with a spoon, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.22 (_passive_).}}
**III** _intransitive_, **undergo a change**, μ. ἐς εὐνομίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.9; μ. εἰς ὀλιγαρχικὸν ἐκ τοῦ τιμοκρατικοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553a, etc.; μ. ἐπὶ τοὐναντίον [prev. author] “Plt.” 270d; ὅταν εἰς ἑτέραν -βάλῃ πολιτείαν ἡ πόλις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1276b14, compare 1301a20: _impersonal_, μεταβάλλει διὰ πλειόνων ζῴων}} **changes run** through a series of creatures, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.4.4}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **come in exchange for** or **instead of**, καιναὶ καινῶν μεταβάλλουσαι.. συντυχίαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1118.}}
**III.b** **vary**, μεταβάλλειν τὰς ἐπιστήμας τοῖς τόποις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.115 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III.2** **change one's course**, μεταβαλὼν πρὸς Ἀθηναίους **turning** to the Athenians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109:}}_aorist_ _participle_ μεταβαλών _absolutely_, **instead, in turn**, μεταβαλόντας ἀντὶ Κρητῶν γενέσθαι Ἰήπυγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.170, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1614, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 204e, “Grg.” 480e}}: also _present_ _participle_ μεταβάλλων {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 166d.}}
**B** _middle_, **turn round, shift** a load, μεταβαλλόμενος τἀνάφορον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 8; προβαλλομένους τὰ ὅπλα ἢ μεταβαλλομένους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.16.}}
**B.2** **cause to be removed**, σῖτον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.45.6 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**B.2.b** **order to be paid, remit,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 1153.8 (1st c.AD), 1419.5 (3rd c.AD).}}
**B.II** **change what is one's own**, μ. τὰ ἱμάτια **change one's** clothes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.6; μ. τοὺς τρόπους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1461 (Lyric poetry)}}; μετεβάλλετ᾽ ὀπωπάν **changed her** appearance, {{Refs|Erinna Lyrica in “PSI” 9.1090.53 + 13 (p.xii).}}
**B.II.2** **exchange**, τίς μεταβάλοιτ᾽ ἂν ὧδε σιγὰν λόγω; silence **for** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1261}}; [τὴν ἄσαρκον τροφὴν] ὑγείας καὶ ῥώμης μεταβαλέσθαι **have given up** asceticism **in exchange for** health and strength, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.2}}; **barter, traffic in**, οἴνου μεταβαλλόμενος καὶ σίτου πρᾶσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 849d; μ. τὰ ἀλλότρια ἔργα [prev. author] “Sph.” 223d; μ. ἐν τῇ ἀγορᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.6, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.13.}}
**B.III** **turn oneself, turn about**, ἄνω καὶ κάτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.17}}; especially
**B.III.2** **change one's purpose** or **mind**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.75, “SIG” 22.20 (5th c.BC), NT.Act.28.6, etc.}}; **change sides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, 8.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.31; πρός τινα 4th c.BC: Axionicus Comicus 6.10.}}
**B.III.3** **turn** or **wheel round**, μ. ἐπ᾽ ἀσπίδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.6; τὸ δόρυ εἰς τοὔπισθεν μ. [prev. author] “Eq.” 8.10}}: _absolutely_, **turn about**, μεταβαλλόμενος τοῖς ἔξω περιεστηκόσι λοιδορήσεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.207.}}

