3329:
** μετάγω** [ᾰ],
_future_ -άξω {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.3}}: _perfect_ μεταγείοχα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PRyl.” 67.5 (2nd c.BC)}}: —**convey from one place to another, transfer**, τινὰ εἰς Βαβυλῶνα {{LXX|LXX.1Esd.1.45, cf. Aristeas 12 (_passive_)}}; τὴν ἐκκλησίαν εἰς Σικυῶνα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.1.9; τὸν πόλεμον εἰς τὴν Λιβύην 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus [same place]; ναόν “SIG” 587.6 (Peparethus, 2nd c.BC)}}; τὰ δικαστήρια ἀπὸ τῆς βουλῆς ἐπὶ τοὺς ἱππέας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 83.7}}; **escort**, τινα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 588.51 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}: in Medicine texts, **divert**, τὰ ῥεύματα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).965: _metaphorically_, τοὺς πολίτας εἰς σωφρονεστέραν βίου τάξιν μ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.225f, compare “SIG” 704 E 12 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 33.3; ψυχὴν ἐπ᾽ εὐφροσύνην “Anthologia Graeca” 10.77 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}; **seduce**, τινὰς ἐς τὸ ἁβροδίαιτον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.8.5.}}
**2** **translate**, εἰς ἑτέραν γλῶσσαν {{Refs|LXX “Si.Prol.” (_passive_).}}
**3** **derive** a metaphor, ἀπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.179 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**4** _passive_, **to be borrowed**, μετῆκται ἀπὸ τῶν ἐν γεωμετρίᾳ τὸ ὄνομα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.58 P.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **go by a different route, change one's course**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.8.}}

3330:
** μεταδίδωμι** [δῐ],
_future_ **μεταδια-δώσω**, **give part of, give a share**, with _genitive_ _of things_, τοῦ μεταδοῦν (_poetry_ _aorist 2_ _infinitive_) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 104; μ. τινί τινος [prev. author] 925 (probable reading), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.143, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 961}}; γῆς (i.e. αὐτοῖσι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.145}}; τῆς ἀρχῆς (i.e. αὐτοῖσι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.150; τῷ πλήθει τῆς πολιτείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306a25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.10, etc.; τοῖς μηδὲν ἀδικοῦσιν ἐξ ἴσου τῆς πολιτείας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.3; τὸ ἄλλῳ τῆς γεννητικῆς.. δυνάμεως μεταδεδωκός 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 56.}}
**2** with _accusative_ of the part given, μ. τὸ τριτημόριόν τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.34, compare 8.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 917; ἀρχῆς μηδ᾽ ὁτιοῦν μ. τοῖς ἡττηθεῖσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 715a; μ. τὸ μέρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.11}}; μ. πυρούς **distribute**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.5.5.}}
**3** _intransitive_, μ. τινὶ ὑπέρ, περί τινος, **communicate** with one about.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.27.4, 38.8.1}}; ὅτι.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 1153.6 (1st c.AD)}}:—_passive_, **to be communicated, transmitted**, of notices, memoranda, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1472.6 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; of diseases, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 19 (“Hermes” 53.64, 65).}}

3331:
** μετάθεσις**, εως, ἡ,
(μετατίθημι) **change of position, transposition**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1024a4; μεταθέσεις ἐξ ἕδρας ἀτόμων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 61; ἡ τῶν ῥημάτων μ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.84, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.23; τοῦ ἀναβαθμοῦ “PSI” 5.546 (3rd c.BC)}}; τοῦ ἐμβρύου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.60}}; **couching** of a cataract, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.990.}}
**2** generally, **change**, θεὸς οὐδεμίαν ἐπιδεχόμενος μ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400b29; μ. ἐκ τῆς οἰκείας φύσεως 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.216 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; νόμου μ. NT.Heb.7.12}}; especially **change of sides** or **opinions**, ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.35.7, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.2, etc.}} (hence, **amendment**, τῶν ἡμαρτημένων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.11. 5); ἐκ μεταθέσεως [prev. author] 30.20.2}}; **going over**, πρός τινα {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.86.8.}}
**3** **exchange, barter**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.1.8 (_plural_).}}
**4** Grammars, **change** of a letter, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.5, +others, “Etymologicum Magnum” 795.34}}; also, **metathesis, transposition** of letters, as κραδίη for καρδία, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus “Excerpta” 4, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus “Lex.” see at {ἀγλαά}.}}]
**5** **plagiarism**, opposed to μίμησις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 112.}}
**II** **power** or **right of changing sides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.29.}}

3332:
** μεταίρω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ (also in Trag.) **πεδ-**, **lift up and remove, shift**, ἄγαλμα ἐκ βάθρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1157}}; πεδαίρειν κῶλον, πόδα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 819 (Lyric poetry), 872 (troch.); ἐκ τόπων νέους πεδαίρουσα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1027 (Lyric poetry)}}; [ἀναθέματα] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 573.15 (Cilicia, 1st c.AD)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 76, 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 37.4.}}
**2** **repeal**, ψήφισμα μ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.174.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **depart**, ἐκεῖθεν {{NT|NT.Matt.13.53, compare 19.1.}}

3333:
** μετακᾰλέω**,
**recall**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.11: especially _metaphorically_, τὴν ψυχὴν ἀπὸ τῆς ὀργῆς ἐπὶ.. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.159, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.2.4, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 3.71}}:—_middle_, τινὰς ἀπὸ τῆς ἀποστάσεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.10.}}
**2** simply, **summon**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.15; ἐς κοινωνίαν μετακληθῆναι [prev. author] “Her.” 2.14; ψυχαῖς ταῖς εἰς χωρία θεῶν καὶ τοὺς ἐγγὺς ἄστρων τόπους καὶ ἱεροὺς δαίμονας μετακεκλημέναις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.44}}; **call in** a midwife, doctor, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.4}}; ὁ ἰατρὸς μετακληθείς **being called in**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 44}}:—_middle_, **summon**, πανταχόθεν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 4.4, cf. NT.Act.7.14, “POxy.” 33vii 2 (2nd c.AD)}}; **invoke**, τὴν σελήνην variant in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.10.}}
**II** **call** or **name differently,** {{Refs|Arch.Papyrus 3.419 (_passive_).}}

3334:
** μετακῑν-έω**,
**shift, remove**, τινὰ ἐκ τῆς τάξιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.74}}; τι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.186 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}:—_middle_, **go from one place to another**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.51; μεταβάλλον καὶ -ούμενον γίγνεται πᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 894a}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 315b14.}}
**2** **change, alter**, μ. τὴν πάτριον πολιτείαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.205, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 15.1 (_passive_)}}; ῥᾷον ἔθος μετακινῆσαι φύσεως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1152a30}}; ἡ τομὴ μετεκινήθη the time of cutting **was altered**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.5.}}

3335:
** μεταλαμβάνω**,
_future_ - λήψομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18}}:—**have** or **get a share of, partake of**, with _genitive_ _of things_, ληΐης, καμάτου, μιαρίας, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.79, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.12; ἀμείνονος μοίρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248e; τροφῆς NT.Act.2.46, etc.:—}}_middle_, μεταλαμβάνεσθαί τινος **lay claim to**, τοῦ οὐνόματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.45.}}
**2** with the part received added in _accusative_, Ἄρεως μοῖραν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 302; τὸ πέμπτον μέρος τῶν ψήφων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.266, etc.; μ. τῶν τῆς ἀρετῆς μορίων οἱ μὲν ἄλλο οἱ δὲ ἄλλο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329e.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, ἢν μὴ μεταλάβῃ τοὐπίπεμπτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 201; δικαστῶν τοσούτων οὐ δὲ διακοσίας ψήφους μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.17.}}
**4** in Platonic Philos., with _genitive_ _of things_, **participate in** the universal, ἤτοι ὅλου τοῦ εἴδους ἢ μέρους μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 131a.}}
**5** with _genitive_ _person_, **have part in, share** his **society**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.51}}; **go shares with** another, ὡς ἐμοῦ τι κεκλοφότος ζητεῖς μεταλαβεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 370.}}
**6** **receive notice** or **information**, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 31 ii 2 (2nd c.BC)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 40.7 (2nd c.BC), LXX.2Mac.4.21: with _accusative_ et}}_participle_, μ. πολιορκοῦντά τινα{{Refs|[prev. work] 11.6; μ. διότι.. Aristeas 316}}:—_passive_, **to be cited**, = Latin _recitari_, ἐκ διπτύχων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 827 B 1 (Delph., 2nd c.AD).}}
**7** **understand**, φωνάς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.19.}}
**II** **receive in succession** or **afterwards**, [χαλινόν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.6; [ἱμάτιον] θάτερον 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 159.6; πλοῦτον ἕτερον 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 201}}; **occupy** a position **left by the enemy**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.40.11, etc.}}; μ. τὴν ἀρχήν **succeed to** the government, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.40.6, compare “PTeb.” 79.49 (2nd c.BC)}}; μ. τὸν λόγον **take up** the discourse, i. e. **answer**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.2.2}}; μ. alone, {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.38.1, etc.}}; οἱ παρά τινος -λημψόμενοι his **successors in title,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PTeb. 294.18 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; ἐκ διαδοχῆς μ. τὸ ἱερόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Stud.Pal.” 22.184.95 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **come after, come on**, ἅμα τῷ μεταλαβεῖν τὸ τῆς νυκτός {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.30.2.}}
**III** **take instead, take in exchange, substitute**, πόλεμον ἀντ᾽ εἰρήνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120; ἄλλο ὄνομα ἀντὶ τῆς ἡδονῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 355c}}; διαναπαύσωμεν αὐτὸν μεταλαβόντες αὐτοῦ τὸν συγγυμναστή; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 257c; τὰ ὄργανα τἀλλήλων [prev. author] “R.” 434a}}, compare b; μ. τὰ ἐπιτηδεύματα ἐς τὸ ὁμοῖον **adopt new** customs so as to resemble others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356d}}; ἱμάτια, ἐσθῆτας μ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.4, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.78.3}}; μ. παλτόν **take another** javelin, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.13}}: with _infinitive_, ἀντὶ τοῦ αἰεὶ φυλάσσεσθαι.. [τὸ] ἀντεπιβουλεῦσαι μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87.}}
**IV** _passive_, **to be changed**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.9, 6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 36.19.}}
**IV.2** in Medicine texts, of humours, blood, **to be transferred, conveyed**, ὑπὸ δηχθέντος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “fragment” 118, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.7.}}
**IV.3** Grammars, **to be changed, altered**, εἰς.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 107.2}}; also, of words, **have their construction altered**, εἰς.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 15.11, +others}}; but μ. ἐκ.. **to be used in place of**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 195.14, +others}}
**V** **take words in another sense**, τὰ πράγματα τοῖς ὀνόμασι μ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.9, compare 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista}} in “de An.” 18.35; **parody**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.336f (_passive_).}}
**V.2** **translate, interpret**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.480 (_passive_).}}
**VI** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τὸ μεταλαμβανόμενον}} **proposition substituted** for the original thesis in hypothetical reasoning, {{Refs|“APr.” 41a39.}}

3336:
** μετά-ληψις**, εως, ἡ,
**participation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 131a}}; λόγων in philosophy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 539d; γένεσις μ. οὐσίας [prev. author] “Def.” 411a}}; γίνεσθαι κατὰ τὴν μ. [τοῦ εἴδους] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 335b14, compare “Metaphorical” 1072b20, etc.}}; **partaking** of food, {{NT|NT.1Tim.4.3.}}
**2** **concurrence,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1273.39 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** Grammars, τὸ λέγων μ. ἐνεστῶτος καὶ παρατατικοῦ **is shared by**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 124.1.}}
**II** **alternation**, τῶν λόγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173b; αἱ μ. τοῦ σχήματος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.20.2; ἐκ μεταλήψεως [prev. author] 2.33.4.}}
**II.2** **succession**, μ. τῆς ἀρχῆς {{Refs|[prev. author] 31.13.3.}}
**II.3** **taking** one thing **instead of** another, ἡ ἀντὶ τοῦ μείζονος ἐλάττονος μ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1369b25.}}
**II.4** Rhet., **use of one word for another**, as of Ἥφαιστος for πῦρ, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus 8.6.37, 1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus “περὶ τρόπων” 5, etc.}}; **transference** of meaning, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 79.12.}}
**II.5** **objection, counterplea**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4d}}; especially **concurrence coupled with objection**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th c.AD: Syrianus Philosophus et Rhetor “in Hermogenem commentaria” 2.153 R., 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “Rh.” p.391 H.}}
**II.6** συλλογισμοὶ κατὰ μετάληψιν hypothetical syllogisms involving the **substitution** of a proposition for the original thesis, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 45b17}}; compare μεταλαμβάνω VI.
**II.7** Grammars, **change of construction**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 210.3}}; **change in dialect**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 335.1}}; **change of name**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1014.60.}}
**II.8** **translation, rendering**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis ad 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 180; ἡ εἰς τὸ Ἑλληνικὸν μ. [prev. author][prev. work] 294.}}
**II.9** **transference**, αἱ ἐς νεφροὺς καὶ κύστιας -λήψιες {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.9.}}
**II.10** κατὰ μετάληψιν κατατεῖναι, of **reflex** tension over a pulley {{Refs 1st c.AD+|compare (μεταληπτικός 11), 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.9.25.}}

3337:
** μεταλλάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **μεταλλάττω**, _perfect_ μετήλλᾰχα and irregular μετήλλᾰγα (see. below):—_passive_, irregular _aorist 2_ _infinitive_ μεταλλάγειν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.674 A 24 (Rhodes, 2nd c.BC)}}:—**change, alter**, θέσμια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; τὰν ἀνθρώπου ζόαν ἆται.. μεταλλάσσουσι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 592.6}}; πότμος.. μ. φύσιν{{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 871.2; μεταβολὴν βίου μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 775c; οὐ γὰρ τὸν τρόπον, ἀλλὰ τὸν τόπον μετήλλαξεν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.78; μετήλλαξαν τὴν ἀλήθειαν ἐν τῷ ψεύδει NT.Rom.1.25; πόνου μεταλλαχθέντος οἱ πόνοι γλυκεῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 374}}; τὰς τύχας ἑκατέρων μετήλλαξαν **interchanged** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.59.}}
**II** **exchange**,
**II.1** **take in exchange, adopt, assume**, ὀρνίθων μεταλλάξας φύσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 117}}; μ. τόπον **go into a new** country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 760c; μ. χώραν ἑτέραν ἐξ ἑτέρας [prev. author] “Prm.” 138c; ἑτέραν μ. τινὰ χώραν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 86}}; μ. διάφορα βρώματα to have varieties of food, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 246}}:— _middle_, μεταλλάσσεσθαι χιτῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 21; τὴν τύχην 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.92.}}
**II.2** **exchange by leaving, quit**, μ. τὸν βίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.17, 9.15, “OGI” 56.55 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 19.14, +others (2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ ζῆν μ. νόσῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.96 M.: μ. alone, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 367c, 1st-2nd c.AD: Sotion Paradoxographus p.185 W., “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).28 (Cyrene, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD)}}; οἱ μετηλλαχότες the **dead**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 369b, compare “Supp.epigram” 3.367.39 (_Boeotian dialect_, 2nd c.BC), “BGU” 1148.8 (1st c.BC), etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ μεταλλαχώς {{Refs|“Test.Epict.” 1.10}}; μεταλλαγότων (sic) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1433.37; also οἱ μεταλλάξαντες [prev. work]22.1323.10; ἐξ ἀνθρώπων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.56 (edict of Polyperchon, 4th c.BC).}}
**III** _intransitive_, **undergo a change, change**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.77, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 262, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 578b10: with _neuter_ _pronoun_, τοσοῦτο μετήλλαξε κατὰ τὸν βίον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.49}} M.: with _genitive_, **change from**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.70.}}
**IV** **substitute, transfer**, τινὰς εἰς τήν τινων χώραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 19a.}}

3338:
** μεταμέλ-ομαι**,
rare except in _present_ and _imperfect_: _future_ - μελήσομαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.16 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, also - μεληθήσομαι LXX.Psa.109(110).4, Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 899}}: _aorist_ -εμελήθην {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.23.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.75, later -ήσθην “PThead.” 51.15 (4th c.AD)}}; also _Epic dialect_ μετεμέμβλετο probably in [{{Refs|Panyasis Epicus in “Etymologicum Genuinum” see at {μῦθος} (“Coll.Alex.” p.249 Powell)}}]: _perfect_ - μεμέλημαι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.1Mac.11.10, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.34 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}:—**feel repentance, regret**:—Constr.:
**1** with _participle_, μετεμέλοντο οὐ δεξάμενοι **they repented that** they had not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.27, compare 7.50}}; μ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.14; ὁ μεταμελόμενος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1110b23.}}
**2** μεταμέλεσθαί τινι **repent at** a thing, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.9; ἐπί τινι [prev. author] 19.75}}; τινων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 43.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **change one's purpose** or **line of conduct**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.5, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.50.6.}}
**II** causal in _future_ _participle_, τὸ μεταμελησόμενον **that which will cause regret, matter for future repentance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.23.}}
**III** ={μεταμέλει}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.1, μεταμέλεσθαι αὐτοῖς περὶ ὧν ἂν συμβουλεύσωνται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Demodocus” 382d}}.:—the form **μεταμελέομαι**, censured by Thom.Mag.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|p.123 R., occurs in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 27.}}

3340:
** μετανο-έω**,
**perceive afterwards** or **too late**, opposed to προνοέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [280]}}; opposed to προβουλεύομαι, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 66}}; **concur subsequently**, τισι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 747 i 11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **change one's mind** or **purpose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 279c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 72}}; μ. μὴ οὔτε.. τῶν χαλεπῶν ἔργων ᾖ τὸ.. ἄρχειν **change one's opinion and think** that it is not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.3.}}
**3** **repent**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.12; ἐν τοῖς ἀνηκέστοις [prev. author] 5.91: frequently in LXX and “NT, Si.” 48.15, +others; ἀπὸ τῆς κακίας NT.Act.8.22; ἐκ τῶν ἔργων NT.Rev.9.20; ἐπὶ τῇ ἀκαθαρσίᾳ NT.2Cor.12.21, compare “OGI” 751.9 (Amblada, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἐπί τινι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 84, etc.; περί τινων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 6; τοῖς πεπραγμένοις [prev. author] “Agis” 19}}: with _participle_, μ. γενόμενος Ἕλλην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 36.}}
**4** with _accusative_, **repent of**, τὴν ἄφιξιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.4.5.}}

3341:
** μετάνοια**, ἡ,
**change of mind** or **heart, repentance, regret**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 70, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 198, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.66.7, LXX.Pro.14.15, Aristeas 188, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.712c (_plural_), etc.; ἀνίατος γὰρ τῶν τοιούτων μ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.12; γαμεῖν ὁ μέλλων εἰς μ. ἔρχεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 91; ἡ εἰς τὸν θεὸν μ. NT.Act.20.21; μ. ἀπὸ νεκρῶν ἔργων NT.Heb.6.1.}}
**II** Rhet., **afterthought, correction**, {{Refs|Rutilius Lupus Rhetor 1.16.}}

3342:
** μεταξύ**
(late form **μετοξύ** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.177.11 (1st c.AD), etc.)}}, _adverb_, (μετά, ξύν) properly **in the midst**: hence,
**I** as _adverb_,
**I.1** of Place, **betwixt, between**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.156, compare “hymnus ad Mercurium” 159, etc.: with _Article_, τὸ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 551; ἐν τούτῳ μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25; νεάτης τε καὶ ὑπάτης καὶ μέσης καὶ εἰ ἄλλα ἄττα μ. τυγχάνει ὄντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 443d; αὐχένα μ. τιθέντες [prev. author] “Ti.” 69e: _metaphorically_, φίλος ἢ ἐχθρὸς ἢ μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1376a30.}}
**I.2** of Time, **between-whiles, meanwhile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.155, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 225, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 207d, etc.}}; τὰ μ. the **intervening** events, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.201: frequently with}}_present_ _participle_, μ. ὀρύσσων ἐπαύσατο **in the midst of** his digging, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.158; ἐπελαυνόντων.. μ. [prev. author] 4.129; μ. θύων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1242; μ. πίνων 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 351.5; μ. πορευομένους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 207b, etc.}}; ἐξαναστάντες μ. δειπνοῦντες **in the middle of** supper, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169}}; ἀπαγχομένη μ. κατεκλίθη (κατεκωλύθη Blass), i. e. **in the interval** between this and reviving, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.125: frequently with Verbs of speaking, λέγοντα μ.}} **in the middle of** my discourse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 40b, compare “Euthd.” 275e, “R.” 336b: without}}_participle_, μ. ὑπολαβεῖν **to interrupt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.26; μ. τὸν λόγον καταλύομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 505c; μ. διαλῦσαι τὴν συνουσίαν [prev. author] “Prt.” 336e}}; ἐν τῷ μ. (i.e. χρόνῳ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.14: with χρόνῳ, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.17.}}
**I.2.b** in late writers, like{μετά} (_adverb_), **after, afterwards**, τὸ μ. σάββατον the **next** Sabbath, {{NT|NT.Act.13.42}}; οἱ μ. τούτων βασιλεῖς the kings **who followed** them, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.4.2}}; οἱ μ. τούτων, = Latin _posterieorum,_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.1913.}}
**I.3** of Qualities, τὰ μ. **intermediate**, i.e. neither good nor bad, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 468a.}}
**I.4** of Degree, ὅσον τὸ μ. how great is **the difference**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 22.1.}}
**I.5** Grammars, the **neuter gender**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 166b12, “Po.” 1458a17.}}
**II** as _preposition_ with _genitive_, **between**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6, 7.85, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118, 4.42, etc.; μ. σοφίας καὶ ἀμαθίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202a; μ. τούτοιν ἀμφοῖν ἐν μέσῳ ὄν [prev. author] “R.” 583c}}; αἱ μ. τῶν λόγων διηγήσεις the explanations **between** the speeches, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 143c}}; but μ. τῶν λόγων **if I may interrupt** the argument, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 230a}}; μ. τῶν βασιλέων **among** kings, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.177c}}; **between** parties to an agreement, τιμὴ ἡ συμφωνηθεῖσα μ. τινῶν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 316.15 (4th c.AD)}}; τὰ μ. σύμφωνα the terms agreed **between the parties,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|POxy. 914.8 (5th c.AD)}}: sometimes one of the extremes is omitted, ἄνωθεν τῶν Θυεστείων ῥακῶν μ. τῶν Ἰνοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 434; ἢ ἐναντίοις οὖσιν ἢ μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 319b12; ἦν συμφέρον ὡς πλεῖστον τὸν μ. χρόνον γενέσθαι τῶν ὅρκων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.26.}}
**II.b** μ. θύρας **in the opening of** the door, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.119.}}
**II.2** of Time, ὁ μ. τῆς δίκης τε καὶ τοῦ θανάτου [χρόνος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58c, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 437}}; τὰ μ. τούτου **meanwhile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 291}}: as a _preposition_, it may either precede or follow its case, but more frequently precedes,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 71a and b.}}

3343:
** μετα-πέμπω**,
**send after** or **for**, Ἀγαμέμνονος πέμψαντος.. μέτα **having sent for** thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 504; παρ᾽ Εὐχαρίδου τρεῖς ἄγλιθας μετέπεμψα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 680}}.
**II** mostly in _middle_, μεταπέμπεσθαί τινα **summon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1087, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.15, etc.}}; of things, **send for**, σῖτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“OGI” 56.17 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), etc.: 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus}} uses _active_ and _middle_ indifferently, compare {{Refs|1.112, 4.30, 6.52, with 2.29, 5.82}}:—_passive_, μεταπεμφθῆναι **to be sent for**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.14, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319b; ἐξ Ἀθηνῶν μεταπεπέμφθαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.28 K.}}

3344:
** μετα-στρέφω**,
_aorist_ _passive_ -εστρέφθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.258, +others, -εστράφην [ᾰ] 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.121, etc.}}:— **turn about, turn round**, τῶ κε Ποσειδάων.. αἶψα μεταστρέψειε νόον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.52; εἴ κεν Ἀχιλλεὺς ἐκ χόλου.. μεταστρέψῃ φίλον ἦτορ 10.107; τὸ πρόσωπον πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190e}}: —_middle_, μεταστρέφεσθαι πρὸς τὸ μαλθακώτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 538 (Lyric poetry)}}:— _passive_, **turn oneself about, turn about**, whether to face the enemy, στῆ δὲ μεταστρεφθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.595, 15.591, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.211}}; or to flee, τῷ δὲ μεταστρεφθέντι μεταφρένῳ ἐν δόρυ πῆξεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.258}}; simply, **turn round**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.121, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116d, etc.}}; **turn about** (to see if any one follows), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 125, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.221}}; **recur**, ἐπὶ τὰ προειρημένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 428d.}}
**2** **turn round, retort**, αἰτίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.13.}}
**3** **twist** or **turn all ways**, πάντα μεταστρέφοντα λόγον βασανίζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 191c; λόγους ἄνω καὶ κάτω μ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 272b}}; **turn upside down**, ἅπαντα μ. τύχη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 111}}:—_passive_, τἄνω κάτω ὁ βίος μεταστραφείς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 5.}}
**4** **misrepresent**, [δικαιοσύνης καὶ ἀδικίας] τὴν δύναμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 367a}}: generally, **change, alter**, τὸ δίκαιον οὐκ ἔστι μεταστρέψαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1376b21, compare 1412a33}}; **invert**, τὰ τοῦ Ξενοφάνους {{Refs|[prev. work] 1377a23}}:—_passive_, ὁρᾷς γὰρ τἄμ᾽ ὅσῳ μετεστράφη how my fortunes **are changed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1329; τὸ ψήφισμ᾽ ὅπως μεταστραφείη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 537.}}
**5** ἀντὶ τοῦ ἰῶτα ἦτα μ. **use** one **for** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 418c.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **turn another way, change one's ways**, ἦ τι μεταστρέψει; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.203:}}_aorist_ _participle_ μεταστρέψας **contrariwise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 457a (_plural_), “R.” 587d.}}
**II.2** **turn so as to punish** or **avenge**, of the gods, μή τι μεταστρέψωσιν ἀγασσάμενοι κακὰ ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.67}} (unless _transitive_, **turn back** (upon the sinners),{{Refs|compare μετάτροπος 2).}}
**II.3** with _genitive_, **care for, regard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1226.}}

3345:
** μετασχημᾰτ-ίζω**,
**change the form of** a person or thing, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 903e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 335b26; τὸ σῶμα τῆς ταπεινώσεως NT.Phil.3.21}}; of a building, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5174.10 (6th c.AD)}}:—_middle_, with _Attic dialect_ _future_ -ιοῦμαι, **change one's form**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1012.12}}; **disguise oneself**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.11.1}}:—_passive_, **to be changed in form**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 906c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 298b31, “GA” 747a15, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.57}}; of grammatical change, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 68.5, +others}}
**II** μ. τι εἰς ἐμαυτόν **transfer as in a figure**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.4.6.}}
**III** **change the posture of**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.62 (_passive_), +others}}
**IV** of stars and planets, in _passive_, **change** their **configuration**, πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus “Vent.” 47.}}

3346:
** μετατίθημι**,
_future_ -θήσω:—_middle_, _future_ - θήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.341:}}
**I** **place among**, τῷ κ᾽ οὔ τι τόσον κέλαδον μετέθηκε (variant{μεθέηκεν}) then he **would** not **have caused** so much noise **among** us, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.402.}}
**II** **place differently**,
**II.1** in local sense, **transpose, change the place of**, τὰ αἰδοῖα εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191b; εἰς βελτίω τόπον [prev. author] “Lg.” 903d; μ. τὰς θύρας “PSI” 5.546.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; μετέθηκεν αὐτὸν (i.e. τὸν Ἑνώχ) ὁ θεός {{LXX|LXX.Gen.5.24}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 20b10}}; **to be transferred**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 338.20 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC), NT.Act.7.16, etc.}}
**II.2** in Logic, μ. τὸ συμπέρασμα **alter** a conclusion to its contrary, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 59b1.}}
**II.3** **change, alter**, of a treaty, μεταθεῖναι ὅπῃ ἂν δοκῇ ἀμφοτέροις Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.18; τὸ νυνδὴ ῥηθέν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 297e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.6; μ. τινὰ ἐς πτηνὴν φύσιν “Anthologia Graeca” 11.367 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator)}}; ἐπὶ ὑὸς τὰς ἐπωνυμίας μ. **change** their names **and call them** after swine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.68}}; **substitute**, προφάσεις ἀντὶ τῶν ἀληθῶν ψευδεῖς μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.225, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 683b (_passive_)}}; **correct**, τοὺς ἠγνοηκότας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.67.5}}; but, **pervert**, μετέθηκεν αὐτὸν ἡ γυνὴ αὐτοῦ {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.20(21).25.}}
**II.4** _middle_, **change what is one's own** or **for oneself**, μ. τὰ εἰρημένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.18}}; νόμους{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.4.14; τὴν δόξαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.229; τὸν τρόπον [prev. author] 19.341; τοὔνομα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 549}}; ὀνόματα **change the use of** words, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 95}} G. (also in _active_, {{Refs|“Nat.” 28.5)}}; [τὸ νόμισμα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257b11}}: _absolutely_, **change one's opinion, retract**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 345b, etc.}}; μεταθέσθω let him **change his mind**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 48}}; also in political sense, **change sides**, μεταθέσθαι πρὸς τὴν Ῥωμαίων αἵρεσιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.9.6}}; Dionysius of Heraclea, who went over from the Stoics to the Cyrenaics, was called μεταθέμενος, **turn-coat**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.37, 166; μ. ἀπὸ τῶν πατρίων LXX.2Mac.7.24; ἐξ ἀδικίας 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 11.}}
**II.4.b** τὴν γνώμην μετατίθεσθαι **change to** or **adopt a new** opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18 (but τῆς γνώμης μ.}} **change from**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.29)}}; μετέθου λύσσαν ἄρτι σωφρονῶν **thou hast changed to** madness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 254}}; μ. τὸ ὄνομα τὸ νῦν ἀπὸ τῶν αἰγῶν **adopted** their present name, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 7.26.3.}}
**II.4.c** μ. [τὸν φόβον] **transfer one's** fear, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.177}}; τῇ μισθαρνίᾳ ταῦτα μετατιθέμενος τὰ ὀνόματα **transferring**.., {{Refs|[prev. work]284.}}
**II.4.d** with _infinitive_, μ. ἀντὶ τοῦ ἀπλήστως.. ἔχοντος βίου τὸν κοσμίως.. ἔχοντα βίον ἑλέσθαι **change one's mind and determine** to choose.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 493c.}}
**II.4.e** with _double_ _accusative_, τὸ κείνων κακὸν τῷδε κέρδος μ. **turning** their misdeeds **into** his gain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 515 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.5** _passive_, **to be changed, alter**, μετετέθην εὐβουλίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 388 (troch.)}}; μ. ἐς Ῥωμαίους **pass over**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 17}}; μ. ἀπὸ τοῦ καλέσαντος ὑμᾶς **are turned away** from.., {{NT|NT.Gal.1.6.}}

3348:
** μετέχω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **πεδέχω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 68.2}}; _infinitive_ πεδέχην {{Refs|Ead. “Supp.” 5.22}}: _future_ μεθέξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.86, later μεθέξομαι (_3rd.pers._ _sing._ misspelt μεθέξετε) “IG” 3.1427}}: _perfect_ μετέσχηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80}}:—**partake of, share in**:— Constr.:
**1** mostly with _genitive_ _of things_ only, κακοτάτων, βρόδων, Alc.[same place], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 68.2}}; ἀγαθῶν, κακῶν, βίου, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 82, 354, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 333}}; τῆς τοῦ Μάγου ὕβριος Hdt.[same place]; μ. τοῦ λόγου **to be in** the secret, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.127; τοῦ ἔργου 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.62: with _genitive_ _person_, μ. τῶν πεντακισχιλίων}} **to be members** of the {{Refs|5, 000, Th.}}[same place]; μ. τῆς πόλεως, τῆς πολιτείας, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.48, 30.15; ἐκκλησίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282a29; also ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς ἄρτου μ. NT.1Cor.10.17: with _dative_ _person_ added, μ. τινός τινι}} **partake of** something **in common with** another, οὔ οἱ μ. θράσεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.83; πόνων μ. Ἡρακλέει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 8; τῶν αὐτῶν ἔργων Ἐρατοσθένει μ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.58; μ. ἱερῶν καὶ θυσιῶν τισι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.20; μ. τῶν ἴσων τισί [prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.1.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 805d; κινδύνων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.16.3; also ξὺν σοὶ μετεῖχον τῶν ἴσων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1168.}}
**2** frequently the part or share is added, τοῦ πεδίου οὐκ ἐλαχίστην μοῖραν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204; μ. τάφου μέρος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 507, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 226, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.5; πλεῖστόν σου μέρος μεθέξομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.54.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, μ. τὸ ἴσον (i.e. μέρος) τῶν ἀγαθῶν τινι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.2.28, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 787; μ. τὰς ἴσας πληγὰς ἐμοί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1144; μ. τινὶ τὴν μερίδα “PPetr.” 3p.67 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** rarely with _accusative_ only, ἀκερδῆ χάριν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1484 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5** with _dative_ _of things_ only in a corrupt passage, τῇ.. κατὰ τὴν χώραν.. οἰκήσει μετεῖχον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.16.}}
**6** μ. περὶ ἔργων καὶ τεχνῶν **have some knowledge** respecting.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282a11.}}
**7** _absolutely_, **to be a partner,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 14.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; οἱ μετέχοντες the **partners, accomplices**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.132.}}
**II** in Platonic Philos., **participate in** a universal, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 990b31, 1037b19; τὰ μετέχοντα, opposed to αἱ ἰδέαι, [prev. work] 991a3:}} —_passive_, μετέχονται (i.e. αἱ ἰδέαι) **are participated in**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 990b30, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 4.16, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.650 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, etc.}}
**III** in Aristotelian Logic, **share in**, _i.e. _ admit the definition of, τὰ μὲν εἴδη μετέχει τῶν γενῶν, τὰ δὲ γένη τῶν εἰδῶν οὔ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 121a12, compare 123a8, 143b14.}}

3349:
** μετεωρ-ίζω**,
**raise to a height**, τὸ ἔρυμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90}}; τὸ ἐμβριθὲς ἄγειν ἄνω -ίζουσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246d}}; τὰ σκέλη **lift** the legs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 10.4}}; ἑαυτόν{{Refs|[prev. work] 11.7, compare “Cyn.” 10.13}}; of a dolphin, δελφινίσκον μ. τῷ νώτῳ **lifts** or **buoys** it **up** on his back, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 631a18, compare 602b27}}; τοὺς πόδας μ., of quadrupeds, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IA” 711b19}}; τὸ πνεῦμα μ. **cause one to pant** {{Refs|compare (μετάρσιος 111), [prev. author] “Pr.” 885a33}}; ναῦν μ. εἰς τὸ πέλαγος **put** it **out to sea**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.12 (also _absolutely_, πλεῖν -ίζουσα ἐς τὸ πέλαγος [prev. author] “Her.” 8.3):}} —_middle_, τοὺς δελφῖνας μετεωρίζου **heave up your** dolphins (see. δελφίς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 762}}:—_passive_, **to be raised up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 63c}}; **to be suspended**, σχοινίοις {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“POxy.” 904.6 (5th c.AD)}}; of smoke or dust, **rise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.5}}; of wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 404}}; of water vapour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 8, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 346b28, +others}}; of ships, μετεωρισθεὶς ἐν τῷ πελάγει **keeping out** on the **high** sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.16}}; **rise up**, as from bed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 15}}; of wind **rising** from the stomach, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Coac.” 613}}; μετεωριζόμενος **suffering from flatulence**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 4.41.}}
**2** _intransitive_, **attain considerable height**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.2.4.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **buoy up, elevate**, especially with false hopes, μ. καὶ φυσήσας ὑμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.12, compare 5th c.BC: Hegemon Comicus et Parodus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.698d, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 25.3.4; τοὺς Ἀθηναίους δι᾽ ἐπιστολῶν 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; παραθαρρύνας καὶ μετεωρίσας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 18}}; **unsettle** a man's mind, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.70.10}}:—_passive_, **to be elevated**, ὑπὸ λόγων ὁ νοῦς -ίζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1447; μετεωρισθεὶς καὶ περιχαρὴς γενόμενος ἐπί τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.70.1; μεμετεωρισμένοι ταῖς νίκαις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.32}}; also, **to be anxious,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1679.16 (3rd c.AD)}}, perhaps in this sense {{NT|NT.Luke.12.29.}}

3351:
** μετοικ-ίζω**,
**lead settlers to another abode**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1352a33, “OGI” 264.7 (Pergam.), NT.Act.7.4; σφᾶς αὐτοὺς εἰς Ῥώμην 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 17: _metaphorically_, τὰς φρένας μ. Melanth. Trag. 1:—}}_passive_, {{Refs|Aristeas 4}}:—_middle_, Μυτιλήνη σῶμα μετῳκίσατο {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).443 (Mytil.)}}; also, **go to another country, emigrate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 754, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 84: _metaphorically_, τὸν κλόνον εἰς ὃν ἡ ψυχὴ μετῳκίσατο 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.232.}}
**2** later _intransitive_ in _active_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 880.45 (3rd c.AD).}}

3352:
** μετοχ-ή**, ἡ,
(μετέχω) **sharing, participation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.144, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 345a, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.316.9 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; περὶ μετοχῆς τοῦ παραδείσου their **shares** in the orchard, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 369.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; παρουσία καὶ μ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.945f}}; τίς μ. δικαιοσύνῃ καὶ ἀνομί; {{NT|NT.2Cor.6.14}}; κατὰ μετοχήν in virtue of **participation** in something else, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1030a13, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.47}}; κατὰ μετοχήν τε καὶ μετουσίαν τῆς ἰδέας Polyxenus cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Aphr. “in Metaphorical” 84.17; μ. καὶ θείων καὶ ἀνθρωπίνων πάντων “GDI” 5040.13, compare 5042.8 (from Hierapytna).}}
**2** Astrology texts, **joint possession** or **occupation** by two planets, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antiochus Atheniensis Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).107,115, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “in Ptolemaei Tetrabiblon” 190.}}
**3** **partaking** of food, τῇ τῶν ζῴων μετοχῇ lamb.{{Refs|“VP” 24.108 codices (uncertain reading).}}
**4** **partnership,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PRev.Laws 14.10 (_plural_, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II** Grammars, **participle**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.5, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1011c, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 15.20, +others, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.9, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 138.16.}}
**III** **compactness**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.121(122).3.}}

3353:
** μέτοχος**, ον,
**sharing in, partaking of**, with _genitive_, [τῆς συμφορῆς] τὸ πλεῦν μέτοχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52}}; μ. ἐλπίδων, τέχνης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 698 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 262d}}; τοῦ βίου, of a wife, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 3.5; δίκης 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 401b29.}}
**II** _substantive_, **partner, accomplice in**, τοῦ φόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 721, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.11: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92}}; **partner** in business, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.109.3 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 176.102 (3rd c.BC), “Ostr.Bodl.” i92, 251 (2nd c.BC), NT.Luke.5.7, etc.}}
**II.2** **member of a board** of officials, frequently in phrase ὁ δεῖνα καὶ μέτοχοι πράκτορες, ἐπιτηρηταί, ἀγορανόμοι, τραπεζῖται, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 358.5 (2nd c.AD), “PSI” 2.160.4 (2nd c.AD), “PStrassb.” 52.17 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 96.4 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.3** **joint owner** of a house, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPHerm.” 119 A iv 20 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** θεῶν μέτοχοι, of the demigods, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 640.20, compare “IG” 14.2117 (from Rome).}}

3354:
** μετρ-έω**,
Heraclean I _plural_ _imperfect_ ἐμετρίωμες {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 2.45:}}_present_ _participle_ _passive_ μετριώμεναι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work = 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta”] 1.22, 28}}: (μέτρον):—**measure**:
**I** of Space, **measure**, i. e. **pass over, traverse**, πέλαγος μέγα μετρήσαντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.179}}; προτέρω μετρεῖν (i.e. θάλασσαν) **to sail** farther, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.915, compare 4.1779}}:—in _middle_, ἅλα μετρήσασθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.157}}; μετρούμενον ἴχνη τὰ κείνου **measuring** them **with the eyes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 5}}:—_passive_, **to be measured**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 209}}; **to be measured round**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 197.}}
**II** of Time, μακροὶ.. ἂν μετρηθεῖεν χρόνοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 561.}}
**III** of Number, Size, Worth, etc.,
**III.1** **count**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 142; ἐπ᾽ ᾐόνι κύματα μ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.60, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 4.3b.10 (assuming variant, 6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**III.2** **measure**, χώρην ὀργυιῇσι, σταδίοισι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6; χώρας κατὰ παρασάγγας [prev. author] 6.42}}; τῇ γαστρὶ μ. τὴν εὐδαιμονίαν **measure** happiness by sensual enjoyments, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.296; μ. πορφύρᾳ τὸ εὔδα᾽ μον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 15, etc.}}; ὁπηνίκ᾽ ἂν εἲκοσι ποδῶν μετροῦντι τὸ στοιχεῖον ᾖ when you **measure** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 119.7, compare 9; ἀριθμεῖν τἀγαθὰ καὶ μετρεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348a; μ. καὶ ἀριθμεῖν καὶ ἱστάναι [prev. work] 602d}}: —_passive_, Πόντος.. καὶ Ἑλλήσποντος οὕτω μοι μεμετρέαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.86; μετρεῖσθαι πρὸς ἄλληλα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 284d, etc.}}
**III.2.b** in Mathematics texts, of magnitudes or numbers, **measure**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 273b12, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 14, 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.13}} (_active_ and _passive_), etc.; μετρηθῆναι κοινῷ μέτρῳ πρός.. to be commensurable with, [prev. passage]
**III.3** **measure out**, τἄλφιτ᾽ ἐν ἀγορᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1009, compare “Ach.” 548 (_passive_)}}; πώλοισι χόρτον μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 772; μέτρησον εἰρήνης τί μοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1021}}; μετρεῖν τὴν ἴσην **give measure** for measure, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.18.2}}; ἢ μετάδος ἢ μέτρησον ἢ τιμὴν λαβέ **lend by measure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 26}}:—_middle_, **to have measured out to oneself**, in buying or borrowing, εὖ μετρεῖσθαι παρὰ γείτονος **get** good **measure** from one's neighbour, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 349; τὰ ἄλφιτα καθ᾽ ἡμίεκτον μετρούμενοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.37, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.5, “SIG” 976.61 (Samos, 2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 48.}}
**III.4** **deliver, pay**, of corn and other measurable commodities, σῖτόν τινι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.20, “PHib.” 1.39.3 (3rd c.BC); ἔλαιον [prev. work] 131 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_middle_, **receive in payment**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 103 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**IV** **moderate**, of pain, {{Refs|Palladius Medicus “in Hippocratem” 12.273 C.}}

3355:
** μετρ-ητής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**measurer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author = 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus] “Just.” 373a}}; μετρηταὶ στρατοπέδων, = Latin _metatores castrorum_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.2.1.}}
**II** a liquid measure, = {ἀμφορεύς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 7, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.20, Sositheus Tragicus 2.8 (assuming variant), “IG” 12(3).436.13 (Thera, 4th c.BC), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 596a7, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Mens.” 9, etc.; of the Hebrew **bath**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.18.32, +others, NT.John.2.6.}}

3357:
Included with: ** μέτριος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59d}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **μέτερρος** {{Refs|“Lyrica Adespota” 66}} (but μέτριος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1231.5)}}: (μέτρον):—**within measure, moderate**, and so,
**I** of Size, μ. ἄνδρες men **of average height**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32}}; μ. πῆχυς the **common** cubit, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.178}}; ἰσχὰς μ. a **fair-sized** fig, {{Refs|Diocl.fragment 140}}; of Time, μ. μῆκος λόγων the **proper** length of speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338b}}; μ. χρόνος ἀκμῆς a **fair average** time of maturity, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 460e.}}
**II** of Number, [ἱππεῖς] μ. **a reasonable number of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.14.}}
**III** mostly of Degree, **moderate**, ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 306; μ. νῦν ἔπος εὔχου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1059 (Lyric poetry); μ. χάρις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 554 (Lyric poetry); σῖτος -ώτατος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.3}}; τὸ μ. **the mean**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1212 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 719e, “Plt.” 284e; ὁμολογεῖται τὸ μ. ἄριστον καὶ τὸ μέσον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295b4; περαιτέρω τοῦ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.5; πέρα τοῦ μ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.1.4; ἐνδοτέρω τοῦ μ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.656f; τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 125 (_anapaest meter_); εἴη γ᾽ ἐμοὶ μέτρια [prev. author] “Ion” 632; τὰ μ. κεκτῆσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.22; μ. καὶ δίκαια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1137}}; μ. φιλία a friendship **not too great**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 253 (_anapaest meter_); μετρίων λέκτρων μετρίων δὲ γάμων.. κῦρσαι θνητοῖσιν ἄριστον [prev. author] “fragment” 503 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μ. ἐσθῆτι χρῆσθαι **simple** dress, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6}}; μετρία φυλακῇ **not in strict** custody, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.30; βίος μ. καὶ βέβαιος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 466b}}; μ. σχῆμα **modest** apparel, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 511e; μ. οὐσίαν κεκτῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b26}}; οἱ μ. **respectable people**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.10}}; later, **poor**, μ. καὶ δυστυχεῖς {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 120.7 (4th c.AD), etc.: with}}_infinitive_, ὅσον οἰόμεθα μέτριον εἶναι πιεῖν **just sufficient**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117b.}}
**III.2** **tolerable**, οἷς μὴ μ. αἰών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 179 (Lyric poetry); ἀπὸ τῶν μ. ἐπ᾽ ἀμήχανον ἄλγος [prev. author] “El.” 140 (Lyric poetry); μ. ἄχθος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 884 (_anapaest meter_); κακά [prev. author] “Tr.” 722; ναύταις μ. χειμὼν φέρειν [prev. work] 688}}; μετρίων δεομένῳ making a **moderate** request, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.84; τυχεῖν τῶν μετρίων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.4}}; τὰ μ. **tolerable** terms. Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.165; ἐπὶ μετρίοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.22}}; μηδὲν μ. λέγειν nothing **tolerably accurate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 181b}}; -ωτάτη ἡ δημοκρατία **least intolerable**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289b4, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 532.17 (_superlative_).}}
**III.3** of Persons, **moderate** in desires and the like, **temperate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 245; - ώτεροι ἐς τὰ πολιτικά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.89; μ. πρὸς τὰς ἡδονάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 816b; σώφρων καὶ μ. πρὸς τὴν καθ᾽ ἡμέραν δίαιταν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.170; ἐν τῷ σίτῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.17}}; of Love, μάκαρες οἳ μ. θεοῦ (i.e. Ἀφροδίτης) μετέσχον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 543 (Lyric poetry), compare “fragment” 967 (Lyric poetry); εἰ δ᾽ ἦσθα μ. τἄλλα γ᾽ ἡδίστη θεῶν πέφυκας [prev. author] “Hel.” 1105}}; also, **moderate, fair**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 615, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396c, etc.}}; a favourite word in democratic states, μ. καὶ φιλάνθρωπος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.185; σαυτὸν -ώτερον παρέχειν [prev. work]134; μ. πρὸς τοὺς ὑπηκόους}} **mild** towards.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77.}}
**III.4** **proportionate, fitting**, μισθὸς σώφροσι μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 18b; μ. λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.3.}}
**III.5** **enjoying 'middling' health** {{Refs|compare (μετριάζω 1.3), “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(1).182.}}
**B** _adverb_ μετρίως **moderately, within due limits**, ἀπηγήσεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.161}}; **in due measure, neither exaggerating nor depreciating**, εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.35; λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 518b; μ. περὶ αὑτῶν διαλεχθέντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.171}}; μ. ἔχειν to be **in due proportion**, neither too much nor too little, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 191d}}; μ. ἔχειν βίου to be **moderately well** off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; μ. φιλοσοφίας ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 305d: _comparative_ μετριώτερον (below 3), also -ωτέρως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 587a1}}: _superlative_ -ώτατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, etc.}}
**B.2** **enough**, μ. κεχόρευται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1511 (_anapaest meter_); μ. πρὸς τὴν ἐμὴν ἀνάγκην εἰρημένα [prev. author] “Ec.” 969}}; **moderately, pretty well**, ἐν οἰκουμένῃ καὶ μ. πολιτείᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936b; σωφρονοῦσι καὶ μ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.19}}; μ. [λέγειν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 262; ἀποδέξασθαι μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b.}}
**B.3** **modestly, temperately**, χαίρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 921, compare “HF” 709; ἀποκρίνασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.20; μ. βεβιωκώς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.3 (but μ. διάγειν}} to be **moderately** off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.8); πενθεῖν μ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 53.1; φέρειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.85.9}}; **on fair terms**, μ. ξυναλλαγῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19, compare 20}}: _comparative_ -ώτερον, πρός τινας φρονεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.7.}}
**B.4** μ. ἔχειν **to be in 'middling' health,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PLips. 108.6 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**B.II** _neuter_ μέτριον and μέτρια as _adverb_, μέτριον ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 846c (sed to be read μέτρον); μέτρια βασανισθείς [prev. author] “Sph.” 237b}}: also with _Article_, τὸ μέτριον ἀποκοιμηθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.26; τὰ μέτρια διαφέρεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19, compare 8.84.}}

3358:
** μέτρον**, τό,
**that by which anything is measured**:
**1** **measure, rule**, μέτρ᾽ ἐν χερσὶν ἔχοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.422; ἐν μέτροισι ταμὼν δόνακας “hymnus ad Mercurium” 47}}; πάντ᾽ ἄνδρα πάντων χρημάτων μ. εἶναι is **a measure** of all things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 183c, compare 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1053a36; μ. αὐτῷ οὐχ ἡ ψυχή, ἀλλ᾽ ὁ νόμος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.18.}}
**1.b** in Mathematics texts, **measure, divisor**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.13, etc.}}
**2** **measure of content**, whether solid or liquid, δῶκεν μέθυ, χίλια μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.471; εἴκοσι δ᾽ ἔστω μ... ἀλφίτου 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.355; ὕδατος ἀνὰ εἴκοσι μ. χεῦε 9.209, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.268, 741, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 350, 600, etc.}}; at Samos, of the μέδιμνος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 976.55 (2nd c.BC)}}; in Egypt, of the ἀρτάβη, μ. δοχικόν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 11.6 (2nd c.BC); also of smaller units, as μ. ἑξαχοίνικον [prev. work] 105.40 (2nd c.BC)}}; μέτροις καὶ σταθμοῖς **by measure** and weight, Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.83}}; in the widest sense, either **weight** or **measure**, Φείδωνος τοῦ τὰ μ. ποιήσαντος Πελοποννησίοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127}}; μ. οἰνηρά, σιτηρά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1135a2; Κιλικίῳ μ. μετρεῖν “OGI” 579.2 (from Cilicia).}}
**3** **any space measured** or **measurable, length, size**, in _plural_, **dimensions**, μέτρα κελεύθου **the length** of the way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.389; μέτρα θαλάσσης 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 648, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47}}; μορφῆς μέτρα bodily **dimensions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1063}}; τὰ μ. τοῦ λίθου its **distances** from a given point in given directions, its **position**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.ά, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 843e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 23; ἄστρων μέτρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 432.8; ἀπέχει.. θαλάσσης μέτρον ἑξήκοντα σταδίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.95}}; τῷ Ἴστρῳ ἐκ τῶν ἴσων μ. ὁρμᾶται [ὁ Νεῖλος] starts from the same **distances** as (i.e. the position corresponding to the source of) the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Ister Historicus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.33; εἰδέναι τὴν ἑαυτοῦ χώραν μέτρῳ καὶ τόπῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.3; ἐντὸς τῶν μ. τετμημένον μέταλλον 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 35}}; later of Time, **duration**, μέτρα βίοιο ἄρκια {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“APl.” 4.333 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἐτέων μέτρα, ὡράων μέτρον, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.334,9.481}}; μέτρα ἐνιαυτῶν, νυκτός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 464.731; χρονικὰ μ. 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus in “de An.” 299.37.}}
**3.b** **limit, goal**, ὅρμου μ. the **goal** which is the mooring-place, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.101}}; ἥβης μ. ἱκέσθαι the **term** which is puberty, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.225, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 132}}; but, ἥβης μ. ἔχειν **full measure** of youthful vigour,{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work] 438, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1119; σοφίης, γνωμοσύνης μ. 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.52, 16.2.}}
**4** **due measure** or **limit, proportion**, μέτρα φυλάσσεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 694; χρὴ κατ᾽ αὐτὸν παντὸς ὁρᾶν μέτρον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.34; μέτρα μὲν γνώμᾳ διώκων, μέτρα δὲ καὶ κατέχων [prev. author] “I.” 6(5).71; κατὰ μέτρον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 720; πίνειν ὑπὲρ μέτρον 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 498; προστιθεὶς μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 797 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί μ. κακότατος ἔφ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 236 (Lyric poetry)}}; μ. ἔχει have **a moderating power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 836a; πλέον πίνειν τοῦ μέτρου [prev. author] “R.” 621a; μ. ἔχειν [prev. author] “Lg.” 957a}}; μέτρῳ, ={μετρίως},{καταβαίνειν} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.78; οὐδεὶς τῷ μ. τὸ πίνειν ἔστεργε 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.32.}}
**5** τίς ἱππείοις ἐν ἔντεσσιν μέτρα.. ἐπέθηκ᾽ **checks**, i.e. bits, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.20.}}
**II** **metre**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 638, 641, etc.}}; opposed to μέλος (music) and ῥυθμός (time), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 502c, etc.}}; λόγους ψιλοὺς εἰς μέτρα τιθέντες putting into **verse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 669d; τὰ ἐν μέτρῳ πεποιημένα ἔπη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.21.}}
**II.2** _plural_, **verses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 205a. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} ***métro-m** from ***métro-m** 'measuring instrument', cf. Gothic _mitan_ 'measure'.)

3359:
** μέτωπ-ον**, τό,
also **μέτωπ-ος**, ὴ, {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum” (assuming variant)}}: (μετά, ὤψ):—properly **the space between the eyes** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 491b12)}}, **brow, forehead**, ὁ δὲ προσιόντα [ἤλασεν] μέτωπον ῥινὸς ὑπὲρ πυμάτης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13. 615, etc.; στίγματα ἔχων ἐν τῷ μ. “IG” 42(1).121.48 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; χαλάσας τὸ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 655}}; mostly of men, but of a horse in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.454, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 727}}; of a boar, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.8}}; of a dog, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 4.1}}: in _plural_, of a single person, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.107, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1568, etc.; τὰ μέτωπ᾽ ἀνέσπασεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 631.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, γαίας μ., of Etna, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.30.}}
**II** **front, face** of anything, as a wall or building, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.178, 2.124; τεῖχος ὡς ἐπὶ δέκα σταδίους.. μ. ἕκαστον measuring 10}} stades on each **face**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.15, compare “IG” 22.463.66, 7.4255.19, “BCH” 20.324.65 (Lebad.)}}; τὰ μ. τῶν κλιμακτήρων vertical **faces** of the steps, {{Refs|“IG” 22.244.80}}; **wall** extending inwards between two doors, {{Refs|[prev. work]1657.3, 1668.23,59 (uncertain meaning in 12.372.30)}}; **front** or **front-line** of an army, fleet, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 720, etc.}}; εἰς μ. στῆναι to stand in **line**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.2; ἐπὶ μετώπου διιέναι, opposed to ἐπὶ κέρως or κέρας (in column), [prev. work] 2.4.3}}; ἐν μετώπῳ καθιστάναι, παρατάξασθαι,{{Refs|[prev. work] 2.4.4, “HG” 2.1.23.}}
**II.2** **margin** of a book, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.624, 17(1).80, 5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 25.}}
**III** ={χαλβάνη}, or **the reed** or **wood which yields it**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.59,3.83.}}
**III.2** variant for{νέτωπον} (which see).

3360:
** μέχρῐ**,
and **μέχρῐς**, _adverb_ **as far as**, so used chiefly in Prose and before a _preposition_, μέχρι πρός.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 25b, “Criti.” 118a; μ. εἰς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.26; ἐς γόνυ μ. χιτῶνα ζώννυσθαι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 11: before Advs. of Place or Time, μ. ἐνταῦθα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 222a, +others; μ. δεῦρο τοῦ λόγου [prev. author] “Smp.” 217e}}; μ. ὅποι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 487c}}; μ. ὅπου.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 169; οὕτω μέχρι πόρρω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.163; μ. τότε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.24; μ. τὰ νῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 686b}}; μ. νῦν (variant{τοῦ νῦν}) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.110; μ. καὶ νῦν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.2.13}}; μέχρι πότε χηρεύομε; {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 4.1.}}
**II** _preposition_ with _genitive_, **even to, as far as**,
**II.1** of Place, μέχρι θαλάσσης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.143; μ. τοῦ γούνατος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.80; μ. τῆς πόλεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.96, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.6, +others}}: rarely following its case, ὀμφαλοῦ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 925a, compare “Supp.epigram” 3.400.5 (Delph., 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** of Time, τέο μέχρι; i.e. τίνος μέχρι χρόνο; how **long**? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.128}}; μέχρις τε; {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.1: in Prose, μέχρι τούτου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4}}; μέχρι οὗ, μέχρι ὅσου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 245a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.3, +others}}; μ. τοσούτου, ἕως ἂν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90; μ. τούτου,.. μέχρις ἂν ῥηθῶσιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.91, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81d: with the _Article_, τὸ μ. ἐμεῦ}} **up to** my time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.10, 5.115}}; μ. τῆς ἐκείνου ζόης **till the end of** his life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.160; μ. ἡμερέων ἑπτά [prev. author] 6.12; μέχρι Πυθίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.1; μέχρι ἡλίου δύντος “IG” 12.188.4.}}
**II.3** of Measure or Degree, μ. τοῦ δικαίου **so far as** consists with right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82; μ. τοῦ δυνατοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 498e}}; μ. ὑγιείας, μ. ἡδονῆς,{{NT|[prev. work] 559a, “Grg.” 500b; μ. θανάτου NT.Phil.2.8.}}
**II.4** with Numbers to express a round sum, **up to, about, nearly**, μ. δώδεκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.8, etc.}}: sometimes without altering the case of the _substantive_, τοὺς μ. τριάκοντα ἔτη γεγονότας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.133; but πίνειν.. τοὺς μ. ἐτῶν τριάκοντα 4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Carystius Comicus 5.19}}; μ. τινὸς πλήθους **up to** a certain number, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 15.3}}: hence, **just short of**, μ. κόρου μετρεῖσθαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.8.5.}}
**II.5** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, μέχρι οὗ}} is sometimes used like the simple μέχρι, μέχρι οὗ ὀκτὼ πύργων {{Refs|1.181; μέχρι οὗ τροπέων τῶν θερινέων 2.19; μ. ὅτεν πληθώρης ἀγορῆς [prev. work] 173.}}
**III** as a _conjunction_, **until**, with _indicative_, μέχρι.. ὁρμὴ ἐνέπεσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220d; μ. σκότος ἐγένετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.4}}; μέχρι ἄν with _subjunctive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.4.13, 2.3.24; μέχρις ἂν ἥλιος δύῃ “IG” 12(5).647.17 (from Ceos)}}; μέχρις κε μένῃ {{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Sosibii Victoria” 5.4: rarely without ἄν, μ. τοῦτο ἴδωμεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.119; μ. πλοῦς γένηται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; μ. οὗ τι δόξῃ [prev. author] 3.28; μέχρι τέκῃ 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Sosibii Victoria” 5.5; μέχρις οὗ εἴπῃ 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.43; μ. καταντήσωμεν NT.Eph.4.13; μέχρις ἵνα ψαύσειε 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 28 (assuming variant)}}: with _infinitive_, μ. σβεσθῆναι τὸ πῦρ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 75; μέχρις ἠῶ δῖαν ἱκέσθαι 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.830; also μέχρι ἂν ἕξιν λαβεῖν 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 35.}}
**III.2** **as long as, whilst**, with _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.10, 98, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.62.4}}; μ. ἄν with _subjunctive_, μέχρις ἂν ζῶσιν πονεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 633; μέχρις ἂν ἐνδημῶσιν οἱ πρέσβεις 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 10.11, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 11}}; _Doric dialect_ μέχρι κα ζώη {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“GDI” 1807.7 (2nd c.BC), +others}}—The **μετώπο-ι** is elided in {{Refs|“IG” 12.115.15, “Supp.epigram” [same place]}} —Cf. ἄχρι throughout and entry near the end

3361:
** μή**,
Elean **μά** [ᾱ] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9.5 (Olympia, 6th c.BC)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _mā´_, Armenian _mi_ [from I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **mē´**], negative used in prohibitions):—**not**, the negative of the **will** and **thought**, as οὐ of **fact** and **statement**; μή rejects, οὐ denies; μή is relative, οὐ absolute; μή subjective, οὐ objective. (A few examples of μηδέ and μηδείς have been included.)
**A** in INDEPENDENT sentences, used in expressions of **will** or **wish, command, entreaty, warning**,
**A.1** with _present_ _imperative_, 2 _person_, μή μ᾽ ἐρέθιζε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.32, +others; 3 _person_, μή μευ πειράτω 9.345, etc.}}: rarely with _aorist_ _imperative_, μὴ.. ἔνθεο τιμῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.410, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.248}}; in _Attic dialect_, μὴ ψεῦσον, ὦ Ζεῦ, τῆς.. ἐλπίδος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 870; 3 _person_, μή τις ἀκουσάτω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.301, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.55, “P.” 5.23, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1041, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1180; μηδεὶς νομισάτω, προσδοκησάτω 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c: with}}_perfect_ _imperative_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3 _person_, μή τις ὀπίσσω τετράφθω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.272; or 2}} _person_ when _perfect_ = _present_, μὴ κεκράγετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 415.}}
**A.2** with _subjunctive_ (usually {{Refs|2 _person_ of _aorist_)}}, in prohibitions, μὴ δή με.. ἐάσῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.684, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 583 (Lyric poetry), +others}}; μή τοί με κρύψῃς τοῦτο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 625, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 470; μὴ φθονήσῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320c}}: coupled with _present_ _imperative_, μὴ βοηθήσητε τῷ πεπονθότι δεινά, μὴ εὐορκεῖτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.211; 3 _person_, μὴ.. γένηται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.37, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.213; μὴ ματεύσῃ θεὸς γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.24: rarely, if ever, with 2}} _person_ _present_ _subjunctive_, μὴ κάμνῃς [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1143 (to be read κάμῃς); 3 _person_, μή τις οἴηται, ={μὴ οἰώμεθα}, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 861e}}]: also with the hortative _subjunctive_ used to supply the {{Refs|1}} _person_ of the _imperative_, _present_ μὴ ἴομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(ἴωμεν) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.216, etc.; μὴ διώκωμεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109, etc.}}: _aorist_ μὴ πάθωμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.11, etc.}}: rarely with _1st pers._ _singular_, μή σε.. κιχείω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.26, compare 21.475, 22.123, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 174}} (_anapaest meter_).
**A.2.b** with _present_ or _aorist_ _subjunctive_ in a warning or statement of fear, μὴ.. γένησθε **take care you do** **not become**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.487}}; μὴ.. ὑφαίνῃσιν **I fear.. may prove to be weaving**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.356; αἷμα μὴ σοῖς ἐξομόρξωμαι πέπλοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1399}}: in _Attic dialect_ Prose, to make a polite suggestion of apprehension or hesitation, **perhaps**, μὴ ἀγροικότερον ᾖ τὸ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 462e, compare “Tht.” 188d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291a8, +others}}: in later Greek the _indicative_ is found, μὴ ἡ ἔννοια ἡμῶν.. ἀντιλαμβάνεται {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 27.}}
**A.3** with _future_ _indicative_, a uncertain usage (νεμεσήσετ᾽ is _subjunctive_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.115), μηδεμίαν ἄδειαν δώσετε 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.13}}; μὴ βουλήσεσθε (Papyrus βούλη[σθ]ε) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.117; compare μαλακὸν ἐνδώσετε μηδέν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 488.}}
**A.4** with past tenses of _indicative_ to express an unfulfilled wish, μὴ ὄφελες λίσσεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.698, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.548; μή ποτ᾽ ὤφελον λιπεῖν τὴν Σκῦρον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 969; εἴθε μή ποτ᾽ εἰδόμαν [prev. author] “OT” 1217 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 70, “Cyc.” 186, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.3.}}
**A.5** with _optative_ to express a negative wish, with _present_, ἃ μὴ κραίνοι τύχη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 426, compare “Eu.” 938 (Lyric poetry)}}: more frequently with _aorist_, μὴ σέ γ᾽ ἐν ἀμφιάλῳ Ἰθάκῃ βασιλῆα Κρονίων ποιήσειεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.386, compare 403, 11.613.}}
**A.6** in oaths and asseverations, ἴστω Ζεὺς.., μὴ μὲν τοῖς ἵπποισιν ἀνὴρ ἐποχήσεται ἄλλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.330; ἴστω νῦν τόδε Γαῖα.., μὴ.. Ποσειδάων.. πημαίνει Τρῶας 15.41; μὰ τὴν Ἀφροδίτην.., μὴ ἐγώ σ᾽ ἀφήσω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1000, compare “Av.” 195, “Lys.” 917.}}
**A.7** with _infinitive_, when used as _imperative_, μὴ δή μοι ἀπόπροθεν ἰσχέμεν ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.501; μὴ πρὶν ἐπ᾽ ἠέλιον δῦναι 2.413; οἷς μὴ πελάζειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 712.}}
**A.8** frequently without a Verb, εἰ χρή, θανοῦμαι. Answ. μὴ σύ γε (i.e. θάνῃς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1441}}; ἄπελθε νῦν. Answ. μὴ (i.e. γενέσθω) ἀλλά **nay but**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 458}}; in curt expressions, μὴ τριβὰς ἔτι (i.e. ποιεῖσθε) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 577}}; μή μοι σύ **none of that** to me! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 964}}; μή μοι πρόφασιν **no** excuses! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 345; μή μοί γε μύθους [prev. author] “V.” 1179.}}
**B** in DEPENDENT clauses:
**B.1** with Final Conjs., ἵνα μή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.348, etc.; ὅπως μή 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.5, +others; ὡς μή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 53, +others; ὄφρα μή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.118, +others: with ὅπως ἄν and ὡς ἄν}}, **that so**, ὅπως ἂν.. μηδέ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 178, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481a; ὡς ἂν μή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.749, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5}}; but
**B.1.b** μή alone, ={ἵνα μή}, **lest**, ἀπόστιχε μή τινοήσῃ Ἥρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.522, compare 587; λίσσεσθαι.., μή οἱ.. χολώσαιτο φρένα κούρη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.147}}: _future_ _indicative_ and _aorist_ _subjunctive_ in consecutive clauses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 495 (Lyric poetry, uncertain reading).}}
**B.2** in the protasis of conditional sentences, see at {εἰ} (for the exceptions see at {οὐ}), and with temporal conjunctions used conditionally, see at {ἐπειδάν, ὅταν, ὅτε}, etc.
**B.2.b** ὅτι μή **except**, ὅτι μὴ Χῖοι μοῦνοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26; ὅτι μὴ πᾶσα ἀνάγκη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67a; ὅσον μή [prev. work] 83a}}; ὅσα μὴ ἀποβαίνοντες **provided only that** they did **not** disembark, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16.}}
**B.3** in later Gr., with causal Conjs., ὁ μὴ πιστεύων ἤδη κέκριται, ὅτι μὴ πεπίστευκεν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.3.18, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 21.2, “D Deor.” 2.1; ἐπεὶ μή [prev. author] “Hist.Conscr.” 3, etc.: also after ὅτι and ὡς}} **that**, ὅτι μὴ ἐστὶν ἐπίπεδος οὕτως ἂν καταμάθοιμεν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.8, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 29, “DDeor.” 20.10.}}
**B.4** in _relative_ clauses, which imply a condition or generality, ὃς δὲ μὴ εἶδέ κω τὴν κανναβίδα **whoever**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.74}}; ὃ μὴ κελεύσει (perhaps κελεύσαι) Ζεύς **such a thing as**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 618, compare 661, 899}}; λέγονθ᾽ ἃ μὴ δεῖ **such things as** one ought **not**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 583; λόγοις τοιούτοις οἷς σὺ μὴ τέρψῃ κλύων [prev. author] “Ant.” 691; ὅπου μὴ ἠθέλησεν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.7}}: frequently with _subjunctive_, ᾧ μὴ ἄλλοι ἀοσσητῆρες ἔωσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.165, +others: with ἄν, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 281: with _optative_, ἃ μὴ σαφῶς εἰδείη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.19, etc.: less frequently with _optative_ and ἄν, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 20a, “Lg.” 839a; γένοιτο δ᾽ ἂν ἐν ᾗ μή τι ἂν προσδοκήσειεν χώρᾳ [prev. work] 872d.}}
**B.5** with _infinitive_,
**B.5.a** regularlyfrom Homer on, except after Verbs of saying and thinking (but see below c): after ὥστε or ὡς, ὥστε μὴ φρονεῖν [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 725 (troch.), etc. (for exceptions see at {οὐ})}}]: always when the _infinitive_ takes the _Article_, τὸ μὴ προμαθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.60; τὸ μὴ ἀμελεῖν μάθε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 86, compare 749, “Pr.” 624; λείπομαι ἐν τῷ μὴ δύνασθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 496.}}
**B.5.b** by an apparent pleonasm after Verbs of negative result signifying to **forbid, deny**, and the like, ὁ δ᾽ ἀναίνετο μηδὲν ἑλέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.500 (without μή [prev. work] 450); ἀντιδικεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.12}} (μηδέν); ἀντιλέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.15}} (μηδέ); ἀπαγορεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.9; ἀπειπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 557, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.19, etc.; ἀπαυδᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1072; ἀπείργειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1559, +others (without μή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 70); ἀπιστεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.40; ἀπεγνωκέναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.34; ἀποστερεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.1}} (μηδέ); ἀποτρέπεσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.32}} (μηδέν); ἀρνεῖσθαι, ἔξαρνος εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 572, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.67; ἐναντιωθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32b; σχεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.158}}; παύειν (where the _participle_ is more frequently) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 634; κωλῦσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1269; ἐπάρατον ἦν μὴ οἰκεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17}}: in these cases the _Article_ frequently precedes μή, τὸ δὲ μὴ λεηλατῆσαι.. ἔσχε τόδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.101}}; ἐξομῇ τὸ μὴ εἰδένα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 535}}; εἴργειν τὸ μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.1, etc.; also ἀπέφυγε τὰ σφῷν τὸ μὴ πίτνειν κακῶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1740 (Lyric poetry): with _Article_ in _genitive_, ἔχειν τοῦ μή.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.71}}; ἐμποδὼν γίγνεσθαι τοῦ μή.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.4.23.}}
**B.5.c** after Verbs of saying and thinking which involve an action of will, as in those signifying to **swear, aver, believe**, and the like; so after ὄμνυμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.133, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.179, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165, 2.179, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1047, etc.; μαρτυρῶ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.15, etc.; ὁμολογῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336b, “Smp.” 202b, compare “Phd.” 93d, etc.; ἐγγυῶμαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336d; πέπεισμαι [prev. author] “Ap.” 37a, etc.; πιστεύω 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.8, etc.}}: occasionally with other Verbs, φημί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155a}}; λέγω, προλέγω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49, 1.139}}; πάντες ἐροῦσι μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.18}}; νομίζω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.5.59, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.102; ἡγοῦμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27d}}: very frequently in later Gr., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|NT.Matt.2.12, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 44, etc.}}
**B.6** with the _participle_, when it can be resolved into a conditional clause, μὴ ἐνείκας, = {εἰ μὴ ἤνεικε}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64}}; μὴ θέλων, ={εἰ μὴ θέλεις}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 504}}; μὴ δολώσαντος θεοῦ, ={εἰ μὴ ἐδόλωσε}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 273}}; μὴ δρῶν, ={εἰ μὴ δρῴην}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 77, etc.: in a general or characteristic sense, δίδασκέ με ὡς μὴ εἰδότα}}, = **ut qui nihil sciam**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1155, compare “Ant.” 1063, 1064}}; τίς πρὸς ἀνδρὸς μὴ βλέποντος ἄρκεσι; **one who** sees **not**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 73}}: in this _signification_ frequently with the _Article_, ὁ μὴ λεύσσων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 828 (Lyric poetry); ὁ μὴ δουλεύσας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 762e; τῷ μὴ εἰργασμένῳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.65; τὸν.. μὴ φροντίσαντα 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 27, compare 45, etc.}}: with causal significance, μὴ παρὼν θαυμάζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 289, etc.; ἄθλια πάσχω μὴ.. μόνον βιαζόμενος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.4; ἑτέρας μηδεμιᾶς ὁμολογουμένης εἶναι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.16; μηδενὸς ἐμποδὼν ὄντος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.8}}: very frequently in later Greek, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 38.16 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 12.4, etc.}}: occasionally after Verbs of knowing and showing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 79, “OC” 656, 797, 1122, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 970, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.76, 2.17.}}
**B.7** with _Substantives_, _adjectives_, and _adverbs_ used generically, with or without _Article_, τὰ μὴ δίκαια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 432; δίκαια καὶ μὴ δίκαια [prev. author] “Ch.” 78 (Lyric poetry); τὸ μὴ 'νδικον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 682 (Lyric poetry); τὸ μὴ καλόν [prev. author] “Ant.” 370 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἡ μὴ 'μπειρία, ={τὸ μὴ ἔχειν ἐμπειρίαν}, **want of** experience, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 115; ἡ μὴ ἐπιτροπή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966c}}; δῆμον καὶ μὴ δῆμον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 759b; ὁ μὴ ἰατρός [prev. author] “Grg.” 459b; νίκης μὴ κακῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 903, compare “Th.” 411; τῷ φρονοῦντι μὴ καλῶς [prev. author] “Pr.” 1012, compare “Ag.” 349, 927.}}
**B.8** after Verbs expressing **fear** or **apprehension** (compare μὴ οὐ):
**B.8.a** when the thing feared is _future_, mostly with _subjunctive_: with _present_ _subjunctive_, δεινῶς ἀθυμῶ μὴ βλέπων ὁ μάντις ᾖ **shall proveto be**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 747, compare “Ant.” 1113; ὅρα μὴ κυβεύῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314a}}: more frequently with _aorist_, δείδοικα.. μή σε παρείπῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.555, compare 9.244, 13.745}}: with _perfect_, **shall prove to have been**, δέδοικα μὴ περαιτέρω πεπραγμέν᾽ ᾖ μοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 663, compare “Ph.” 494, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119, 4.140, etc.}}: less frequently with _future_ _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 488, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 13a}}: with _optative_ according to the sequence of moods and tenses: _present_ _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 482, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.9}}: _aorist_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.634, etc.}}: _perfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.10}}: with _future_ _optative_ in oratio obliqua, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 6.4.27, “Mem.” 1.2.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 15d: with _optative_ and ἄν, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 631, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus}}**vect.**4.41.
**B.8.b** when the action is _present_ or past, the _indicative_ is used, εἰσόρα μὴ σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν τίθης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1523, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 493, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196c; ὅρα μὴ παίζων ἔλεγεν [prev. author] “Tht.” 145b, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 119; φοβούμεθα μὴ ἀμφοτέρων ἡμαρτήκαμεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 209, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 218d; δείδω μὴ δὴ πάντα θεὰ νημερτέα εἶπεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.300.}}
**B.8.c** with _indicative_ and _subjunctive_ in consecutive clauses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 93.}}
**C** in QUESTIONS:
**C.I** direct questions,
**C.I.1** with _indicative_, where aneg. answer is anticipated (but more generally in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 683 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1502, “Tr.” 316, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 488b), in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only ἦ μή..; 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.200, 9.405}}; μή σοι δοκοῦμεν..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 344, compare “Pr.” 249, 959, etc.: in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ frequently ἆρα μ; [prev. author] “Th.” 208, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 446, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 405a: for questions in which μή}} (μηδέ) follows οὐ, see at {οὐ μή}.
**C.I.1.b** in other questions, τί μὴ ποιήσ; what am I **not** to do? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1276 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί μ; why **not**? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 668 (assuming variant)}}; compare μήν
**C.I.2** with _subjunctive_, when the speaker deliberates about a _negative_ action, μὴ οὕτω φῶμε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 335c, compare 337b, 417b}}; ὁ τοιοῦτος μὴ δῷ δίκη; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.35}}; πῶς μὴ φῶμε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161e: with _optative_ and ἄν, πῶς ἄν τις μὴ θυμῷ λέγοι}}; how can a man **help being** excited when he speaks? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 887c, compare “Grg.” 510d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.10.}}
**C.II** indirect questions, frequently with Verbs implying **fear** and **apprehension** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8), ὄφρα ἴδωμεν μὴ τοὶ κοιμήσωνται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.98, compare 101, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.395; περισκοπῶ μή πού τις.. ἐγχρίμπτει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 898, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; also σκοπεῖσθαι πῶς ἂν μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.8, compare 15.6}}; later in simple indirect questions, ἐπυνθάνετο μὴ ἔγνω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 23.5.}}
**C.II.2** in questions introduced by εἰ, ἤρετό με.. εἰ μὴ μέμνημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.36 (εἰ οὐκ in same sense, 1.84): in the second part of a disjunctive question, εἰ.. ἢ (or εἴτε) μή.., εἴτε.. εἴτε μή.., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 468, 612, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a, “R.” 457d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.7}}; εἴτε.. εἴτε μή.., εἰ.. ἢ οὔ, εἰ.. ἢ μή without difference of meaning between μή and οὐ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.9; so also, τοὺς νόμους καταμανθάνειν εἰ καλῶς κεῖνται ἢ μή,.. τοὺς λόγους, εἰ ὀρθῶς διδάσκουσι τὸ πρᾶγμα ἢ οὔ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14.}}
**D** POSITION of μή. When the _negative_ extends its power over the whole clause, μή properly precedes the Verb. When its force is limited to single words, it precedes those words. But Poets sometimes put μή after the Verb, ὄλοιο μή πω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 961}}; φράσῃς.. μὴ πέρα{{Refs|[prev. work] 332, compare “OC” 1522.}}
**D.2** μή is sometimes repeated, μή, μή καλέσῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1418, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 190, “OC” 210 (both Lyric poetry).}}
**E** PROSODY: in Trag. μή may be joined by synizesis with a following ει or ου, μὴ οὐ, μὴ εἰδέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 13, 221, “Tr.” 321, etc.}}: initial ε after μή is cut off by aphaeresis, μὴ 'πὁθουν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 962; μὴ 'μβαίνῃς [prev. author] “OC” 400; μὴ 'γώ [prev. author] “Ph.” 910: in Prose, μὴ 'κ “IG” 12.115.11: μή}} followed by α is sometimes written μἀ.. (see. μὴ ἀλλά, etc.); sometimes separately, μὴ ἀδικεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 85, etc.}}
**F** μή in COMPOSITION (joined with other words), as μὴ ἀλλά, μὴ γάρ, μὴ οὐ, μὴ ὅπως or ὅτι, μή ποτε, etc., will be found in alphabetical order.

3362:
Related to: ** ἐάν**
(so early Attic Inscrr., as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.3.20, εἰάν sometimes after BC 400 4th c.BC, [prev. work]22.28.17,compare “PEleph.” 1.8, 10 (4th c.BC))}}, also _contraction_ **ἤν** and **ἄν**, see at {ἤν},{ἄν} (B) [ᾱ], which by crasis with καί become κἄν:—**if haply, if**, regularly followed by _subjunctive_: for its use and for examples, see at {εἰ} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11, and ἄν (A) 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1.}}
**II** in Hellenistic and late Greek, ={ἄν} after relative Pronouns and Conjunctions, as ὃς ἐάν who**soever**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.15.14, “PTeb.” 107.8 (2nd c.BC), NT.Matt.5.19, +others; ὅσος ἐάν “PPetr.” 3p.120 (3rd c.BC), NT.Matt.18.18; ὅστις ἐάν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.220, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 9.23; ὅπου ἐάν NT.Matt.8.19, etc.; ὅθεν ἐάν “Geoponica” 1.3.3: followed by _indicative_, LXX.1Ki.2.14. [The second syllable of ἐάν is long, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1407, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 228, 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 6.9.]}}

3363:
Included with: ** ἵνα**,
_adverb_,
**I** of Place,
**I.1** **in that place, there**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἵ. γάρ σφιν ἐπέφραδον ἠγερέθεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.127 (_accusative_ to 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis).}}
**I.2** elsewhere _relative_, **in which place, where**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.558, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.136, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.133, 9.27, 54, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.95, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.79, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 21, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 22, 855, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1231, etc.}}: rarely in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.72 (see. below), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c, “Phlb.” 61b}}; ἵ. ἡ Νίκη (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1407.13: rare in later Greek, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.2, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 22, “Ind.” 3: with particles, ἵ. τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.478; ἵ. περ 24.382, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.364, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator}} [same place]; ἵν᾽ ἄν with _subjunctive_, **wherever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 405, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 315}}; as indirect _interrogative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179, 2.150, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1008.}}
**I.2.b** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, like other Advs. of Place, with _genitive_, ἵ. τῆς χώρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98}}; ἔμαθε ἵ. ἦν κακοῦ **in what** a calamity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.213; οὐδ᾽ ὁρᾶν ἵν᾽ ἐ̄ κακοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 367}}; ἵν᾽ ἕσταμεν χρείας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1442; ἵν᾽ ἦμεν ἄτης [prev. author] “El.” 936; ὁρᾷς ἵν᾽ ἐσμὲν αὐτοῦ πέρι τῆς ἀπορίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 243b.}}
**I.2.c** with Verbs of motion, **whither**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.821, +others; ὁρᾷς ἵν᾽ ἥκεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 687, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.10; ιναπερ ὥρμητο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74.}}
**II** of circumstance, γάμος.., ἵ. χρή **at which, when**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.27}}; ἵ. μὲν ἐξῆν αὐτοῖς.., ἐνταῦθα.. **when** it was in their power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.9.}}
**II.2** ={ἐάν}, uncertain in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.353 (variant{ἵν᾽ ἄν}, cf. Scholia), 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.7 codices, variant in 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.1, and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 176b.}}]
**B** Final _conjunction_, **that, in order that**, from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} downwards, mostly first word in the clause, but sometimes preceded by an emphatic word, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 169d; ἵ. δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.26, 23.207, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420e, 610c: never with ἄν}} or κε (if found, these particles belong to the Verb, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.156, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1579).}}
**B.I** general usage:
**B.I.1** with _subjunctive_,
**B.I.1.a** after primary tenses of _indicative_, also _subjunctive_ and _imperative_: _present_ _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.252, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.111, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.2.3, “Cyr.” 1.2.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.2}}: _perfect_ _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.203, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.129}}: _future_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.307, 4.591, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.15}}; _subjunctive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 364; _imperative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.348, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 61, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 880, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 297, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341b, “Men.” 71d.}}
**B.I.1.b** after historical tenses, in similes, where the _aorist_ is gnomic, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.490 (αὔοι codices)}}; where _aorist_ is treated as equivalent to _perfect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.99, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.580, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.26}}: when the purpose is regarded from the point of view of the speaker's present, σὲ παῖδα ποιεύμην ἵ. μοι.. λοιγὸν ἀμύνῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.495, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 6.100, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.44, +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.11,12, +others}}
**B.I.1.c** after _optative_ and ἄν, when _optative_ with οὐκ ἄν is used with sense of _imperative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.264, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.58}}; after βουλοίμην ἄν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12.}}
**B.I.1.d** after _imperfect_ with ἄν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.7.}}
**B.I.2** with _optative_,
**B.I.2.a** after historical tenses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.3, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.2, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 215, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314c, etc.}}: after the historical _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 11}}: sometimes both moods, _subjunctive_ and _optative_, follow in consecutive clauses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.77, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.76, 9.51, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.93, 49.14.}}
**B.I.2.b** after _optative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.369, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 325; βούλοιντ᾽ ἂν ἡμᾶς ἐξολωλέναι, ἵνα.. λάβοιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 413.}}
**B.I.2.c** rarely after primary tenses, by a shifting of the point of view, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.250, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410c.}}
**B.I.3** with past tenses of _indicative_,
**B.I.3.a** after unfulfilled wishes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cri.” 44d.}}
**B.I.3.b** after _indicative_ with ἄν, to express a consequence which has not followed or cannot follow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1389, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.17: especially after ἐβουλόμην ἄν.., 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 961, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.3.}}
**B.I.3.c** after such Verbs as ἐχρῆν, ἔδει, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 647, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c, “Smp.” 181e, “Euthd.” 304e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.48, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 349.5, etc.: whenan unfulfilled obligation is implied, τεθαύμακα ὅτι οὐκ εἶπεν (={ἔδει εἰπεῖν}).. ἵ... 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161c}}]; ἀντὶ τοῦ κοσμεῖν (={δέον κοσμεῖν}).. ἵ... {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.47.}}
**B.I.3.d** after _present_ _indicative_ in general statements (including the past), οὐδὲ γὰρ τὸ εἶναι ἔχει ἡ ὕλη, ἱ. ἀγαθοῦ ταύτῃ μετεῖχεν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.5.}}
**B.I.4** ἵ. μή as the _negative_ of ἵνα, **that not**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.348, etc.}}
**B.II** special usages:
**B.II.1** like{ὅπως}, after Verbs of command and entreaty, is common only in later Gr. (but[{{NT+8th c.BC+|compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.327 with [prev. work] 19), ἀξιοῦν ἵ... Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.155; δεήσεσθαι ἵ... 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.83; παρακαλεῖν ἵ... 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.23.27: frequently in “NT”, ἐκήρυξαν ἵ. μετανοήσωσιν NT.Mark.6.12, +others; of will, ὅσα ἐὰν θέλητε ἵ. ποιῶσιν.. [prev. work] 25: hence ἵ. with _subjunctive_ stands for infinitive, ἐν τούτῳ ἵ. καρπὸν φέρητε (={ἐν τῷ φέρειν}) NT.John.15.8, etc.; πρῶτόν ἐστιν ἵ. κοιμηθῶ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.8, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 8.29}}]; also for ὥστε, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.22.14, +others, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.333a, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.33, etc.}}
**B.II.2** **because**, ἵ. ἀναγνῶ ἐτιμήθην I was honoured **because** I read, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anonymus cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 266.5, compare “Conj.” 243.21, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.257, +others}}; not found in literature.
**B.II.3** elliptical usages,
**B.II.3.a** where the purpose of the utterance is stated, Ζεὺς ἔσθ᾽, ἵν᾽ εἰδῇς 'tis Zeus,— [I tell thee this] **that** thou may'st know it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 989; ἵ. μὴ εἴπω ὅτι οὐδεμιᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507d; ἵ. συντέμω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.5; ἵν᾽ ἐκ τούτων ἄρξωμαι [prev. author] 21.43}}; ἵ. δῶμεν.. **granted that.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.34, compare 1.79.}}
**B.II.3.b** in commands, introducing a principal sentence, ἵ. συντάξῃς **order** him.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 240.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἵ. λαλήσῃς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.412.1 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἵ. ἐλθὼν ἐπιθῇς τὰς χεῖρας αὐτῇ {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Mark.5.23,cf. NT.2Cor.8.7, LXX.2Mac.1.9, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.41, 1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus cited in Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 156.}}
**B.II.3.c** ἵ. τί (i.e. γένηται); **to what end?** either _absolutely_ or as a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 719}}; or with a Verb following, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 409, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26d, etc.}}; ἵ. δὴ τ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1192.}}
**B.II.3.d** in indignant exclamations, **to think that.. !** Σωκράτης ἵ. πάθῃ ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.29.16.}}
**B.III** in later Gr. with _indicative_, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.1.11, +others, NT.Gal.2.4, 4.17, etc.}}

3364:
** οὐ μή**,
in independent sentences, is used either in Denial or in Prohibition:
**I** in Denial,
**I.1** with _subjunctive_,
**I.1.a** chiefly of _aorist_, οὔ τι μὴ ληφθῶ δόλῳ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 38, compare 199, 282, “Ch.” 895; οὔ τοί σ᾽ Ἀχαιῶν.. μή τις ὑβρίσῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 560, compare “El.” 42, 1029, “Ph.” 103, 381, “OT” 329, 771, “OC” 450, 1023 (see. below); οὔ τι μὴ φύγητε λαιψηρῷ ποδί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1039, compare “HF” 718; οὐ γὰρ μὴ ἀπώσηται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199, compare 7.53; οὐ μήποτε.. ἐσβάλωσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.95, compare 5.69; οὐ μή ποθ᾽ ἁλῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 662, compare “V.” 394; οὐ μή ποτε δέξηται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 105d, compare “Phdr.” 260e, “R.” 609b; οὐ μὴ κρατηθῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.17, compare 3.2.8 (variant{δέξονται}), “An.” 4.8.13; so οὐδεὶς μή ποθ᾽ εὕρῃ κατ᾽ ἔμ᾽ οὐδὲν ἐλλειφθέν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.246.}}]
**I.1.b** rarely of _present_, mostly with Verbs expressing possibility or ability, οὐ μὴ δύνηται (variant{δυνήσεται}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.5, compare “An.” 2.2.12, “Hier.” 11.15; οὐ μὴ οἷός τ᾽ ᾖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 341b; οὐ γὰρ μὴ δυνατὸς ὦ [prev. author] “Phlb.” 48d: in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1023, for οὐ μή ποτε.. φυγόντες.. ἐπεύχωνται θεοῖς, two Mss. give ἐπεύξωνται}}, and this has been adopted by most _editions_; and in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.24, for οὐ μὴ εἰσίῃς Bekk. restored οὐ μὴ εἴσει εἰς..:—Note: οὐ μή}} with _subjunctive_ probably arose from the ellipsis of a Verb or phrase expressing fear or apprehension; such words are sometimes expressed, οὐ γὰρ ἦν δεινὸν.. μὴ ἁλῷ κοτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.84, compare 7.235, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 650, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28b, “Phd.” 84b, “Grg.” 520d, “R.” 465b.}}—Sts. there is no idea of fear, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 103, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 18}}; the _construction_ is freely used after ὅτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 499b}}; after ὡς, **since**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 461}}; after ὥστε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227d.}}
**I.2** with _future_ _indicative_, οὔ σοι μὴ μεθέψομαί ποτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1052, compare “OC” 176 (Lyric poetry); οὐ μή σ᾽ ἐγὼ περιόψομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 508}}; οὐ μὴ δέξονται (variant{δέξωνται}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.8: the reading in [prev. author] “HG” 1.6.32 is doubtful: in indirect speech the _optative_ is used, ἐθέσπισεν ὡς οὐ μή ποτε πέρσοιεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 611}}: or _infinitive_, εἶπεν.. οὐ μή ποτε.. εὖ πράξειν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1590.}}
**II** in Prohibition, οὐ μή is used interrogatively with _future_ _indicative_ (chiefly of the 2 _person_) so as to express a strong prohibition, οὐ μὴ 'ξεγερεῖς τὸν ὕπνῳ κάτοχο; ={μὴ ἐξέγειρε},{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 978 (_anapaest meter_)}}; οὐ μὴ προσοίσεις χεῖρα μηδ᾽ ἅψῃ πέπλων **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 606, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 296, “Ra.” 298.—The prohibition is changed into a direct command by ἀλλά or δέ, οὐ μὴ λαλήσεις ἀλλ᾽ ἀκολουθήσεις ἐμο; [prev. author] “Nu.” 505, compare “Ra.” 202, 462, 524, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 792}}; οὐ μὴ προσοίσεις χεῖρα βακχεύσεις δ᾽ ἰών**;**{{Refs|[prev. work] 343, compare “Med.” 1151, “El.” 383.}}
**II.2** later οὐ μὴ ἐμπέσω **let me not** fall, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.24.14.}}
**II.3** in questions, οὐ μή, = Latin _nonne_, ὁ θεὸς οὐ μὴ ποιήσῃ τὴν ἐκδίκησιν τῶν ἐκλεκτῶν αὐτοῦ **;** {{NT|NT.Luke.18.7.}}
**III** in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 250, οὐ σῖγα μηδὲν τῶνδ᾽ ἐρεῖς κατὰ πτόλιν}}**;** seems to mean **keep silent and say nothing**.., σῖγα being short for σιγήσει and καὶ being omitted: similarly, οὐ σῖγ᾽ ἀνέξει, μηδὲ δειλίαν ἀρῇ**; submit silently and do not play the coward**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 75, compare “Tr.” 1183, “OT” 637, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 498, “Hel.” 437, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175a.}}

3366:
** μηδέ**,
(μή, δέ) _negative_ Particle (compare οὐδέ): as _conjunction_, **and not** (_Epic dialect_ also, **but not), nor**, connecting two whole clauses, used with the same constructions as μή, μή τι σὺ ταῦτα.. διείρεο μηδὲ μετάλλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.550, etc.: without a _negative_ preceding, 4.302, etc.}}; τεκνοῦσθαι, μηδ᾽ ἄπαιδα θνῄσκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 754 (Lyric poetry), compare “Eu.” 714, “Supp.” 409; ὕδατος, μελίσσης, μηδὲ προσφέρειν μέθυ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 481, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77.}}
**2** in μηδέ.. μηδέ.. the first μ. may belong to μηδέ A, e.g. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.303f, or to μηδέ B, e.g. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391c; μήτε.. μηδέ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327d}}; but μήτε cannot follow μηδέ:—for μηδέ after οὐδέ, see at {οὐ} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.3.}}
**B** as _adverb_, joined with a single word or phrase, **not even, not either**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.375, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.710, etc.}}; repeated emphatically, μηδ᾽ ὅντινα γαστέρι μήτηρ κοῦρον ἐόντα φέροι μηδ᾽ ὃς φύγοι let **not** the babe unborn —**no let not even** it escape, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.58; τὸ μήποτ᾽ αὖθις μηδ᾽ ἀναστῆναι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 569:}} —for μηδέ τι see at {μήτις}.

3367:
** μηδείς**, μηδεμίᾰ, μηδέν
(i.e. μηδὲ εἷς, μηδὲ μία, μηδὲ ἕν): _feminine_ μηδὲ ἴα or μηδεΐα (or -έϊα) {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).6.12 (Mytil.)}}:— **not one, not even one, nobody** (in _neuter_ **nothing**), once in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (who elsewhere uses μή τις, see at {μήτις}), ἀναίνετο μηδὲν ἑλέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.500; μή πως.. μηδὲν ἀνύσσῃς 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 395; μηδὲν ἄγαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 216, etc.: rare in _plural_ (μηδαμοί being used in _Ionic dialect_), μηδένες ἄλλοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.20; μηδένας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 303c.}}]
**2** μηδὲ εἷς (so written) is found in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., as {{Refs|“IG” 12.114.41, 22.487.9 (μηδ᾽ hενί [prev. work]12.73.6), but is used especially in an emphatic sense}}**not even one**, μηδὲ ἕν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 37}}: frequently with an intervening Particle or _preposition_, μηδ᾽ ἂν ἕνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 414d; μηδ᾽ ἐν ἑνὶ χρόνῳ [prev. author] “Prm.” 156c; μηδ᾽ ἐξ ἑνός [prev. author] “Phdr.” 245d; μηδ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἑνί [prev. author] “R.” 553d; μηδὲ περὶ ἑνός [prev. author] “Tht.” 171c}}; μηδ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἑνός, μηδ᾽ ὑπὸ μιᾶς, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 222d, Alcaeus1.122a; μηδὲ ὑφ᾽ ἑνός “IG” 12.32.8.}}
**II** **nobody, naught, good for naught**, κἄμ᾽ ἴσον τῷ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 918: _plural_, οὐ γὰρ ἠξίου τοὺς μηδένας [prev. author] “Aj.” 1114}}; μηδέν or τὸ μηδέν as _substantive_, **naught, nothing**, κεἰ τὸ μ. ἐξερῶ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 234}}; μ. λέγειν to say **what is naught**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.20, etc.; ἡ ἡμετέρη εὐδαιμονίη.. ἀπέρριπται ἐς τὸ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦ μηδενὸς ἀξίη [prev. author] 6.137; ἐπὶ μηδὲν ἔρχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1000; ἐς τὸ μ. ἥκειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 622}}; of persons, τὸ μ. **a good-for-nothing**, τὸ μ. εἶναι, of a eunuch, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106; τοιγὰρ σὺ δέξαι μ᾽ ἐς τὸ σὸν στέγος, τὴν μηδὲν ἐς τὸ μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1166; κἂν τὸ μ. ὦ [prev. author] “Tr.” 1107; τὸ μ. ὄντας [prev. author] “Aj.” 1275; ὁ μ. ὤν [prev. work] 767; ὅτ᾽ οὐδὲν ὢν τοῦ μηδὲν ἀντέστης ὕπερ [prev. work] 1231, compare 1094, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 843, etc.}}; ἧττον αὐτοῖς ἔνι ἢ τὸ μ., i.e. it is a mere impossibility, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180a}}; also μ. εἶναι without the _Article_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 2.}}
**III** _neuter_ μηδέν as _adverb_, **not at all, by no means**, μηδὲν ἐγκέλευ᾽ ἄγαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 72, compare 344; μ. διαφέρειν πλὴν ὀνόματι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 280a, etc.}}: with an _adverb_, μ. αἰνικτηρίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 949: frequently with _comparative_, μ. μᾶλλον, ἧσσον, etc., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 280, 1329, etc.}} — When other negatives, also derived from μή, are used with it, they do not destroy, but strengthen the negation, μηδέποτε μηδὲν αἰσχρὸν ποιήσας ἔλπιζε λήσειν **never** hope to escape, when you have done **anything** base. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.16}}; compare μηθείς.

3368:
** μηδέ-ποτε**,
_Doric dialect_ **μηδέ-ποκα** [prev. work]22.1126.11 (Amphict. Delph., 4th c.BC): _adverb_:— **never**, with _present_ and past tenses, as well as _future_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1000, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315b.}}
**II** μηδέ ποτε **and never**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 717, 744, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1073 (_anapaest meter_).}}

3372:
** μῆκος**,
_Doric dialect_ **μᾶκος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1}}: εος, τό:— **length**, of a club, τόσσον ἔην μῆκος, τόσσον πάχος so large was it **in length**, so large in thickness, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.324; φιλότης ἴση μ. τε πλάτος τε 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.20, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, etc.; ἐς μῆκος [prev. author] 2.155; εἰς τὸ μῆκος LXX.Gen.12.6; ἐν μήκει καὶ πλάτει καὶ βάθει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 235d, compare 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 209a5}}; ἐπὶ μῆκος **lengthwise**, ἐπὶ μ. ἔκτασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HA” 504a15, +others; κατὰ μῆκος [prev. author] “Mete.” 387a2; μ. ὁδοῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 378, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.72, etc.; πλοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; μᾶκος ἔδικε threw **a long distance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).72: _plural_, μήκη καὶ βάθη καὶ πλάτη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 284e, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 26}}; τὰ μεγάλα μ. great **lengths**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356d.}}
**b** **height**, of a wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1130}}; of persons, **stature**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.71}}; μῆκος **in height**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.312; εἰς μ. αὐξάνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.6.}}
**c** generally, μήκει **in linear measurement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147d, compare 148a}}; **linearity, one-dimensional magnitude**, opposed to ἐπίπεδον, βάθος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 817e}}: in Arith., **in the first power,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 3,4.}}
**2** of Time, μ. χρόνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1020; ἐν μ. χρόνου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 69; ἐν χρόνου τινὸς μήκεσιν ἀπλέτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 683a}}; μ. λόγου, μ. τῶν λόγων, a **long** speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 201, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1139; ἐν μήκει λόγων διελθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.62}}; μῆκος **at length**, εἰπέ μοι μὴ μ., ἀλλὰ σύντομα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 446.}}
**3** of Size or Degree, **greatness, magnitude**, ὄλβου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 119}}; μῆκος **in greatness**, ἔοικεν ἄλλῃ μ. οὐδὲν ἡδονῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 393.}}
**4** **longitude**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.4.5, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.1, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 2.12, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 260.5, etc.}}
**5** in Prosody, **length**, opposed to βραχύτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b32, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 15: _plural_, μήκη καὶ βραχύτητας προσῆπτε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400b.}}
**6** **first line of phalanx**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.5. (From same Root as μακρός. Hence μήκιστος, _superlative_ of μακρός.)}}

3373:
** μηκύν-ω**,
_Doric dialect_ **μᾱκ-** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below)}}: _future_ - ῠνῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17}}, _Ionic dialect_ -ῠνέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.35}}: _aorist_ ἐμήκῡνα {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.60:}} —_middle_, see below {{Refs|7}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ μεμήκυσμαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus (see. below), Eust.ad 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 64}}:— **lengthen, prolong**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.12; τὸ μέτωπον τῆς τάξεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 4.9; τὰς ὁδούς [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.13.5; μηκυνθέν τε καὶ σχὸν πλάτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 282e}}; of Time, μ. χρόνον, βίον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 87, 143}}:—_passive_, νοσεύματα μηκυνθέντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 7; ἐμηκύνετο ὁ πόλεμος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.102.}}
**2** **delay, put off**, τέλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.286.}}
**3** μ. λόγον, λόγους, **spin out** a speech, **speak at length**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.35, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1484; τέκν᾽ εἰ φανέντ᾽ ἄελπτα μηκύνω λόγον [prev. author] “OC” 1120; τὴν ἀπολογίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.44; λόγους μακροτέρους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.17: without λόγον}}, **to be lengthy** or **tedious**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1132, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 437a, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 23}}; μ. περί τινος **enlarge** upon.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 71: _accusative_ objecti, μ. τὰ περὶ τῆς πόλεως, τὴν ὠφελίαν}}, **talk at length about, dwell upon**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42, 43}}:—_passive_, **to be expounded at length**, αὖθις ταῦτα μηκυνθήσεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.5; μεμηκυσμένον σύγγραμμα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.26}}; **to be continually repeated**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 12}}; **to be dwelt upon**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 137.}}
**4** μ. βοήν **raise a loud** cry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 489.}}
**5** Grammars, **lengthen** a syllable, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.5.8}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.275f, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 146.18}}; φωνῆεν μηκυνόμενον a vowel **capable of being scanned long**, as αιυ, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 1.4.}}
**6** Arith., **multiply by a fresh factor,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 24,48.}}
**7** _middle_, ἐμακύναντο κολοσσόν **reared a tall** statue, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.171.}}

3374:
** μηλωτή**, ἡ,
(μῆλον A) **sheepskin, any rough woolly skin**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 25, “PTeb.” 38.22 (2nd c.BC), LXX.1Ki.19.13, NT.Heb.11.37, “OGI” 629.32 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 191.9, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 670.}}
**II** (μήλη) = {μηλωτίς}, Erot. see at {κάτοπτρον}.

3375:
** μήν**,
_Doric dialect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 78, etc.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|(7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.5, etc.)}}, and old _Epic dialect_ **μάν** (in Hom.always followed by a vowel except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.895, 765, whereas μήν}} is followed by a consonant except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.45}}; original μάν has probably been changed to μέν except when the metre prevented), a Particle used to strengthen asseverations, **verily, truly**; a synonym of μέν but stronger, and like it always following the word which begins the clause, ὧδε γὰρ ἐξερέω, καὶ μ. τετελεσμένον ἔσται and so **verily**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.410}}; ἴστε μὰν.. ye know **doubtless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).35: frequently with _imperative_, ἄγε μ.}} on **then**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.302; ἄγρει μάν 5.765; ἄναγε μάν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 963 (Lyric poetry); ἕπεο μάν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 182 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** after other Particles,
**II.1** ἦ μήν, **now verily, full surely**, ἦ μὴν καὶ πόνος ἐστίν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.291, compare “hymnus ad Apollinem” 87, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 101; ἦ μάν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.370, 13.354, Sapph.[same place], 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.40, +others (εἶ μάν “IG” 5 (1).1390.27 (Andania, 1st c.BC))}}: strengthened, ἦ δὴ μάν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.538}}: frequently later in strong protestations or oaths, with _infinitive_, ὄμνυσι δ᾽ ἦ μὴν λαπάξειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 531, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1186, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.26: in negation, ἦ μ. μή.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.81, etc. (but also ὀμνύω μὴ μὰν φρονησεῖν “SIG” 527.36 (Crete, 3rd c.BC))}}: in Prose also to begin an independent clause, ὀμνύω.., ἦ μ. ἐγὼ ἐθυόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.31; καὶ νὴ τὸν κύνα,.. ἦ μ. ἐγὼ ἔπαθόν τι τοιοῦτον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22a.}}
**II.2** καὶ μήν, sometimes simply to add an asseveration, see entry [near the start],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.13, etc.; καὶ δὴ μάν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.120}}: frequently to introduce something new or deserving special attention, καὶ μὴν Τάνταλον εἰσεῖδον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.582, compare 593, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 459, “Pers.” 406, etc.}}; especially in dramatic Poets to mark the entrance of a person on the stage, **here comes**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 372, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 339; also ὅδε μ. Αἵμων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 626 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; of new facts, **and besides, nay more**, καὶ μήν.. γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 982, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 369, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.15, etc.}}; in Orators to introduce new arguments, καὶ μήν.. γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.56}}; to introduce a counter-argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1185, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 452c; καὶ μὴν καί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.30, etc.: also in answers, to denote approbation or assent, ἀλλ᾽ ἢν ἐφῇς μοι.. λέξαιμ᾽ ἂν ὀρθῶς. Answ. καὶ μ. ἐφίημι}} **well**, I allow it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 556}}; μὴ νῦν διάτριβ᾽, ἀλλ᾽ ἄνυε πράττων.. Answ. καὶ μ. βαδίζω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 413 (variant), compare “Ra.” 895, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 317; so καὶ μ... γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 426e, etc.}}
**II.3** ἀλλὰ μήν, yet **truly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 233, etc.; ἀλλὰ μάν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 765; ἀλλὰ μήν.. γε [prev. author] “Ra.” 258}}; to allege something not disputed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187a: rarely separated, ἀλλ᾽ ἐστὶ μ. οἰκητός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 28}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἐγὼ μ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 401: more strongly, ὅμως μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 297d.}}
**II.4** ναὶ μήν, **above all**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 76.2.}}
**II.5** οὐ μήν, **of a truth** not, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.52, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1068, etc.: elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus οὐ μάν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.318, etc.}}; μὴ μάν (_Attic dialect_ μὴ μήν) **oh** do not, {{Refs|8.512, 15.476}}, etc.; ἀλλ᾽ οὐ μάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.41; ἀλλ᾽ οὐ μάν.. γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 153 (Lyric poetry); οὐδὲ μάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.87.}}
**II.6** ὡς μήν, = {ἦ μήν}, ὀμμνύω Δία.. ὡς μ. κρινεῖν τὰ ἀντιλεγόμενα {{Refs|“Delph.” 3(1).362 i 40.}}
**III** after interrogatives, τί μ.; **well**, what of it? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 203, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 145e, etc.}}; τί μ. οὔ **well**, why not? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 706 (Lyric poetry)}}; τῶς μ.; **well, but how**.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.28; τίνος μ. ἕνεκ; [prev. passage]; ποῦ μ.}}; to express surprise, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142a}}; ἀλλὰ πότε μή; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.23.}}
**IV** with adversative force, especially after a _negative_, so that it is equivalent to μέντοι, νῦν ἐμὲ μὲν στυγέει.., ἔσται μὰν ὅτ᾽ ἄν.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.370 - 373; οὐ μὴν ἄτιμοι.. τεθνήξομεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1279}}; ἀνάγκη μὲν καὶ ταῦτ᾽ ἐπίστασθαι.., οὐδὲν μ. κωλύει κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268e, compare “Grg.” 493c, “R.” 529e, etc.; χαλεπῶς ἔχει ὑπὸ τραυμάτων, μᾶλλον μ. αὐτὸν αἱρεῖ τὸ νόσημα [prev. author] “Tht.” 142b}}; expressed more strongly by γε μ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.18, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1067 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 587, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.7, etc.; also οὐ μ... γε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 270, “Th.” 538}}; οὐδὲ μ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 809, “Ch.” 189}}; οὐ μ. οὐδέ **nor yet indeed**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, 82, etc.; οὐ μὰν οὐδέ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.512; ἀλλ᾽ οὐ μὰν οὐδέ 23.441:—on οὐ μὴν ἀλλά, see at {ἀλλά} II.5.}}]

3376:
Included with: ** μείς**, μηνός, ὁ,
_nominative_ _singular_ μείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.117, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 557, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 11, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 6.1, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑπταμήνου” 3 (variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 409c, “Ti.” 39c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 777b23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 27, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.1.12, “SIG” 1009. 27 (Chalcedon, 3rd-2nd c.BC), 1047.46 (Amorgos, 2nd-1st c.BC), “IG” 9(1).682 (Corc., 4th c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.199, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars Prooem.”, 13.1, 2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 153, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.64, “Sammelb.” 1191}}; _Doric dialect_ μής {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 243 D 45 (Delph., 4th c.BC), “IG” 42(1).102.10 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 42.6 (3rd c.BC), “Tab.Heracl.” 1.1}}; Elean **μεύς** {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 418.15}}: _nominative_ _singular_ **μήν** {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 240 codices, “IG” 12.377.14, +others (5th c.BC), 387.32 (5th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.54, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.1, 7.4.28, LXX.1Ki.20.24, +others, “SIG” 672.85 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; both μείς and **μήν** in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 27}}: oblique cases formed from stem **μην-**, _Aeolic dialect_ **μηνν-** {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).6.39, +others (Mytil.)}}, Thess. **μεινν-** {{Refs|[prev. work]9(2).258.5 (from Cierium)}}; _dative_ _plural_ μησί [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.43, 8.51 (variant{μήνεσἱν})), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771b, μησίν “SIG” 45.18 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}]:—**month**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place], etc.; τοῦ μὲν φθίνοντος μηνός, τοῦ δ᾽ ἱσταμένοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.162, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 780, “Th.” 59; ἱσταμένου τοῦ μ. εἰνάτη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.106, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.52, etc.}}; μηνὸς τετάρτῃ φθίνοντος on the fourth day **from** the end of **the month**, Foed. cited in [same work]{{Refs|5.19}}; Μαιμακτηριῶνος δεκάτῃ ἀπιόντος, i.e. on the 21st, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.37}}; μηνῶν φθινὰς ἁμέρα the last of the **month**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 779 (Lyric poetry)}}; τελευτῶντος τοῦ μηνός at the end of **the lunar month** (when there was no moon-light), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4}}; ἐκείνου τοῦ μηνός **in the course of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.2}}; κατὰ μῆνα **monthly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1287, etc.; μισθὸν διδόναι κατὰ μ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.10}}; κατὰ μῆνα ἕκαστον, κατὰ μῆνας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 830d, 762b}}; ἐκάστου μηνός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 760d; τοῦ μηνὸς ἑκάστου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 859 (Lyric poetry)}}; τοῦ μηνός alone, **by the month**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 612, etc.; συνόδους ποιεῖσθαι δύο τοῦ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771d}}; τόκον δραχμὴν τοῦ μ. τῆς μνᾶς interest a drachma per mina **per month**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.104}}; μ. πλήρης, κοῖλος, civil **month** of 30 or 29 days respectively, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.3}}; μ. ἐμβόλιμος intercalary **month**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, “IG” 12(7).237.56 (from Arcesine), 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 13.13.}}
**2** **crescent moon**, Thphr. [prev. cited] (μείς); **the part of the month corresponding to a phase of the moon**, {{Refs|[prev. passage] (μείς and μήν)}}; **the visible part of the moon**, Chrysipp. [prev. cited] (μείς).
**3** **ornament in form of crescent moon,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.387.32 (5th c.BC, μήν).}}
**3.b** ={μηνίσκος} 1, probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1115, compare 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.5.}}
**II** as _proper name_, the god {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Lunus”, _masculine_ of Μήνη, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 8. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} stem **mēns**- 'moon', 'month', cf. Latin _mensis_, _genitive_ _plural_ **mensum**, etc.)

3377:
** μην-ύω**
(see. near the end), _Doric dialect_ **μᾱν-** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 10, “SIG” 417.7 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}: _future_ -ύσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121}}. γ᾽, etc.: _aorist_ ἐμήνῡσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 264, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.26, etc.:}}_perfect_ μεμήνῡκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 48b, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 28}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ μεμήνῡται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.10, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20}}: _aorist_ ἐμηνύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1563, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 108e}}: _future_ μηνυθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 5.15}}:—**disclose what is secret, reveal**: generally, **make known, declare**, μ. τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 254, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.4, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.23}}; τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 102, 1384, etc.}}; τί μηνύεις νέο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1029}}; τὸ πρᾶγμ᾽ οὐ μεμήνυκ᾽ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus [same place]}}; τοὺς ἑτερογνάθους μ. ἡ πέδη **indicates, betrays** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 3.5; Λυδία λίθος μανύει χρυσόν 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}} [same place]:—_passive_, κατὰ τὸ μεμηνυμένον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Acad. Ind.” p.81 M.}}
**2** with _accusative_ et _participle_, πρὸς τὸν βασιλέα μ. τινὰ ἔχοντα **show** that he has, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. γ᾽; ἐξ ἐπιβουλῆς ἀποθανόντα τινὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.5; γεγονὼς ἐμηνύθη πόλεμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Criti.” [same place]: the}}_participle_ is sometimes omitted, τόδ᾽ ἔργον.. σε μηνύει κακόν (i.e. ὄντα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1077}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, [ποιηταὶ] ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχειν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366b}}; also ἡ ἐπιστήμη μ. ὡς.. ἑπομένης τῆς ψυχῆς **gives indication** of the soul as following, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 412a.}}
**3** followed by an _interrogative_ or _conjunction_, μήνυσον αὐτοῖς τίς ἐστιν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 24d}}; ἀλλά μοι μηνύσατε εἰ.. **inform** me whether.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 206}}; μ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101b29.}}
**4** _absolutely_, ὡς ὁ ἔμπροσθεν μεμήνυκεν ἡμῖν λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277c, compare “Phlb.” 19b.}}
**II** at Athens, **inform, lay information against** another, κατά τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.20, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.23}}; τινας Docum. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.13; ταῦτα And.[prev. passage]; περὶ τῶν μυστηρίων [prev. work] 19; μ. τι κατά τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60; μ. τοῖς ἄρχουσίν τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 730d; πρός τινας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.11: _absolutely_, ὁ μηνύσας “OGI” 665.29 (Egypt, 1st c.AD): _impersonal_ in}}_passive_, μηνύεται **information is laid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.28; ὑποτοπήσαντες.. Ἱππίᾳ μεμηνῦσθαι [prev. author] 1.20, compare 6.57, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.10; ὧν πέρι ἄλλων ἐμεμήνυτο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61}}:— _passive_, also of persons, **to be informed against, denounced**, τῶν μετ᾽ αὐτοῦ μεμηνυμένων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 53, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.10; πρᾶγμα μηνυθέν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1563; μηνυθέντος τοῦ ἐπιβουλεύματος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.89}}. [ῡ always in _future_, _aorist_, and _perfect_; and in _Attic dialect_ so in _present_ and _imperfect_; ῠ in _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry in _present_ and _imperfect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 254, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” [same place], “O.” 6.52, “P.” 1.93, “I.” 8(7).60}}, B.[same place], but ῡ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 373, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.14, and later.]}}

3378:
Included with: ** μὴ οὐ** is used of an apprehended _negative_:
**I** with finite forms of the Verb, after Verbs expressing **fear** or **apprehension** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (μή 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8):}}
**I.a** mostly with _subjunctive_, δείδω μὴ οὔ τίς τοι ὑπόσχηται τόδε ἔργον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.39, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53, 57, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89d, etc.: after historical tenses, with _optative_, ἠθύμησάν τινες ἐννοούμενοι μὴ οὐκ ἔχοιεν ὁπόθεν λαμβάνοιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.3, etc.}}: with _future_ _optative_ representing _future_ _indicative_ in indirect speech, μὴ οὐκ ὀρθῶς αὐτὸ ποιήσοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 15d.}}
**I.b** with _indicative_, ὁρᾶτε μὴ οὐκ ἐμοὶ μάλιστα τῶν πολιτῶν προσήκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.103, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196c, Alcaeus2.139d.}}
**I.2** without introductory Verb, with _subjunctive_, μή νύ τοι οὐ χραίσμῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.28, compare 566, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 982}}; also to suggest hesitation, **perhaps** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (μή 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9), μὴ οὐ τοῦτο ᾖ τὸ χρηστήριον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.79, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67b, “Smp.” 194c, 214c, etc.: also with _indicative_, μὴ τοῦτο οὐ καλῶς ὡμολογήσαμεν [prev. author] “Men.” 89c.}}
**I.3** μή is sometimes doubled, irregularly, for μὴ οὐ, ἐθαύμαζε δ᾽ εἴτις.. φοβοῖτο, μὴ ὁ γενόμενος καλὸς κἀγαθὸς.. μὴ τὴν μεγίστην χάριν ἔχοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.7, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13.}}
**II** after a _negative_ expressed or implied:
**II.1** with _infinitive_,
**II.1.a** after Verbs of **hindering, denying, avoiding, needing**, when these Verbs are themselves negatived or questioned, οὐκέτι ἀνεβάλλοντο μὴ οὐ τὸ πᾶν μηχανήσασθαι **they no longer hesitated to try every expedient**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.88, compare 8.100, 119}}; τί δῆτα μέλλεις μὴ οὐ γεγωνίσκει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 627, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 540, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 320, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.2, 4.3.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 304c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.24; οὐκ ἀνατίθεμαι μὴ οὐ καλῶς λέγεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89d, compare “Phd.” 87a; πολλοῦ δέω μὴ οὐ δύο γε φεύγειν [prev. author] “Euthd.” 297b}}; τίνος ἐνδέομεν μὴ οὐ πανσυδίᾳ χωρεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 797 (_anapaest meter_)}}; after ὥστε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.57}}: with the _Article_, οὐκ ἐναντιώσομαι τὸ μὴ οὐ γεγωνεῖν πᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 787, compare 918, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 283, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1176; αὐτὴν οὐ μισοῦντ᾽ ἐκείνην τὴν πόλιν τὸ μὴ οὐ μεγάλην εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 37}}; τοῖς θεοῖς οὐδὲν ἂν ἔχοιμεν μέμψασθαι τὸ μὴ οὐχί.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.42, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 13a, etc.; compare μή 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5b.}}
**II.1.b** after Verbs and phrases signifying **impossibility, impropriety, reluctance**, when not negatived, ἄνδρα δ᾽ οὐκ ἔστι μὴ οὐ κακὸν ἔμμεναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.10; δεινὸν ἐδόκεε εἶναι μὴ οὐ λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187; ἔστι οὐδεμία μηχανὴ μὴ οὐκ ἀπολωλέναι [prev. author] 2.181, compare 3.51, 7.5; οὐδεὶς ἂν τολμήσειεν μὴ οὐ χρῆσθαι τῷ νόμῳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.87, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.60; αἰσχύνη ἦν μὴ οὐ συσπουδάζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11; αἰσχρόν ἐστι μὴ οὐκ ἄλλας πληγὰς ἐμβάλλειν τῷ υἱεῖ [prev. author] “Lac.” 6.2}}; οὐδεὶς οἷός τ᾽ ἐστὶν ἄλλως λέγων μὴ οὐ (**nemo potest non**) καταγέλαστος εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 509a}}: after an implied _negative_, μὴ οὐχὶ παντὶ τρόπῳ ἐλέγχειν μαλθακοῦ εἶναι ἀνδρός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Phd.” 85c; μόνῃ τῇ μορφῇ μὴ οὐχὶ πρόβατα εἶναι διαφερόντων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 15}}; after ὥστε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1068, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.8}}; μή and μὴ οὐκ in consecutive clauses, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 34}}: with the _Article_, οὐκ ἀνέξομαι τὸ μὴ οὐ.. τιμᾶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 914}}; οὐδείς γέ μ᾽ ἂν πείσειεν.. τὸ μὴ οὐ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 68, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.36.}}
**II.2** less frequently with _participle_ after a _negative_ expressed or implied, οὔκων δίκαιον [ἀνδριάντα] ἱστάναι.. μὴ οὐκ ὑπερβαλλόμενον τοῖσι ἔργοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.110, compare 6.9, 106; δυσάλγητος γὰρ ἂν εἴην τοιάνδε μὴ οὐ κατοικτείρων ἕδραν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 13, compare 221, “OC” 360, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.47 (uncertain reading), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 212d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.5: hence}}
**II.3** = {εἰ μή}, **except**, πόλεις.. χαλεπαὶ λαβεῖν, μὴ οὐ χρόνῳ καὶ πολιορκίᾳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.123.}}
**III** in questions expecting an affirmative answer, {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|LXX.Judg.14.3, NT.1Cor.9.4, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 404.}}

3379:
** μήποτε**
or **μή ποτε**, _Ionic dialect_ **μή κοτε** (see. below 11):
**I** as _adverb_ **never, on no account**, after ὡς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 205, “Eu.” 882}}; after εἰ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 182, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 977, “Supp.” 617}}; especially in oaths, **never**, ὀμοῦμαι, μή ποτε τῆς εὐνῆς ἐπιβήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.133, 275; ἐπεκέκλετ᾽ Ἐρινῦς, μή ποτε.. ἐφέσσεσθαι [prev. work] 455}}; in aposiopesis, ἢ μήπορ᾽ ἆρ᾽.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 97: in indirect speech, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 86.}}
**I.2** in prohibition or warning, with _aorist_ _subjunctive_, μή ποτε καὶ σὺ.. ὀλέσσῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.81, etc.: with}}_infinitive_ for _imperative_, {{Refs|11.441.}}
**I.3** in later Gr., **perhaps**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1172a33, LXX.Gen.24.5, Aristeas 15, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.13, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.80, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.106d, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 18.4.}}
**II** as _conjunction_, **lest ever**, αἰσχυνόμενοι φάτιν ἀνδρῶν.., μή ποτέ τις εἴπῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.324, +others; οὐδαμὰ ἐλπίσας μή κοτε ἄρα.. ἐλάσῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, compare 8.53.}}

3380:
** μήπω**
or **μή πω**,
**I** as _adverb_ **not yet**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.431, etc.}}; ἀλλὰ μήπω τοῦτο (i.e. σκοπεῖτε) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.90: in expostulation, μή τώ τι μεθίετε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.234, 17.422, etc.}}; μήπω γε **nay, not yet**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 631: followed by πρίν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.134, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 961, 1409 (_anapaest meter_): with _optative_ precantis, μήπω μανείη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1278; μήπω νοῦ τοσόνδ᾽ εἴην κενή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 403.}}
**II** as _conjunction_, **lest yet**, κελόμην ἐπιβαινέμεν.., μή πώ τις.. λάθηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.102 (variant).}}

3381:
** μήπω**
or **μή πω**,
**I** as _adverb_ **not yet**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.431, etc.}}; ἀλλὰ μήπω τοῦτο (i.e. σκοπεῖτε) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.90: in expostulation, μή τώ τι μεθίετε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.234, 17.422, etc.}}; μήπω γε **nay, not yet**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 631: followed by πρίν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.134, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 961, 1409 (_anapaest meter_): with _optative_ precantis, μήπω μανείη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1278; μήπω νοῦ τοσόνδ᾽ εἴην κενή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 403.}}
**II** as _conjunction_, **lest yet**, κελόμην ἐπιβαινέμεν.., μή πώ τις.. λάθηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.102 (variant).}}

3382:
** μηρός**, ὁ,
**thigh**, φάσγανον ὀξὺ ἐρυσσάμενος παρὰ μηροῦ drawing his sword from **his thigh**, where it hung, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.190, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.231, +others}}; μηρὼ πληξάμενος, in sign of vehement agitation, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.125; ἐπαίσατο τὸν μηρόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.6; τύπτειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.27.11; τὸν μ. ἀλοῆσαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 2}}; ἐπὶ μηρόν τινος **beside** it, {{LXX+7th c.BC+|LXX.2Ki.16.14: in _plural_, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 11.6, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 135, 136.}}
**2** **thigh-bone**, κατ᾽ ἰσχίον, ἔνθα τε μηρὸς ἰσχίῳ ἐνστρέφεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.305, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 57, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).472}}; especially of **thigh-bones with flesh** offered in sacrifice, μηροὺς ἐξέταμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.460, +others (compare μηρία); καταρρυεῖς μ. καλυπτῆς ἐξέκειντο πιμελῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1011; θεοῖσι μηρὸν θύετε 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 130; τίθεσο τὼ μηρὼ λαβών 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1039.}}
**3** generally, **leg-bones**, κάμηλος ἐν τοῖσι ὀπισθίοισι σκέλεσι ἔχει τέσσερας μηροὺς καὶ γούνατα τέσσερα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.103}}. (Cf. OIr. **mīr** 'piece', Latin _membrum_, from **mēmsro**-, Sanskrit _māmsám_ 'meat'.)

3383:
** μήτε**,
**and not**, mostly doubled, μήτε.. μήτε.. **neither.. nor**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; μήτε.., μήτ᾽ οὖν.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 358 (_anapaest meter_), 472 (Lyric poetry); μηδέ τῳ ἐκφάσθαι, μήτ᾽ ἀνδρῶν μήτε γυναικῶν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.308}}; μήτε.., μηδέ, see at {μηδέ}; μήτε..,{τε}.. **both not.., and**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.230, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 454, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.72}}; also μήτε.., δέ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 421, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 627e}}; μή.., μήτε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 496 codices, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 978 codices}}
**2** μήτε is perhaps sometimes omitted in the former of two clauses, ἑκόντα μήτ᾽ ἄκοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 771 (variant), compare “Ant.” 267.}}

3384:
** μήτηρ**,
_Doric dialect_ **μάτηρ**, ἡ: though _paroxytone_ in _nominative_, it follows πατήρ in the accent of the _indirect_ cases, _genitive_ μητερος _contraction_ μητρός, _dative_ μητέρι, μητρί, both forms being found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, but the longer forms rarely in Trag. exc. Lyric poetry, as ματέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 539; ματέρι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1481}}; μητέρος in iambics, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 843, “Or.” 580, “Rh.” 393: _accusative_ always μητέρα, μητέρας: _vocative_ μῆτερ}}:—**mother**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.351, etc.}}; of animals, **dam**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|17.4, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.414}}; of a **mother-bird**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.313}}; of **queen** bees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 553a29, etc.}}; ἀπὸ ματρὸς φίλας, ἐκ ματρός, from one's **mother's womb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.114, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 422 (Lyric poetry)}}: in _plural_, **mother and grandmother**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 9}}; as an address to elderly women, ὦ μῆτερ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.37, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.60, etc.: in titles, μ. πατρίδος}}, = **Mater Patriae**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.2}}; μ. τῶν ἀηττήτων στρατοπέδων, = **Mater invictorum castrorum**, of Julia Domna, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 362 xi 16 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** of lands, μ. μήλων, θηρῶν, **mother** of flocks, of game, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.696, 8.47, etc.}}; frequently of Earth, γῆ πάντων μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 563; πὰρ μέσον ὀμφαλὸν εὐδένδροιο.. ματέρος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.74; γῆ μήτηρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 16, etc.; ὦ γαῖα μῆτερ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 601}}; ἡ Μήτηρ, ={Δημήτηρ}, τῇ Μητρὶ καὶ τῇ Κούρῃ ὁρτὴν ἄγουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65}}; also of Rhea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.78; ὦ Πὰν.., Ματρὸς μεγάλας ὀπαδέ [prev. author] “fragment” 95, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1355 (Lyric poetry); μ. ὀρεία 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 746 (Lyric poetry); Γαλλαὶ μητρὸς ὀρείης φιλόθυρσοι δρομάδες “Lyrica Adespota” 121; M. θεῶν “SIG” 1044.8 (Halic., 4th c.BC)}}; as title of Isis, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.2 (compare p.xi) (3rd c.BC).}}
**3** frequently of one's native land, μᾶτερ ἐμά, Θήβα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.1, compare “P.” 8.98, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 416, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.25}}; and so, like{μητρόπολις}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.20, compare 6.100; ἡ Σκῦρος ἀνδρῶν ἀλκίμων μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 326.}}
**II** _poetry_, the **origin** or **source** of events, μ. ἀέθλων, of Olympia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.1; πειθαρχία γὰρ τῆς εὐπραξίας μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 225; ἡ γνώμη κακῶν μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1361}}; of night, as **the mother** of day, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 265}}; the grape of wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 614, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 757; ματέρ᾽ οἰνάνθας ὀπώραν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.6}}; Aphrodite of the Loves, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 122.4}}; φάτις ὦ μᾶτερ αἰσχύνας ἐμᾶς, of a rumour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 174 (Lyric poetry)}}: also in Prose, γεωργίαν τῶν ἄλλων τεχνῶν μητέρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.17}}; πολιτειῶν μητέρες δύο (i.e. μοναρχία and δημοκρατία) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 693d}}. (Cf. Latin _mater_, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **módor**, etc.)

3385:
Included with: ** μήτῐς**
or **μή τις**, ὁ, ἡ, _neuter_ μήτῐ, _genitive_ μήτῐνος: (τὶς) old Gr. and Cretan dialect for μηδείς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.272, +others, “Schwyzer” 175 (from Gortyn), “Leg.Gort.” 5.13, +others}}; Cretan dialect _dative_ _singular_ μηδιμί {{Refs|“IG” 22.1130.4:}} —hence
**1** μήτι or μή τι, _adverb_, with _imperative_, and _infinitive_ used imperatively, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.550, 5.130, etc.}}: with _optative_ to express a wish, ὄλοιντο μή τι πάντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 383.}}
**2** after Verbs of fear or doubt, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.470, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.67, etc.}}
**3** in direct questions, μή τί σοι δοκῶ ταρβεῖ; **do I**.. ? (i.e. I do not), {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 959, compare 249, LXX.Gen.20.9, NT.Mark.4.21.}}
**4** μή τί γε **let alone, much less**, οὐδὲ στρατιώτης οὗτός γε οὐδενός ἐστιν ἄξιος, μή τί γε τῶν ἄλλων ἡγεμών {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.148, compare 19.137}}; later, **not to mention**, οὐκ οἴδατε ὅτι ἀγγέλους κρινοῦμε; μήτι γε βιωτικά {{NT|NT.1Cor.6.3}}: with a word between, ὡς.. δώσοντι δίκην, μή τι ποιήσαντί γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.27; also μή τι δή 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.8.6; μή τί γε δή, οὐκ ἔνι οὐδὲ τοῖς φίλοις.., μή τί γε δὴ τοῖς θεοῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.23; also μητιγοῦν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.9; μή τι δή γε 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.261 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others}}

