3388:
** μήτρα**,
**A**_Ionic dialect_ **μήτρη**, ἡ, (μήτηρ) **womb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.24, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.108 (uncertain reading), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91d, etc.}}: also in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 47, “Vict.” 1.30, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} [same place]: **the cervix including the orifice of the womb**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 510b14.}}
**A.2** a swine's **matrix**, reckoned a great dainty, μήτρας τόμοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 1.14; μήτραν.. πωλοῦσιν, ἥδιστον κρέας 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 220; ὑπὲρ μήτρας.. ἀποθανεῖν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 193, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.733e, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.96f.}}
**A.3** _metaphorically_, **source, origin**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.46; μῆτραι τῆς ψυχῆς 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.441.}}
**A.II** **core, heart-wood** of trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.6.1.}}
**A.II.b** **diseased condition** of the wood, 'soft-wood', {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.7.3.}}
**A.III** **queen-wasp**, opposed to ἐργάται, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 627b32, +others}}
**A.IV** μ. χελωνίων, χελωνίοις, **bolts** for locks, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1028.20, 26 (2nd c.AD)}}; μ. θύρας, = **repagulum,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}

3390:
** μητρό-πολις**,
_Doric dialect_ **ματρό-**, _poetry_ **μητρόπτολις**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“epigram Gr.” 537.4 (from Tomi), 842a1 (from Cyrene), “Syria” 7.209 (from Damascus), 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 13.166}}: εως, ἡ:— **mother-state**, as related to her colonies, of Athens in relation to the Ionians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.51, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.82}}; of Doris in relation to the Peloponn. Dorians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.31, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107, 3.92}}; of Meroe in relation to the Ethiopians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29}}; of Thera, μεγαλᾶν πολίων μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.20; μ. Λοκρῶν Ὀπόεις 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 93}}; of the Attic Salamis, as the μ. of the Cyprian, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 895 (Lyric poetry)}}; of Corinth, as the μ. of Corcyra, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.24}}; of Rome, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.296.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἐστὶ μ. τοῦ ψυχροῦ [ὁ ἐγκέφαλος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ σαρκῶν” 4; ἡ ἱστορία μ. τῆς φιλοσοφίας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.2, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.199}}; γεωμετρία ἀρχὴ καὶ μ. τῶν ἄλλων (i.e. μαθημάτων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.718e.}}
**II** **one's mother-city, mothercountry, home**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.8, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 707 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1122 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** ἁ σὰ ματρόπολις thy **mother's city**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 59.}}
**III** **capital city**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.3, 5.4.15}}; ἡ μ. τῆς Ἀσίας, of Ephesus, {{Refs|“OGI” 496.6, “IG” 3.485; ἡ μ. τῆς Ἰωνίας, of Miletus, [prev. work]480.}}
**III.b** in Egypt, **chief town** of a νομός, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 48.16 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 326 ii 10 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

3391:
Related to: ** εἷς**, μίᾰ, ἕν
(μίη only in late _Ionic dialect_ Prose): _genitive_ ἑνός, μιᾶς, ἑνός:—_Epic dialect_ **ἕεις** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 145, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.341 (5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus), conjecture in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.603}}:—_Doric dialect_ **ἧς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 12, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.136}}:—_Epic dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_, and _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ ἴᾰ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.354, probably in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.37; _accusative_ ἴαν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33.6 (probably), 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 69.1 (compare μηδεΐα), 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.56, “IG” 9(2).517.22(Thess.); _genitive_ ἰῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.173, 24.496; _dative_ ἰῇ 9.319, 11.174, etc.}}: _neuter_ _dative_ (ἰῷ κίον ἤματι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6.422. (In Comedy texts οὐδὲ (μηδὲ) εἷς, οὐδὲ (μηδὲ) ἕν, occur, mostly at the end of an iambic trimeter, without elision, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 302, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 927, “Pl.” 37, 138, +others)}} (Orig. **ἕνς**, assim. ἔν (δ) probably in {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 9.50, from *εμς}} Indo-European **sem**-(compare ὁμός); μία from **sm**-ί; ἴα is not related to μία, but probably to _pronominal_ stem **i**-(Latin _is_), compareἰός.)
**1** as a Numeral, εἷς κοίρανος ἔστω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.204, etc.}}; strengthened, εἷς οἶος, μία οἴη, a single
**A** **one, one** alone,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.397, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.65; μία μούνη 23.227; εἷς μοῦνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1053, etc.; εἷς καὶ μόνος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.74; εἷς ὤν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 247, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 894, etc.; opposed to πολύς, μία τὰς πολλὰς ψυχὰς ὀλέσασα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1456, compare 1465, “Ch.” 299, etc.}}
**b** emphatically with a _superlative_, εἷς οἰωνὸς ἄριστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.243, etc.: frequently in Trag., εἷς ἀνὴρ πλεῖστον..πόνον παρασχών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 327; πλείστας ἀνὴρ εἷς.. ἔγημε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 460; κάλλιστ᾽ ἀνὴρ εἷς [prev. author] “OT” 1380; ἕνακριθέντ᾽ ἄριστον [prev. author] “Ph.” 1344}}; also in Prose, ἐπὶ πλεῖστον δὴ χλιδῆς εἷς ἀνὴρ ἀπίκετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; Μυτιληναίους μάλιστα δὴ μίαν πόλιν [prev. author] 3.39; πάντων εἷς ἀνὴρ μεγίστων αἴτιος κακῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.143}}: without a _superlative_, Ἐτεοκλέης ἂν εἷς πολὺς κατὰ πτόλιν ὑμνοῖθ᾽ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 6.}}
**c** in oppos., made emphatic by the _Article_, ὁ εἷς, ἡ μία, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.272, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.110, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 48a; τοῦ ἑνὸς οἱ δύο ἀγαθοὶ βελτίους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b13, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.22.}}
**d** with a _negative_, εἷς οὐδείς no **single man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἓν οὐδὲ ἓν ἴαμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}; οὐκ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἑνί γε χωρίῳ in no other **single** country, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.80}}; οὐχ εἷς, i.e. more than **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 103, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 96}}; εἷς οὐ.., εἷς μή.., emphatic for οὐδείς, μηδείς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 549, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.12}}; more emphatic, οὐδὲ εἷς, μηδὲ εἷς, see at {οὐδείς},{μηδείς}.
**e** εἷς ἕκαστος each **one,** each **by himself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 332c, etc.}}; αἴσθησις μία ἑνός (i.e. γένους) **one** of **each,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1003b19: _plural_, ἑκάτεροι ἕνες “POxy.” 276.8 (1st c.AD).}}
**f** with κατά, καθ᾽ ἓν ἕκαστον each **singly,** piece by piece, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν **one** by **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217a, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν, τό, **list,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 20.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν᾽ ἡμῶν ἕκαστον ἀποστερεῖν to deprive each of us **singly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.142, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 164, 186}}; εἷς κατὰ εἷς **one** by **one,** {{NT|NT.Mark.14.19}}; but καθ᾽ ἓν γίγνεσθαι, εἶναι, to be **united,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.16.}}
**g** with other Preps., ἓν ἀνθ᾽ ἑνός **above all,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331b, “Phlb.” 63c}}; but μίαν ἀντὶ μιᾶς **alternately,** {{Refs|PStrassb. 25.13}}, etc.; ἐπὶ μίαν ἑκάστην ῥάβδον τιθέντες θεσπίζουσι **one by one, separately,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67; ἐπὶ ἑνός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157a; ἓν ἐφ᾽ ἑνί [prev. author] “Sph.” 229b, “Lg.” 758b: ἓν πρὸς ἕν, with or without συμβάλλειν, in comparisons, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647b; πρὸς ἕν᾽ εἷς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.131: παρ᾽ ἕνα}}**alternately,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 12; εἰς ἓν συναγαγεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1640; ἰσχὺς τοσαύτη εἰς ἓν ξυστᾶσα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.85; εἰς ἓν μοίρας συνέκυρσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1172; ἐς μίαν βουλεύειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.379; in full, ἐς μίαν βουλήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111; εἰς μίαν νοεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.9; ἓν ἐξ ἑνὸς ἐπισεσώρευκεν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 54}}; ἀπὸ μιᾶς with **one accord,** {{NT|NT.Luke.14.18; ὑφ᾽ ἕν}}**at once,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.124; also ὑφ᾽ ἓν θέσθαι τὸ ὂν τῷ μὴ ὄντι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.1}}; compare ὑφέν.
**h** in _compound_ numerals, as an ordinal, τῷ ἑνὶ καὶ τριηκοστῷ [ἔτει] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.89, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.109, etc.}}; so in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 2.660.30, +others,: later εἷς}} alone,=**first,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.1.5; μιᾷ τοῦ μηνός [prev. work] 8.13.}}
**i** μίαν μίαν, ={κατὰ μίαν}, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 201; εἷς εἷς LXX.1Ch.24.6.}}
**i.2** **one,** i.e. **the same,** τώ μοι μία γείνατο μήτηρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.238, etc.}}; εἷς καὶ ὁ αὐτός **one** and the same, ἓν καὶ ταὐτὸν ἀριθμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1039a28, etc.; ὑπὸ μίαν καὶ τὰν αὐτὰν ἀρχάν Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.121; ταὐτὸν καὶ ἕν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 201b3; so ἓν καὶ ὅμοιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271a; εἷς καὶ κοινός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.699f: with _dative_, ἐμοὶ μιᾶς ἐγένετ᾽ ἐκ ματρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 156; ἐκ μιᾶς οἰνοχόης Ἐπικούρῳ πεπωκότες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1089a.}}
**i.2.b** **possessing unity,** ἧττον μία ἡ μίμησις ἡ τῶν ἐποποιῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1462b3; λίαν ἓν ποιεῖν τὴν πόλιν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1263b7; τὰ κυρίως ἕνα 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 437.}}
**i.3** **one,** opposed to **another,** ἓν μὲν..ἓν δὲ.. [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1139a6, “Pol.” 1285b38, etc.; ὁ μὲν..εἷς δὲ..εἷς δ᾽ αὖ.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.421 {εἷσα}, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d; εἷς μὲν..ἕτερος δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.23.}}]
**i.4** indefinitely, εἷς τις some **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 118, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471e, etc.; ἐξ ἑνός γέ του τρόπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34; rarely τις εἷς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 269, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 145d}}; εἷς γάρ τις ἦν ἕκαστος οὑξειργασμένος each **single** one was suspected, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 262; εἷς ὁστισοῦν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1325b28}}; εἷς ὁ πρῶτος, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **der erste beste,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.9, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 61}}: alone, like our _indefinite_ _Article_, **a, an,** Κάδμου θυγατέρων μιᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 917}}; εἷς κάπηλος, στρατηγός, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1292, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.50; εἷς Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.87, cf. LXX.Gen.21.15, NT.Matt.21.19, etc.}}; εἷς ἀπό.. {{LXX|LXX.Lev.6.3(22).}}
**i.5** οὐχ εἷς **many,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 103, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 33}}; οὐχ εἷς οὐδὲ δύο not **one** or two only, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.12}}; οὐ μίαν οὐδὲ δύο not **once** nor twice, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.4Ki.6.10; ἓν ἢ καὶ δύο ληφθὲν μαρτύριον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.38.10; εἷς ἢ δεύτερος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.190d: proverbial, εἷς ἀνὴρ οὐδεὶς ἀνήρ}} **one** man's no man, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 48.10.}}
**i.6** in Mathematics texts, τὸ ἕν **unity,** opposed to πλῆθος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus “fragment” 5, etc.: _plural_, ἕνα}}**units,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1056b21; ὁ ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἕνα πλείω [prev. author] “Ph.” 207b7; τῶν προτέρων ἑνῶν 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 460.}}
**i.7** Philos., ἕν, τό, **unity,** the **One,** ἐκ πάντων ἓν καὶ ἐξ ἑνὸς πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 10, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.1, etc.}}: later _indeclinable_, ἓν εἶναι τοῦ ἓν παρουσίᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.14, compare 5.5.5.}}

3392:
** μῐαίνω**,
_future_ μιᾰνῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.11}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ μιᾰνεῖ {{LXX|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.158 (from Cyrene), LXX.Isa.30.22, +others}}: _aorist_ ἐμίηνα {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.141, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 14, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.104, ἐμίᾱνα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.16, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 104, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1000, “IA” [1595], LXX.Gen.34.5, +others}}; _participle_ μιάνας {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 32.3}}: _perfect_ μεμίαγκα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 21}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐμιήνατο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 45.288}}:—_passive_, _future_ μιανθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621c:}}_aorist_ ἐμιάνθην, _Epic dialect_ μιάνθην (see. below): _perfect_ μεμίασμαι{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|(see. below), μεμίαμμαι LXX.Num.5.14, +others, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 51.22}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ μεμίανται {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.16}} (but _3rd.pers._ _plural_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 121.2)}}; _infinitive_ μεμιάνθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 36.13, but μεμιάσθαι 4th c.AD(?): Horapollo 1.44: Cyrenaic}}_aorist 2_ _passive_ ἐμίᾱν in _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ μιᾷ {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.160, +others:}}_future_ _passive_ μιᾱσέω in _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _indicative_ μιᾱσεῖ{{Refs|[prev. work]}} 164:—**stain, dye**, ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε τίς τ᾽ ἐλέφαντα γυνὴ φοίνικι μιήνῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.141; ὥσπερ ἔβενος περίδρομος ἐλέφαντα τὸν βραχίονα μιαίνων 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.15.}}
**2** **stain, sully**, μιάνθησαν ἔθειραι αἵματι καὶ κονίῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.795, compare 23.732; μιάνθην αἵματι μηροί 4.146; αἵματι πεσεῖ μιανθείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1374, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 209 (Lyric poetry); τοὺς θεῶν βωμοὺς αἵματι μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 782c; μ. βωμὸν εὐγενεῖ φόνῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “IA” [same place]; βορβόρῳ.. ὕδωρ μιαίνων λαμπρόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 695.}}
**3** frequently of moral pollution, **taint, defile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} [same place], etc.; κλέος Sol.[same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hel.” [same place]; τὸ καλῶς πεφυκὸς οὐδεὶς ἂν μιάνειεν λόγος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 104; ἑνὶ πόνῳ πολλὰ καὶ λαμπρὰ ἔργα μιῆναι 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.104; εὔφημον ἦμαρ κακαγγέλῳ γλώσσῃ μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 637; μιαίνων εὐσέβειαν Ἄρης [prev. author] “Th.” 344 (Lyric poetry); τὴν δίκην [prev. author] “Ag.” 1669}}; τὰ ἱερά, τὸ θεῖον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 868b, “Ti.” 69d; οἶκον θόρυβος μιαίνει 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.12; θεοὺς μιαίνειν οὔ τις ἀνθρώπων σθένει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1044; τὴν ἁγνείαν τῶν θεῶν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, compare 2.1.10}}; **dishonour** a woman, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.34.5, +others}}:— _passive_, **incur defilement**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 366, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 75, “Berl.Sitzb.” [same places], etc.; τὴν ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621c; τῆς ἄλλης [γῆς] αὐτῷ μεμιασμένης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.102; ἡ ψυχὴ μεμιασμένη καὶ ἀκάθαρτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81b; μιανθέντες τῷ τῆς ἀσεβείας μολυσμῷ Aristeas 66}}; of ritual defilement in funeral rites, {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).593.25 (from Iulis).}}

3393:
** μί-ασμα** [ῐ], ατος, τό,
(μιαίνω) **stain, defilement**, especially by murder or other crime, **taint of guilt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 169 (Lyric poetry), 281, etc.; οὐκ ἔστι γῆρας τοῦδε τοῦ μ. [prev. author] “Th.” 682; μ. φεύγων αἵματος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 35; μ. τῶν φυτευσάντων λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1012; οὐ προσῆκον μίασμα εἰς οἴκους εἰσάγεσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.3; μ. τινὸς ἐπεξέρχεσθαι [prev. author] 4.3.6; τὸ μ. εἰς αὑτὸν δέχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 871b: in _plural_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1420, “Ch.” 1017; αἱμάτων μιάσμασι χρανθεῖσα γαῖα [prev. author] “Supp.” 265, etc.}}
**II** **that which defiles, pollution**, of persons, χώρας μ. καὶ θεῶν ἐγχωρίων {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1645}}; πατροκτόνον μ. καὶ θεῶν στύγος, of Clytaemnestra, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 1028: μ. χώρας ἐλαύνειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 97; ὡς μ. τοῦδ᾽ ἡμὶν ὄντος [prev. work] 241}}: in Prose more generally, πνεῦμα μεμιασμένον νοσηροῖσι μιάσμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 5.}}

3394:
** μι-ασμός**, ὁ,
={μίανσις}, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.14.26, NT.2Pet.2.10, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.393c, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.20 (_plural_), 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.31 (_plural_).}}
**2** **scandal, crime**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ ψυχῆς παθῶν κτλ. - Galeni Scripta Minora” 4 (_plural_).}}

3395:
** μίγμα**, ατος, τό,
(μείγνυμι) **mixture, compound**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus and 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 187a33, compare “Metaphorical” 1012a28.}}
**2** μίγματα, τά, of drugs, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.80a, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.44, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.655; μ. σμύρνης καὶ ἀλόης NT.John.19.39}}; of pigments, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isaeo” 4, “Comp.” 21}}; of condiments, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.997a}}; of amalgams, Zos.Alch.p.197 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus (In codices sometimes μῖγμα}}, for which **μεῖγμα** (formed like{χεῦμα}) should perhaps be restored in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus and 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, but μίγμα (formed like{χύμα}) may be retained in later texts.)}}]

3396:
** μείγνυμι**
or **μίγνυμι**, μ<ε>ίγνυσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 691e}}; _imperative_ μ<ε>ίγνυ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 63e}}:—also μ<ε>ιγνύω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.60, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 627a23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 53, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἐμ<ε>ίγνυν, _plural_ ἐμ<ε>ίγνυσαν (συν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.46}}; _poetry_ μ<ε>ίγνυον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.21}}: _future_ μ<ε>ίξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.221 (μετα-), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1047 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 64b}}: _aorist_ ἔμ<ε>ιξα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 86, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).25, etc.}}; _infinitive_ μ<ε>ῖξαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.510:}}_perfect_ μέμῐχα (συμ-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.10.1, 38.13.5:}}_pluperfect_ ἐμεμίχειν [ῐ] (συν-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 47.45}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, **μ<ε>ίγνυμαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113c}}: _imperfect_ ἐμ<ε>ίγνυντο (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.1}}: _future_ μ<ε>ίξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.136, 24.314}}, μεμ<ε>ίξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 179}}, μ<ε>ιχθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.166 (ἀνα-), 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 13}}; also μῐγήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.365:}}_aorist 1_ ἐμίχθη{{Refs|[prev. work] 457}}, ἐμ<ε>ίχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 295, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.181, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 70.5, etc.}}; _infinitive_ μιχθήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.438; but in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and _Attic dialect_ more commonly}}_aorist 2_ ἐμίγην [ῐ]; _Epic dialect_ μίγην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.143}}; _infinitive_ μιγήμεναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|15.409, μιγῆν 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 12.5; both forms in Trag., μιχθῆναι A.[same place], +others (see. below), μιγῆναι [prev. author] “Pr.” 738}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _passive_ ἔμικτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.433, μίκτο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.354, 16.813, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1223}}; _participle_ μίγμενος in _transitive_ sense, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 574}}: _aorist_ _middle_ ἐμ<ε>ιξάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.22.3}}: _perfect_ μέμιγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.424, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἀνα-μεμ<ε>ίχαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.146}}: _pluperfect_ ἐμέμικτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.438.}}—For the _present_ and _imperfect_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus always use μίσγω}}, which occurs once in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 271 (_anapaest meter_)}}, never in Comedy texts, sometimes in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.104 (προς-), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 43}}; _participle_ μίσγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41d}}; also _imperfect_ ἔμισγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22 (προς-), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ti.” [same place]}}; also in later Prose, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.8.9 (προς-), 18.32.2, 31.17.5 (συμ-), “PTeb.” 12.7, 18, 26.3 (συμ-, 2nd c.BC), etc.: _Epic dialect_}}_imperfect_ ἐμισγέσκοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.7}}. (In codices usually **μι-** in all tenses and derivations; in Inscrr. and Papyrus frequently **μει-**, e.g. μειγνύς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.13 K., μειγνύμενος 2nd c.BC: Limenius Lyricus 14 (2nd c.BC), ὀν-εμείχνυτο 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20c. 2 (= pp.21,78 Lobel, ὀνεμίγνυτο [prev. work] 20b. 4):}}_future_ _infinitive_ συν-μείσχι[ν] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 12.920 (6th c.BC)}}: _aorist_ συνέμειξα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.64 (3rd c.BC)}}; _infinitive_ συμ-μεῖξαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 29.11 (3rd c.BC)}}: _perfect_ _passive_ μέμειγμαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.34 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}: _aorist_ _passive_ ἐμείχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 99.5 (Papyrus of 2nd c.BC), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Antiop.” iv B 45 (Papyrus of 3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.12; similarly μεῖξις [prev. author] “Mus.” p.65 K.}}; σύμ-μεικτος frequently in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1388.63 (4th c.BC), +others}}; μεικτός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 292.25, +others (3rd c.BC)}}: **μι-** is found in **συνανα-μιγνύμενα**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.9, μιγνύωσι [prev. author] “Ir.” p.41}} K.: _aorist_ _infinitive_ συμ-μῖξαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 568.6 (Halasarna, late 3rd c.BC):}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ μεμιγμένος Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 198.12 (3rd c.BC)}}: _aorist_ _participle_ _passive_ μιχθείς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Paean Erythraeus [4th c.BC]” 5 (4th c.BC and 2nd c.AD, see l. μει- 2nd c.AD)}}; similarly σύμ-μικτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“AJA” 31.350 (vase, 5th c.BC)}}; the oldest forms were probably μίσγω μείξω ἔμειξα μέμιγμαι ἐμίχθην (μίκτο) μεῖγμα μίξις μικτός (compare the forms of τεύχω, φεύγω, etc.); the μει- forms already in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC had encroached, and after 2nd c.BC were frequently written}} μι- (i. e. μῑ-)):—**mix**, strictly of liquids, οἶνον ἐνὶ κρητῆρσι καὶ ὕδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.110, etc.}}; also of a solid and liquid, θρόμβῳ δ᾽ ἔμ<ε>ιξεν αἵματος φίλον γάλα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 546}}; of two solids, ἅλεσσι μεμιγμένον εἶδαρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.123; also μ. ἐκ γῆς καὶ πυρός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320d; μιγνὺς [ταῦτα] μετὰ τῆς οὐσιας [prev. author] “Ti.” 35b}}:—_middle_ for _active_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.44 (from Ion), 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 603}}:—_passive_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II** generally, **join, bring together**, in various ways:
**II.1** in hostile sense, μ<ε>ῖξαι χεῖράς τε μένος τε **join** battle hand to hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.510; μίξαντες.. Ἄρευα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 31; Κόλχοισι βίαν μ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.213}}; χερσὶν ἐναντία χεῖρας ἔμ<ε>ιξεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.78}}; Ἄρη μ<ε>ίξουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1047 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, μ<ε>ιγνυμένου πολέμου {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.11.}}
**II.1.b** in good sense, ἀλώπηξ καἰετὸς ξυνωνίην ἔμ<ε>ιξαν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 86.}}
**II.2** **bring into connexion with, make acquainted with**, ἄνδρας.. μισγέμεναι κακότητι καὶ ἄλγεσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.203}}; Καδμεῖοί νιν.. ἄνθεσι μ<ε>ίγνυον **covered** him **with** flowers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.21}}; reversely, ᾧ πότμον.. Ἄρης ἔμ<ε>ιξεν **upon** whom {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} **brought** death, {{Refs|[prev. author]}}**l.**7(6).25.
**B** _passive_, with _future_ _middle_ μείξομαι (see. entry [near the start]):—**to be mixed up with, mingled among**, προμάχοισιν ἐμίχθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.134, etc.; ἐνὶ προμάχοισι μιγέντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.379; [σῆμα] οὔ τι μεμιγμένον ἐστὶν ὁμίλῳ 8.196}}; ἐώλπει μ<ε>ίξεσθαι ξενίῃ hoped to **hold intercourse** in guest-friendship,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.314; Τρώεσσιν ἐν ἀγρομένοισιν ἔμιχθεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.209, compare 10.180}}; ἐν ταῖς κακαῖσιν ἁγαθαὶ μεμ<ε>ιγμέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 399}}; **hold intercourse with, live with**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.247, etc.; ἐμίσγετο δαίμονι δαίμων 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 59.1; αἷς οὐ μίγνυται θεῶν τις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 69}}: _absolutely_, **hold intercourse**, θάμ᾽ ἐνθάδ᾽ ἐόντες ἐμισγόμεθ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.178.}}
**B.b** **to be mixed** or **compounded**, μεμ<ε>ιγμένον μέλι σὺν γάλακτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.77; Κύπριδος ἐλπὶς.. μειγνυμένα Διονυσίοισι δώροις 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “Scolia” 1361 “fragment” 1.9}}; σύλλογος νέων καὶ πρεσβυτέρων μεμ<ε>ιγμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 951d, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 997; μεμιγμένην πολιτείαν ἐκ κακοῦ τε καὶ ἀγαθοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 548c; ἔκ τε ταὐτοῦ καὶ θατέρου καὶ τῆς οὐσίας μ. [prev. author] “Ti.” 35b.}}
**B.2** **to be brought into contact with**, κάρη κονίῃσιν ἐμίχθη his head **wasrolled** in the dust, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.457, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.329; ὅτ᾽ ἐν κονίῃσι μιγείης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.55}}; οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἔασε [ἔγχος].. μιχθήμεναι ἔγκασι φωτός she let not the spear **reach** them,{{Refs|11.438; κλισίῃσι μιγήμεναι 15.409}}; ἐς Ἀχαιοὺς μίσγετο **went to join** them, {{Refs|18.216}}; ἔσω μίσγεσθαι **to come among us** in the house, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.49}}; μίσγεσθαι ὑπὲρ ποταμοῖο **to join** the rest across the river, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.73: frequently in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus in various senses, with _dative_ (with or without ἐν)}}, **come to**, ἔν τ᾽ Ὠκεανοῦ πελάγεσσι μίγεν {{Refs|“P.” 4.251; Λακεδαιμονίων μιχθέντες ἀνδρῶν ἤθεσιν [prev. work] 257}}; ἐν αἱμακουρίαις μέμικται **is present at** that feast, {{Refs|“O.” 1.91}}; φύλλοις ἐλαιᾶν μιχθέντα, στεφάνοις ἔμιχθεν (_3rd.pers._ _plural_), **come to**, i.e. **win**, the crown of victory, {{Refs|“N.” 1.18, 2.22; μ. εὐλογίαις “I.” 3.3; μ. ἐν τιμαῖς [prev. work] 2.29}}; μ. θάμβει **to be affected by** amazement, {{Refs|“N.” 1.56}}; also βροτοὶ ξὺν κακοῖς μεμ<ε>ιγμένοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1485.}}
**B.3** in hostile sense, **mix in fight**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.456, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.317}}; ἐν δαΐ, ἐν παλάμῃσι μ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.286, 21.469.}}
**B.4** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} most frequently of the sexes, **have intercourse with**, both of the man and the woman, sometimes _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.275, etc.: more frequently μιγῆναί τινι, of the man, 21.143}}, etc.; of the woman, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.73; ἄρσενι θῆλυ μιγῆν 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 12.5, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.14, +others}}; but in Trag. only of the man, as μητρὶ μ<ε>ιχθῆναι, μιγῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 791, 995}}; but in Comedy texts μ<ε>ιγνυμένας τοῖσιν ἀδελφοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1081 (_anapaest meter_): in Prose}}_present_ μίσγεσθαι in this sense, of the man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.64, etc.}}; of the woman, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.5, 199, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.445}}; in full, φιλότητί τινι μιγῆναι, of the man, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.165}}; of the woman,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 161, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 927, 970, etc.}}; ἐμισγέσθην φ., of the two, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.295}}; ἐν φιλότητι μίσγεσθαι (with or without τινι), of the man, {{Refs|2.232, 24.131}}; of the woman, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 33.5}}; Διὸς φιλότητι μιγῆναι, Διὸς ἐν φ. μ., of the woman, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 920, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 4}}; σῇ φ. μ., of the man, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Venerem” 150}}; εὐνῇ μ., of the man, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.433}}; φιλότητι καὶ εὐνῇ, of the man, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.445, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.420}}; of the woman, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.126; but ἐν ἀγκοίνῃσι Διός 11.268: with _accusative_ _cognate_, φιλότης.., ἣν ἐμίγης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.33.}}—The _aorist_ I is not used in this sense by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, but occurs in the Hymns, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Venerem” 46, +others}}; the _aorist_ I is more frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus and 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus}} (Cf. Latin _misceo_, Sanskrit _meksáyati_ 'stir', **miśrás** 'mixed'.)

3397:
Included with: ** μικρός**
and **σμῑκρός**, ά, όν, _Doric dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ **μικκός** (which see): **σμικρός** is corroborated by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.757, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 361}}, and might be restored in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.801, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.296 (μικρός codices)}}; it is probably the only form in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} (μικρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.74 codices): frequently in Lyric poetry and probably always in Trag. (except where metre requires μικρός, as 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 161 (_anapaest meter_, _comparative_))}}; most frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus; but in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, also 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus and other Comedy texts, μικρός prevails, σμικρός being found 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.13, 7.75, 8.81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 523, “V.” 5}}; _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. have σμικρός {{Refs|“IG” 12.313.111, +others, μικρός [prev. work]369.10, +others}}:—**small, little**,
**1** in Size, μ. ἔην δέμας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.801; μ. λίθος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.296; κίρκον, ὅ τε σμικρῇσι φόνον φέρει ὀρνίθεσσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.757; σμ. ἄστεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5; μεγάθεϊ σμικροί [prev. author] 2.74: with Dims., μ. πολίχνιον, γῄδιον, παιδάρια, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.145, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.38, “Ages.” 1.21}}: as a Comedy texts exaggeration, δικαστηρίδιον μ. πάνυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 803; σκαλαθυρμάτι᾽ ἄττα μ. [prev. author] “Nu.” 630, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, μικροὶ δ᾽ ὁρᾶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pax” 821}}: as a term of reproach, Κλειγενὴς ὁ μικρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ra.” 709, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 323d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123b7, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 98.7; Ἀμύντας ὁ μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1311b3}}; οἱ ἐν μικρῷ μεγάλοι **short** but stoutly built, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 36; ὁ μ. δάκτυλος “SIG” 1172.4 (from Lebena).}}
